28-year-old chef found dead in San Francisco Mall
A 28-year-old chef who recently moved to San Francisco was found dead in the stairwell of a local shopping centre.
The victim's brother testified that he couldn't imagine anyone wanting to harm him, "Finally it went uphill with him again."
The corpse, which was founded at Westfield Mall on Wednesday morning, was identified as 28-year-old Frank Galicia from San Francisco, the San Francisco-based medical department said.
The San Francisco Police Department said that the death was classified as a murder and the investigation is ongoing.
The victim's brother, Louis Galicia, told ABS broadcaster KGO in San Francisco that Frank, who formerly worked as a chef in Boston, had snatched his dream job six months ago as a chef at the Sons & Daughter's restaurant in San Francisco.
A Spokesperson for the Sons & Daughters said they were "shocked and devastated" by his death.
"We are a small team that works like a close family and we will sorely miss him," the spokesman said.
Our thoughts and condolences are with Frank's family and friends during this difficult time.
Louis Galicia stated that Frank initially lived in hostels, but that, "things were finally going uphill for him."
"He found an apartment, he had a girlfriend," Louis Garcia told KGO.
Louis Galicia said he couldn't imagine anyone who would want to harm his younger brother.
He was a friendly person with a big heart.
His way to connect with his family was to always cook us something, to prepare dinner for us," Louis Galicia said.
He never wanted to participate in any kind of altercation.
He was the brother who swam with the stream.
"In all that's going wrong in the world, he's this uncut diamond that shone bright every day," he said.
Anyone who has information about the case is asked to call the SFPD's whistleblowing telephone at 415-575-4444.
Jennifer Aniston: I'm always put in drawers
Jennifer Aniston doesn't always have to be perfect or successful.
That's what the Hollywood star has now made unequivocally clear in an interview.
“I’m always put in drawers or I’m glued on from the outside, which usually have little to do with myself and reality.”
“I don’t have a desire to always be perfect and successful.”
“Honestly, these things are rather sniffy for me.”
Jennifer Aniston (47) can only laugh that she's counted as "Miss Perfect," as she explains in the current "Cosmopolitan."
The actress prefers to spend her time at home, in front of the TV with loose dud and Mexican food, according to the magazine.
"We women don't have to be married or have children to be perfect," he said.
In her new film "Mother's Day - Love Isn't a Child's Game," Aniston can be seen as a single mother of two sons.
The film will run on August 25.
Golfer Langer gets the sports pyramid
His experiences on the horse are manageable.
At the end of a golf tournament in South Africa, Bernhard Langer and some colleagues were looking for a bit of variety with a ride on the beach.
For the 58-year-old, it was a first.
CHIO: "Golden sports pyramid" for Bernhard Langer
After a few minutes, the horses remembered their after-hours, chasing in the fast gallop home to the feeding troughs.
One kilometer at this pace, I've been scared.
"It wasn't a good experience," Langer says.
And with this, the riding career ended again.
On Saturday, Germany's best golfer of all time - 101 tournament victories - was back close to horses.
He and his family let themselves be guided over the facility of the ALRV, on a horse he did not even swept himself for photographic purposes.
Langer was in Aachen for the first time and thus also at the CHIO.
The reason was quite convincing.
The German sports aid handed over the golden sports pyramid to the man from Anhausen as part of a small gala.
There is no shortage of awards for the sympathetic athlete.
Federal Cross of Merit, Silver Laurel Leaf, Champions-Tour-Player of the year...
Even the British Queen has ennobled him.
Langer is the 18th winner of the sports pyramid.
In Aachen, he met the first prize winner Hans Günter Winkler on Saturday.
"This award means a lot to me," says Langer, "becaused not only for sporting achievements, but also for social commitment."
Langer has been promoting offspring for decades.
And so he passed on the prize money of 25,000 euros.
With 15 other outstanding athletes, the two-time U.S. Masters winner was inducted into the "Hall of Fame of German Sport" on Saturday.
The winners include the athletes Hartwig Gauder and Harald Schmitt, the gymnast Eberhard Gienger, the fencers Cornelia Hanisch, Alexander Pusch and Arnd Schmitt, the rower Peter-Michael Kolbe, bob driver Meinhard Nehmer, the judoka Frank Wieneke, the show jumping rider Alwin Schockemöhle and posthumous.
Many of these sports legends had come to Aachen on Saturday.
After the official part, the illustrious company migrated to Springstadion, where the Lavazza Prize ran.
Bernhard Langer kept his distance from the large animals.
Clinton's and Trump's economic policies in comparison: It's all about the money
Investing in infrastructure and preventing the TTP trade agreement - that's what both Clinton and Trump want.
However, the two U.S. presidential candidates disagree on tax and energy policy as well as on a fundamental issue.
The differences in economic policy between Donald Trump and Hillary Clinton are based on a fundamental, rather philosophical difference.
that, about the role of the state.
Trump wants to keep those rather small when in doubt and warns against how the state can harm the economy - with taxes and regulations.
Clinton wants to tax the economy to death and regulate to death, he says.
He himself would be completely different, Trump claims.
He will massively reduce regulations, and he is campaigning for far-reaching tax reform with lower tax rates - for private individuals and for companies.
Clinton, on the other hand, says: The state has certain tasks to perform, it has chances to open up the disadvantaged and needs money for it.
That's why taxes for the rich would be increased, she says.
Interestingly, they are both very close to infrastructure.
They have to be invested in it, Trump and Clinton say almost identically.
The democratic candidate even announces the largest investment in new jobs since the Second World War.
State investment programs in infrastructure are classic democratic politics and are strictly rejected, for example, by the state-critical tea-party wing of Republicans.
So Trump could still have in-party discussions.
The differences in energy and climate policy are clearer.
Trump wants to build coal-fired power plants and ramp up coal production in the US again.
Oil production should also be possible in previously protected areas, he wants to terminate the Paris climate agreement.
Clinton, on the other hand, explicitly wants to expand renewable energies with her investment programme and demand: "One country will become the superpower of clean energies and create millions of jobs."
“It’s going to be either China, Germany or America—I want us to be.”
But not only in terms of infrastructure - even with international trade agreements, the two competitors are not too far apart.
TPP, the Trans-Pacific trade agreement, both do not want to sign.
At the same time, however, both do not want to isolate the United States from international trade - but simply better protect American jobs.
They should also be paid better, for example, Clinton demands.
The current minimum wage of $7.25 is a starvation wage.
She's trying to raise him to $15 an hour.
What Trump wants to do on this issue isn't entirely clear: once he says the minimum wage has to go up, then he says he shouldn't be changed.
But the great discussion of the coming months is likely to develop rather around the tax policy of the two camps.
On the one hand side is Trump, who wants to exonerate the economy and the citizens in order to stimulate growth.
His critics accuse him that the budget deficit would only become even greater.
On the other side is Clinton, who at least wants the riches to pay more taxes, and whose critics say that more taxes aren’t the solution either.
"Every refugee woman has experiences with sexual violence"
Sexual violence is almost part of everyday life for women fleeing alone.
When they arrive in Germany, they are often traumatized.
A house provides refuge.
There are four women in a large kitchen.
Everyone has a baby who is at most one year old.
One put her young son in her arm.
He's looking into the room with big eyes.
His mother stares at the table top.
Until she realizes she's being watched.
She smiles briefly and kisses the little one.
The scene seems normal.
Until you talk to Katrin Bahr.
She is managing director of Condrobs, a carrier for social assistance in Bavaria, and deals, among other things, with the topic of women on the run.
The four women in the kitchen are refugees.
They live in the north of Munich in one of the few decentralised shelters for single women and their children in Bavaria.
In January, Condrobs opened the facility together with Pro Familia and Münchner Frauenhilfe.
It was recently officially opened.
60 people live there - about 40 women and 20 children.
Men don't exist - not even as visitors.
The exception is a security guard at the door.
"It's not exaggerated to say: every woman who arrives here has had experiences with sexual violence on her escape," Bahr says.
"Men are fleeing because of wars because they are politically persecuted, because they threaten torture or death, or because their families send them to Europe as beaconers of hope," she recounts.
In women, other reasons predominate for the escape: "Many secretly steal from it because they see no other way out".
In their home, they have experienced sexual violence, coercived marriages, honour killings, slavery or forced prostitution.
That's what drives them away.
On the run, this continues.
"It's not uncommodating that smugglers demand a sexual act for a bottle of water, for example, isn't uncommon," Bahr says.
And the women are alone, they aren't protected by their families.
Anyone who thinks that will end as soon as they arrive in Germany is mistaken, says Bahr.
Here, too, single women in refugee shelters would often be considered inferior and at worst abused.
As a result, about 90 percent of women who live in the accommodation in the north of Munich are traumatized, she estimates.
“Some of the children certainly did not originate voluntarily.”
Many women are used to say nothing.
“Or they don’t dare to speak openly about the experience because the shame is too great.”
Since they often don’t know how to help each other, it needs more accommodation just for them, Bahr demands.
In Bavaria, the seven district governments are responsible for the accommodation of refugees.
When asked if they knew about a similar project as in Munich in their area of responsibility, some answer no.
In the Upper Palatinate and Upper Franconia, for example, there is not such a thing in shelters of governments.
In Swabia, refugees alone are not accommodated separately, says Karl-Heinz Meyer, the government's press spokesman.
In Upper Bavaria, Lower and Middle Franconia, in some shared accommodations there are floors or own houses only for single women and their children.
The exception is Lower Bavaria, where there are two central accommodations exclusively for women.
The Women's Rights and Gender Equality Committee in the European Parliament also calls for women to be accommodated separately from men.
In a study published for the committee, it said: "It's very important that men and women are accommodated separately."
“If privacy is lacking or if men and women are accommodated together, there may be – more continuous – violence against women.”
The washrooms in particular should be separated and easily accessible to women, according to the study.
How much the women feel this need for protection can be seen in the rush, which there is on the house in Munich.
"Since it's been that we've gone, we've got a long waiting list," Bahr says.
It's easy to open a second house.
Assistant doctors strike: Demands for new industrial action
Substitutes of resident physicians have called on their union to approve new industrial action in their disputes regarding a new contract.
The British Medical Association's (JDC) Junior Doctors Committee (JDC) has called for its entire council to approve more industrial action from the beginning of September.
The JDC testified that ministers had failed to respond to concerns regarding the treaty.
Assistance physicians and medical students voted in July on rejecting a contract agreed with the BMA.
It was rejected by 58% of its members who voted in the elections.
In a letter to members posted on Twitter on Thursday night, JDC Ellen McCourt's chairwoman said the government would have remained "perseveringly calm" on issues, leading the treaty being rejected.
She said: "In view of this, the JDC board has voted to fully reject the proposed new contract and requested official renegotiations on all your concerns."
In response to the government's silencence, the JDC board today made a formal request for a special meeting of the BMA Council to approve an ongoing programme of escalating industrial action that begins in early September.
The disputes led to resident doctors striking six times this year, the first overall outages in NHS history.
The head of BMA assistant physician Dr. Johann Malawana resigned after the election to reject the agreed contractual conditions, which had been recommended by the BMA.
He informed BMA members during the meetings, which took place even before the vote by 54,000 assistant physicians and medical students, that the contract was beneficial and should be accepted.
After the vote, Health Secretary Jeremy Hunt said the contract would be forcibly introduced for doctors in England.
Russia and Turkey: an "alliance of outsiders"?
It was a gesture that ended a crisis.
The leaders of Russia and Turkey met on Tuesday to shake hands and announce an official end to the eight-month-long war of words and economic sanctions.
But when Vladimir Putin receives his Turkish opponent in the gilded hall of St. Petersburg's palace, I can't deny the impression that Ankara wants this reconciliation the most.
There's been the handshake, yes.
But Putin's smiles seemed narrow-lipped and he barely radiated warmth, even for his restrained circumstances.
Recep Tayyip Erdogan, on the other hand, repeatedly spoke of his "dear friend" Putin - according to a report five times.
I couldn't count it anymore.
He also promised that relations with Russia would not only return to its old level before the crisis, but will even improve.
The next day, a newspaper described that Erdogan had behaved as if nothing bad had ever happened.
His enthusiasm seemed to me the opposite.
But a sustainable cool radiated by Putin showed that Russia's head of state had not forgotten anything.
Rather, the cause of the crisis was the first thing he mentioned in his introductory words: the downing of a Russian warplane on Turkey's Syrian border.
Moscow's reaction at the time was furious.
Putin distributed, accusing Ankara of having stabbed Russia.
The offense was even greater as it was committed by a supposed friend.
Rebuilding trust will be difficult, if not impossible.
The public opinion in Russia has changed since November.
For months, the state-controlled media carried out a violent, unrestricted attack on Ankara.
All of a sudden it seemed like the Turks were to blame for everything.
The most serious were the accusations by senior officials that Erdogan's own family would have benefited from the illegal oil trade in areas of Syria controlled by the so-called Islamic State.
He vehemently denied this.
But in St. Petersburg, the official message was that it was time to look forward.
After all, this meeting took place only because Putin received the apology he wanted to hear from President Erdogan.
Russia was able to record some sort of victory.
For Ankara, the advantages of a ceasefire are obvious.
Erdogan, first and foremost, needs all the friends he can get after he was almost ousted from office last month in a failed coup d’état.
Repeated terrorist attacks on Turkey have also rocked him.
But there are economic motives too.
Russian sanctions have hit Turkey harshly - in particular the ban on charter flights that usually bring several million Russian tourists to the Turkish coast each year.
The number has declined by almost 90%.
In Russia, tour operators and charter companies will be relieved when flights are resumed.
They are counted on a late-season rush to the Mediterranean.
This week, state television predicted cheaper fruits and vegetables as soon as Turkey's agricultural imports are allowed again.
A report in Vedemosti put it this way: "Tourists long there, tomatoes here long."
But the visit had additional political value for Moscow.
Ankara is angry with the West, as it considers its reaction to the attempted takeover as a weak response.
In addition, there is the ongoing grudge about the snail's pace of the EU accession negotiations and Putin - which is eager to benefit from the cool mood and reduce Turkey's relationship with the West.
The Russian head of state certainly won a bonus in Ankara for demanding support for the elected state organs after the attempted coup d’état.
This is a matter of course for Moscow, which itself has a deep-seated fear of regime change.
The summit in this glamorous palace by the sea allowed Russia and Turkey to represent one, as an analyst once described to me, "alliance of outsiders": two countries that feel violated by the West and now allied.
Nevertheless, in spite of public reconciliation, there are still great differences between the two.
The biggest difference is Syria, where Moscow recently gave itself the role as a peacemaker, but where Russia and Turkey support opposing sides.
Significantly, after almost three hours of initial talks, both presidents testified at the press conference that they didn't even graze the issue.
Turkey's president deliberately avoided respondingingly to a question about their differences, while Putin decided to emphasize them.
There is no clear consensus on where a common denominator of Syria can be found.
But after months of open hostilities - and given the potential for a complete disaster when NATO member Turkey shot down the Russian fighter jet - it's certainly better for the two leaders to at least talk to each other again.
Royal Bank of Scotland no longer available to customers outside Scotland
The RBS brand is to be reduced only to a back-office role, according to the bank's chief executive.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will no longer be available to customers outside Scotland.
Ross McEwan told BBC Scotland that the RBS brand was linked to the bank's global ambitions.
The bank has since distanced itself when it nearly collapsed and had to be rescued eight years ago.
During this time, brand strategists have used "RBS" to protect other end-consumer financial brands.
She backed the international sport with millions of pounds in sponsorship, from Six Nations rugby to Wimbledon winner Andy Murray.
But now she's decided to bring more national brands into focus.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will only be available to customers in Scotland, but will not be initialised.
In England and Wales, all RSB references, decentralised headquarters and stock market listing will switch to NatWest.
The Ulster Bank brand is already being used for clients in the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland.
There are other, smaller brands for the retail business that will get more importance - Coutts, Adam & Co, Drummond and Holt's Military Bank.
McEwan was interviewed during a sightseeing tour by customers and staff in Inverness-shire.
"The RSB brand will become our investor brand as well as the brand under which our employees will be employed, as we are now becoming more of a bank of brands."
When the bank itself became a global brand, RBS became a global brand.
I say now that we aren't pursuing global goals but local goals.
Each of these brands will stand for something completely different in their own communities, and our employees will work with customers under these brands.
RBS has already testified that it will no longer continue its Six Nations sponsorship and that it will increase the perceptiveness of the various brands in its sports sponsorship.
"It's time for us to develop into the bank of brands, because how do we focus inside (we asked) to make this a better bank for customers?" the chief executive said.
It would have been very cynical three years ago if we had said we were going to be a great bank for our customers and launched these brands.
But with our work, the focus on customer needs and not our own, I think you will see a lot of changes.
We can launch these brands again, so I think the time has come.
Frankie Dettori achieves 3,000th profit at Newmarket on Predilection
Dettori sprays champagne after a double at Newmarket that brings him 3,000th profit
Jockey Frankie Dettori secured his 3,000th British profit at Newmarket on Friday.
He relied on Predilection, which is coached by John Gosden, on his local racetrack with his second win of the night.
The 45-year-old Italian is the sixth jockey to reach the milestone in the flat race.
He succeeds Sir Gordon Richards, Doug Smith, Lester Piggott, Pat Eddry and Willie Carson.
The three-time winner Dettori, who won on Ghayyar before that, said: "It's special."
I'm very emotional because my family is here too
I'm very happy that it happened at Newmarket because I landed here 30 years ago.
It's not a big meeting, it's not Royal Ascot - it's a banal Friday night with 20,000 people.
I'm relieved and very modest.
Dettori picks up the jubilation as he picks up victory on Predilection
Two jockeys have reached the milestone in the obstacle race - jockey Richard Johnson and retired 20-time winner Sir Anthony McCoy, who reached more than 4,300 victories in a record-breaking career.
Lester Piggott, the nine-time derby winner had 4,493 career wins
Frankie is one of the best jockeys of modernity and it's no surprise that he's reached this milestone that few before him reached.
He has style and is strong, but it's his tactical intellect that sets him apart from the others in the weight control room.
He's a fantastic ambassador for racing, his enthusiasm and charisma has been delighting fans for this sport for years.
I told Frankie a few weeks ago at Epsom that he would have already picked up 6,000 wins if he weren't just a weekend and grand tournament jockey!
Frankie has already ridden almost 30 years winner in the UK, which is great.
He has a living personality, which is good for a British race, but beyond that he's an incredible jockey.
Frankie Dettori celebrated the milestone with a typical Flying Dismount
From Flying Finishes to Flying Dismounts, Frankie Dettori has been the figurehead of the British flat race for two decades.
It was nerve-wracking.
A whole series of major tournament wins, including his famous "Glorious Sevens," at Ascot in 1996, but he also survived a plane crasp and was given a drug ban.
Three years ago, he initially had difficulties when he returned from his six-month suspension, but he showed an impressive character strength to defy his doubters and return to his best form.
And he's far from the end - Dettori once said earlier that he wants to continue in competitions up to the age of at least 50th.
Russia's Putin fires Chief of Staff Sergei Ivanov
Russian President Vladimir Putin has unexpectedly dismissed his chief of staff Sergei Ivanov.
Ivanov was part of Putin's closest circle of trust for many years.
The 63-year-old has been Appointed Special Representative for Environmental and Transport Affairs.
In a Kremlin's statement, it reads that Putin, without giving reasons, "ordered Ivanov's dismissal from his duties as chairman of the Russian presidential administration."
Anton Vaino, who had been Ivanov's deputy since 2012, was appointed as his successor.
Vaino, 44, is a former diplomat.
Born in the Estonian capital Tallinn in 1972, he graduated from the prestigious Moscow State Institute of International Relations (MGIMO) and worked at the embassy in Tokyo.
The Kremlin's website states (in Russian) that he was later protocol chief and chief of staff of government personnel.
Speaking about his appointment, he told Putin, "Thank you for your confidence."
I think that the most important task of the government is to support your work as head of state regarding draft laws and checks to implement your instructions.
Putin told the Russian TV broadcaster on Friday that Ivanov had askeded to leave his post and recommended Vaino as his successor.
Convince the smiling faces?
This move has made Moscow secrecy.
Sergei Ivanov has long been one of Vladimir Putin's closest confidants and, like him, works for the Soviet secret service, the KGB.
As chief of staff, he was one of the country's most powerful men.
At a meeting with President Putin, which aired on television, both men claimed that the chief of staff resignated at his own request.
But despite the smiling for the cameras, few are convinced of it here - especially now, just before parliamentary elections.
Is there the consequence of a kind of power struggle?
No one currently knows that.
But the official claim - that a man who was once touted as a potential president suddenly wants to direct Russian environmental policy - met with great skepticism.
In his remarks to Putin, quoted on the Kremlin's website, Ivanov said "it's true that at the beginning of 2012 I asked you in a conversation to entrust me this very complicated post - you could almost say - for four years.
As it turns out, I've been chairman of Russia's presidential administration for four years and eight months.
Ivanov accepted the post in December 2011.
Previously, he was deputy prime minister and secretary of defense.
He is a member of Russia's Security Council and, like Putin's former member of the state security service KGB.
In the late 1990s, when Putin was chairman of the Federal Security Service (FSB), which replaced the KGB, Ivanov was appointed as his deputy.
When Putin came to power, he named Ivanov as one of five people he trusted the most.
Ivanov was once believed to become president of Russia after Putin's second term, as a third tenure of Putin would have violated the constitution.
But this post has been taken by another, close ally of Putin, Dmitry Medvedev.
Putin became prime minister before becoming president again three-and-a-half years later.
Criminal Minds star Thomas Gibson fired after he beaten producers
Gibson was involved in a physical altercation on the set of the series in Los Angeles last month.
ACB Studios and CBS Television Studios, which produce the series, announced its resignation in a joint statement.
Gibson, who played special agent Aaron Hotchner, publicly apologised Thursday prior to announcing his dismissal.
There were artistic differences and a disagreement on the set.
"I regret the incident," he said in a statement.
Details of Gibson's character from the serie, which follows a team of FBI agents, is expected to be released at a later date, according to ABC Studios and CBS Television Studios.
The 54-year-old, who previously starred in the sitcom Dharma & Greg, has been involved since the first season of the series in 2005.
"I love Criminal Minds and I've put all my lifeblood into it for the last twelve years," Gibson said.
I had hoped to be there until the end, but that won't be possible now.
I’d just like to thank the writers, producers, actors, our wonderful crew and, above all, the best fans you could wish for a series.
The planned broadcast of the twelfth season is scheduled for September 28.
Mysteries surrounding fish that landed in garden in Banff
A resident of Banff requested help in identifying the fish, which appeared in his garden in Banff.
Kevin Bain thinks the 75 fish could be sanda eels spewed out by a fountain.
Mr. Bain, who lives about 500 meters from the sea, posted on Periscope a video of the spectacle in the hope that someone could shine some light on the appearance of the two-inch-long fish.
Queensferry Crossing: Bridge Now Connected to Fife
The £1.35bn project to be completed by May 2017
Engineers have connected the northern deck of Queensferry Crossing with the viaduct - meaning the bridge is now connected to Fife.
It is the first of four closures of the "cover blower" approaching completion around each of the three towers of the bridge.
Fife's bridge section is now 600 metres long and weighs 30,000 tonnes.
It has 10,000 tons of steel and 20,000 tonnes of concrete as well as 46 bevels.
The £1.35bn project is expected to be completed by May 2017.
Economy Secretary Keith Brown paid a visit to the construction site today and was one of the first to go from land to the bridge.
He said: "This is a historic and symbolic moment in the construction of the Queensferry Crossing.
We are all witnesses of a truly epic dimension in this project with over 30,000 tons of concrete and steel used alone for this part of the bridge.
Despite the massive size and weight of the bridge, the completion of the closure between the viaduct and the bridge deck is a delicate activity that requires extremely precise tolerances for fitting.
Overall, almost 79% of the total bridge decks are in place and the last section of the deck today gets a concrete cast for the deck in Rosyth, which means that the entire deck is ready to be lifted to its place on the bridge.
Michael Martin, project manager of Forth Crossing Bridge Constructors, said: "The first closure represents a significant milestone in every bridge project.
With this fantastic project, the closure represents future-oriented building and civil engineering.
After the installation of the last section of the road and its connection with the Nordturm deck under construction, we had to close the still existing gap on the north or land side.
This was achieved by pulling the northern feeder bridge 700 millimeters south.
This was a massive and at the same time very delicate activity.
Massive since the viaduct is 222 meters long, weighs about 6,000 tons and had to be pulled upwards with a gradient of about 3%.
Delicate, since the tolerances the team is working towards were tiny - on each side they were only a few millimeters.
Luckily everything went very well.
Rescue at Moelfre Bay: Three men are 'lucky to still be alive'
Three men are "lucky still to be alive" after being thrown by their fishing boat off Anglesey.
The Moelfre RNLI rescue station sounded BST at 13:20 on Friday after a crew observed the event at Moelfre Bay.
The three men struggled to swimme to safety while their boat moved on on their own.
"We saw the boat swirling around it near them and imagining the worst thing to us," said Moelfre Vince Jones's header.
"Fortunately, the strong winches have already blown the boat out of their range until we let it to water," he added.
Two of the men were rescued by a local fishing boat, while the third man, aged 60, was found exhausted and hanging from a buoy without a lifejacket.
They all came aboard the coastal rescue boat and were taken back to the rescue station
Mr Jones said: "The three men who have been thrown into the water are uninjured and still alive.
The boot engine had an emergency stop line, but it detached from the person who steered the boat when capsizing the boat.
Someone woke up over these men today.
Seriously injured - man stabs his ex-girlfriend
A man has attacked his ex-girlfriend in Hamburg-Harburg with a knife and seriously injured.
As the police announced yesterday, the 50-year-old had gotten the key to the apartment to get his personal belongings.
Instead, the alcoholic offender cut his ex-girlfriend's clothes.
When she came into the apartment, he stabbed her twice.
The 44-year-old was injured on the hand and body.
The police were able to arrest the perpetrator.
While the 50-year-old destroyed his ex-girlfriend's clothes, he called his mother.
She alerted his sister.
Together with her partner, the 45-year-old wanted to reassure her brother.
She couldn't persuade him to leave the apartment, though.
That's why she went out of the house with her partner.
On the street, they met the man's ex-partner.
When the two women entered the apartment again, the 50-year-old shoved his sister aside and attacked his ex-girlfriend.
Who builds Braunschweig's cheap apartments?
The SPD and Greens believe that investors should be made much more obligatory in the future.
How do you react to the bad news that Brunswick will lose almost 1300 cheap apartments in the next few years?
The SPD proposes an "action concept housing" at the next council meeting.
However, quarrels are programmed.
First and foremost, the question is: who should actually build the cheap apartments?
A regulation is missing so far.
SPD group chairman Christoph Bratmann says: "We agree with the Greens: If you want to build on urban areas, 20 percent of all apartments as social housing in the future."
However, the cheap apartments can also be created in stock.
According to Bratmann, however, the big point of contention in the town hall is: "How do you deal with investors who want to build on private land?"
"We want investors to build at least 10 percent, but as much as possible 20 percent of the apartments as social housing."
A new urban housing funding programme, which will cost millions and has yet to be funded, is intended to provide incentives.
Bratmann said: “There is currently no agreement between the factions.”
The CDU wants to make investors as few regulations as possible.
The left still wants tough conditions for investors.
Regardless, according to Bratmann, an intensification of the situation in the housing market.
If the action concept is implemented, this will ensure additional tasks in the construction administration.
More staff is needed.
It's unclear whether we're going to get the skilled workers.
However, the programme is not yet complete: because the approximately 1260 new apartments, which were to be built anyway.
But how to compensate for the loss of a further 1300 low-cost apartments, the funding of which will expire in the next few years?
There will be 500 apartments by 2020 alone.
To prevent that, according to planning policy spokeswoman Nicole Palm, expiring ties should now be bought by the city.
In addition, it would have to be thought about bringing apartments back into price fixing.
This is possible in the western city.
The problem could be solved in one fell swoop.
