Pokémon Go, a hunt for health and happiness that's worthwhile
It’s amazing to see how the world is changing.
That teenagers and child no longer go outside to play, but rather prefer to spend their free time staring at screens has been lamented by many over the last decade.
Pokemon Go is about getting people off the sofa and making them more active.
The time on the screen was initially confineded to the TV, then to video games and most recently to smartphones.
Therefore, it’s great to see that technology and outdoor activities aren’t mutually exclusive.
Of course, I’m referring to the Pokémon Go delusion, an app based on the characters that were first created for the video games in the 1990s (later an animated series, playing cards and other spin-offs).
Like many phenomena of pop culture, interest plummeted somewhat before the game's release in the U.S., Australia and New Zealand in early July.
The market launch in other countries has been shifted due to the massive demand that overloaded the servers.
Within a few days Pokémon Go had more users than Tinder and almost as many as Twitter.
how does it work?
In short, players go outside with their smartphones and look for Pokémon that are tracked with a GPS and, once they have been founded, "photographed" (via a screenshot on the phone) or "captured."
A generation who played the game when she was even younger joined the madness and jumped off the sofa for the search.
In the U.S., the game led to a considerable increase in steps that the "average" person travels every day.
Cardiogram, an app for Apple Watch that tracks 35,000 users, recently told The Washington Post that within two days of the game's launch, the number of people tracked taking 30 minutes every day jumped from 45 percent to 53 percent.
The company doesn't know who's playing Pokémon Go - it only sees the raw data to increase performance.
Lots of public education campaigns on health that make people want to "take 30 minutes," "coming from the sofa" and "being active every day," came across deaf ears because a lot of public messages on health are boring and come from people who look miserable.
Nevertheless, the importance of sport cannot be overstated.
Those who exercise regularly reduce their chances of developing cancer or depression.
Physical exercise helps with weight control, high blood pressure, diabetes, and a long list of benefits that’s too long to cite here.
But the wonderful thing about Pokémon Go is that it makes it get people to do something outdoors that they enjoy.
Thus, three pillars of health are covered - exercise, fresh air and sunshine as well as fun.
Some people even make new friendships while hunting.
As always, there are grumpy spoilers who always have to complain about something.
And yes, someone could run against a door.
What's the matter?
A breakthrough has occurred.
Even though smartphone apps like Fitbit were able to track our activity earlier, they didn't give us a reason to exercise, as this game does.
The new will lose its stimulus and the use will subside over time.
Due to Pokemon’s success, however, there will be more games that will unite apps and virtual reality with becoming active in the real world and will help many people on the hunt to be more active, fitter and healthier.
Read Dr. Joe's blog at www.drjoetoday.com
