Mother of a 6-year-old with microcephaly: 'It's getting better'
Like most 6-year-olds, Edmund Picciuto loves singing, toys and, of course, his mother.
But like fewest of his peers, Edmund has microcephaly, the result of a genetic disorder that his mother Elizabeth Picciuto didn't know anything about during her pregnancy.
When he first came home, my first thought, I couldn't do it.
I can't do this.
I didn't agree to that.
What's not true at all -- of course you've embarked on it," she said.
Her husband Vincent and her have been toldered that Edmund may never be able to recognize them and that he may not even survive.
Some doctors even went so far that they proposed to institutionalize him.
"My first answer to that was 'I didn't know people were still doing that,'" Picciuto explained.
Edmund can walk with the help of a walking aid.
Edmund could neither sit nor crawl until the age of three, and he still can't speak.
But he's slowly making progress.
He can walk with support and ride a bike.
He also loves playing and singing with his two brothers.
Elizabeth depicts the sign language Edmund came up with for "please sing something to me."
"We're teaching him sign language," Picciuto told CBS News.
But it'sn't a typical sign language -- she says. Edmund invented some signs on his own.
Microcephaly has a variety of causes: genetic as well as in Edmund and infections with viruses such as measles or the Zika virus during pregnancy.
Symptoms and prognotions can vary greatly.
It's too early to say how it will affect the lives of those infected with the Zikavirus.
Picciuto remembers the emotional price of an uncertain future.
I kept asking, what's going to happen?
What's going to happen?
And she still doesn't really know.
I haven't asked about the prognosy in a long time because I don't expect anyone to really be able to tell me.
But Picciuto has hopeful advice for mothers of infants with birth defects caused by the Zika virus.
It's getting better.
You know it's going to get better for you.
You'll love your child and your child will love you.
