The £2-per-day drug that can reduce heart attack risk
Thousands of heart attack patients will benefit from a £2-per-day drug after authorities have been advised to advise this more people for an extended period.
The anticoagulant drug Ticagrelor reduces the risk of repeated heart attacks for people with heart disease.
The drug is already administered for 12 months after a heart attack, reducing the risk of a stroke or another heart attack.
NICE's NICE has recommended a four-yer-long intake to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
NICE's NICE has recommended a four-yer-long intake to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
Around 140,000 people suffer a heart attack each year and a quarter of them suffer another heart attack or stroke.
Heart attacks and strokes are caused by accumulations of fatty material in the arterial walls that make toppings.
When the coating breaks apart, it can cause a blood clot that clogs the blood flow to the heart, causing a heart attack as a result.
When the blood clot dissolves, it can flow through the bloodstream and clog blood flow to the brain, causing a stroke.
Individuals who have already had a heart attack are at a higher risk of having another.
Ticagrelor, which is manufactured by the British company AstraZeneca and is marketed under the trade name Brilique, reduces this risk by making the formation of blood clots more unlikely.
NICE's draft guidance, published today, recommends taking 90mg ticagrelor 12 months, followed by 60mg with a twice-daily intake of aspirin for the next three years.
Professor Carole Longson, director of the NICE Health Technology Evaluation Center, said: "Despite the availability of secondary prevention, a quarter of all individuals who have suffered a heart attack have another heart attack or stroke - often with disastrous consequences."
Fear of another heart attack can have a significant negative impact on a person’s quality of life.
Experience shows that Ticagrelor, in combination with aspirin, is effective in reducing further heart attacks and strokes in people who have already had a heart attack.
Through a preliminary recommendation from Ticagrelor, we are pleased that we are able to expand available treatment options to thousands of people who can benefit.
The information about the effectiveness and safety of Ticagrelor - especially the risk of bleeding - is limited to a period of up to three years. The draft guide does not recommend treatment that goes beyond this period.
