Former mayor of Tower Hamlets Lutfur Rahman was removed from office last year at instigation of a government report for corruption
A culture of political correctness has meant that widespread electoral fraud is knowingly ignored in Muslim communities, as a scathining government report revealed today.
Former cabinet member Sir Eric Pickles verbally attacks police, election observers and town halls for ignoring evidence of electoral abuse due to "hypersensitivities to ethnicity and religion."
The former minister of integration and local government, who is now an anti-corruption king, said the intimidation of voters for religious motives would be so bad that it should be allowed to set up barrier tapes outside voting booths to protect intimidated voters.
He recommends that voters should carry an ID with them to prove their identities in elections.
At the moment, a voter just needs to give his name and address.
The report proposes applying non-removable ink to a person's finger when casting their election vote to prevent someone from voting twice.
Whistleblowers should be given more protection, says Sir Eric.
He demands that the press should have more access to community documents and decisions from nonofficial committees or working groups.
"This not only serves against electoral fraud, but is also intended to protect the municipal government from the broader culture of corruption and financial fraud that goes along with it," he said.
The report was ordered by Downing Street as a result of the Tower Hamlet's electoral scandal in eastern London.
Last year, a special electoral court ruled that incumbent mayor Lutfur Rahman should be relieved of office following a litany of corrupt and illegal practices.
The court found that Rahman had "perverted the religious sentiments of his Muslim community in a cynical manner" and that he would have silenced his critics with racism and Islamophobic accusations.
Rahman is said to have "led his campaign on the basis that it is the religious duty of Muslims to elect him."
Up to 300 votes were either dubious or the result of an imitation in which one person chooses as someone else.
