CBC commentator for the Olympics apologises for his statement that Chinese swimmer 'died like a pig
Byron MacDonald's analysis of 14-year-old Ai Yanhan's fourth-place finish at 4x200m freestyle swimming squad outraged viewers of Wednesday's CBC live broadcast
Canadian broadcaster CBC had to apologise after one of its commentators said the 14-year-old Chinese swider "went off like Mief" and died during the race like "a pig."
The commentator Byron MacDonalds thought that his microphone was turned off as he gave his own opinions at the end of the 4x200m free-style swimming squadron.
MacDonald, who had been brought on board to the CBS commentator team to add more "color", could be heard saying: "This little 14-year-old Chinese has lost the connection, baby".
Too agitated, went off like Mief, died like a pig.
Thanks for that.
Chinese swimmer Ai Yanhan, 14, swammed the second stage of the swimming squad 1:57.79 - 1.61 seconds slower than Canada's Taylor Ruck, allowing Canadians to fill the gap with China.
Viewers across Canada reacted quickly online after shocking the tone and language
Emmett Macfarlane tweeted, "Did the CBC commentator just say that the 14-year-old swimmer from China "died like a pig"???
The jerk didn’t realize they were still on air.”
Ai Yanhan from China was described at the final 4 x 200m freestyle swimming squadron for women as follows: "This little 14-year-old Chinese woman has lost the connection, baby."
Criticism against Byron MacDonald on Twitter came quickly and harsh, where many confronted him
Twitter users didn't hold back when they hear how insensitive Byron MacDonald was
Another Twitter user Sarah Paradis wrote: "Wow, #ByronMacDonald.
You represent the CBC and Canada and make us all look like racists.
A short time later Scott Russel, who hosted the CBC broadcast, apologised live for MacDonald's comment, saying: "We apologise that the commentary about a swimming feat was broadcast."
It's been an unhappily chosen wording, we're sorry it happened."
CBC apologized in the end over 90 times to the people who have responded to the noticed via Twitter
Commentator colleague Scott Russel had to apologise on behalf of MacDonald
CBC copied and inserted its apologies to more than 90 other Twitter users who had complained about MacDonald's comments
The network also apologised quickly and issued a notice.
We sincerely regret that these statements were made and that it was made possible for them to be broadcast.
We apologised quickly to our viewers on air and our followers on social media last night.
To make one thing clear, Byron's comments related to the swimmer's performance rather than her as a person.
Nevertheless, they were inappropriate and an unhappily chosen wording. Byron is very sorry for what he said.
On Thursday afternoon, MacDonald apologised on the broadcast and tried to justify his remark by testifying that it was not his intention for her to be construed as a personal attack.
"I'd like to take a moment to apologise for the comment I made after the women's swimming squadron in the CBC Olympic broadcast," he said.
“I was referring to the swimmer’s performance rather than her as a person.”
Of course, no disregard was intentional and I am very sorry."
Buyer in Primark outraged over woman shamefully trying on underwear in the middle of the shop
Buyers didn't know where to look when a woman stripped her clothes in the middle of a Primark to try on underpants.
To worsen the situation, she surveyed how she looked inside, stripped her out and put her back on the shelf.
She continued to rummage, trying on four pairs of underpants at a shop at Bouverie Place shopping centre in Folkestone.
The Primark store at Bouverie Place shopping centre in Folkestone, where the woman tried on the underpants before putting her back on the shelf
A mother of two, Jenny Davidson, who was in the business at the same time, said that after the mysterious woman tried on the underwear, "she contracted again and left the business without buying anything."
She said, "People couldn't believe what they saw."
Standing in the middle of the sales area, she took a pair of underpants from the display, took off her own tights and trousers and tried them on.
"She didn't even try to find a slightly more discrete place to do it - she just stood there, naughty like Oskar."
"Everyone starred as she took off the underpants again, which she obviously didn't like as she put them back on the shelf."
She repeated the same with three more underpants before finally deciding she didn't like anything, got dressed again and went out.
"She didn't seem to bother that anyone was observing her - or thinking about the person who might buy the underpants she'd tried on."
Ms Davidson, 33, who lives in Kent, said, "How disgusting to think that someone would take home underpants that had another person attached and who weren't washed."
The incident happened Tuesday afternoon.
Ms Davidson added: "As I've noticed, the staff have been trying to pull the garments out of the sale after she left - which is just as good."
"I'd hate to go home with a new pair of underpants and find out that another woman was wearing them out and they weren't washed."
A spokesman for Primark said: "Primark is aware of the incident that occurred on Tuesday, August 9 at our Folkstone branch."
All eligible products have been withdrawn from sale.
How to protect yourself from gun attacks with Krav Maga
The world outside is a dangerous place, but this video from Dailymail.com could make it a little safer for you.
We spoke to Rhon Mizrachi, a grandmaster of the Krav Maga Association in New York to find out how to deal with armed robbers at close distance.
Regardless of being approached from the front or from the back, this video will show you how to turn the tables for your robber.
If you're approaching from behind, the first thing you're supposed to turn around so you can see your attacker and "get out of the firing line," Mizrachi says.
Then seize the weapon between your forearm and your shoulder before punching it in the face with your free elbow.
Then hit him with his knee into the groin and twist, as the attacker bends together in pain, the gun out of his hands. Slap him with the barrel in the face and move backwards as they point the weapon at him.
For attackers approaching from the front, the technique is similar - move your body out of the firing line, seize the gun and twist it so the attacker can't shoot.
At the same time shift the weight to the weapon and hit the attacker's chest, then twist the weapon out of its handle before moving backwards and pointing the weapon at him.
Sounds difficult?
Wate the video to see exactly how Mizrachi demonstrates it.
But keep in mind that it's safest to hand over your money during a robbery - unless you're Krav Maga grandmaster.
Traveller Ian Wright scrapes his tongue for a guide in Europe's dirtiest places
One British traveler subjected his immune system and strangle reflex to serious scrutiny - by licking some of Europe's dirtiest and most disgusting places.
The presenter and travel author Ian Wright, 51, from Suffolk scraped his train on a euro bill, a railing at Piccadilly Circus, a flushing button of a train toilet as well as a public phone in Russia to demonstrate the resilience of his immune system.
Before licking each object, he used a luminometer to show himself and us what he's going to get into.
But the experiment, filmed under "The Lickhiker's Guide to Inner Strength," brought some surprising results to light.
The thought of licking off the rinsing knob of a train toilet that has already been used by thousands of others may be completely repulsive, but there are actually more bad bacteria in a kitchen sink.
On his journey through Europe, Mr. Wright spoke with doctors and healthcare professionals about the promotion of gut bacteria as well as his own health.
Luckily, nothing he licked left more than a bad aftertaste in his mouth.
Mr. Wright became known for testing the resilience of his intestines by traveling around the world and ate peculiar and wonderful things.
From public railings at train stations to flush buttons of toilets, the task definitively put his immune system under a sample
In the film, he recounted: "Even as a child, I was relatively unconcerned about my health and I put all sorts of things into my mouth, earth, worms and mud water."
In the film, he definitely lived up to his reputation.
The only thing he declined was a dirty public toilet in Russia.
His sample smear showed a luminometer number of over 4000 units.
In comparison, the luminometer number of freshly washed hands is less than 60.
But there was a Russian toilet he refused to lick after its luminometer count was above 4000 units, while freshly washed hands have one of less than 60
Mr. Wright and Finnish dairy producer Valio produced the travel film "The Lickhiker's Guide to Inner Strength," which showcases the number of bacteria in various places like the bathroom
In the movie, he says, "This comes close to the worst I smelled in a toilet."
There's nothing dirtier than that!
I won't like anything here because it's disgusting in here.
"The Lickhiker's Guide to Inner Strength" was produced in collaboration with the Finnish manufacturer of dairy products Valio.
The company equips Mr. Wright with Valio Gefilus, which contains both lactic acid bacteria and vitamin C and D to support the promotion of intestinal bacteria and the immune system.
Valio's senior Venice president for snacks, juices and new categories Niko Vuorenmaa says: "We really wanted to offer consumers something new.
Something that’s intriguing to look at and at the same time explains the benefits of probiotics.
We conducted crazy brainstorming, which ended with us saying "Let's call Ian."
They claim that interest in bowel health is becoming a global phenomenon
Instead of just talking about the products, we wanted to put them in a larger context - the good and bad bacteria in our everyday environment, the importance of intestinal health and how it is linked to the immune system.
The growing interest in intestinal health is a global phenomenon and with the documentary we were able to provide consumers with more information in an interesting way.
I also think that the movie shows that we have a great sense of humor!
Tom Hakala, who directed the film, added: "Let me put it this way - I would have lost my respect for Ian if he had licked some of the things I instructed him to lick, just like the disgusting Russian toilet shown in the film."
Of course, there were things where it was easier to get Ian to lick them and others where this was quite difficult.
I'm glad we've picked up some of these dirty treats on film.'
In a 2015 survey conducted by Valio, 84 percent of all respondents associated lactic acid bacteria in particular with the well-being of stomach and intestines, and 58 percent said that they at least occasionally use products that contain attached lactic acid bacteria.
Asocial family destroyed before delogation social housing in Saltash, Cornwall
An anti-social family left her social housing after a £50,000-degree deployment for terrorising her neighbours.
Tanya Skeldon and her partner Shaun Trebilcock were kicked after a long list of complaints from their estate in Saltash, Cornwall.
After being forcibly removed from their property, shocked employees of the housing cooperative found the property in an irreparable condition.
The walls had gaping holes, wooden planks were torn open, rubbish was scattered on the floor and graffiti was smeared against the walls.
The family have been relocated and it is now believed to be living in a private accommodation
Donna McEvoy, who works for property management at Cornwall Housing, said she feared the apartment could no longer be repaired.
She said: "It's been an absolute nightmare for the people here and I'm of the view that people don't have to live that way.
There are many things to consider, as this property will presumably be seen as a not-so-nice part of Cornwall.
The family is notorious and so is their reputation.
The couple had three sons who, in the meantime, lived in the apartment and the police said the terror that had inflicted the family included street fights with baseball bats, intimidators of local residents, substance abuse, drug trafficking and excessive screaming, arguing and cursing.
Skeldon, 47, whose name was on the lease, got an order for anti-social behaviour in June.
In some rooms of the house, which may be demolished, old rubbish was found
The wooden floorboards were ripped open in the property where drugs were traded in the past
Neighbors testified that they saw them fighting in the street and the police had to be called regularly
Cornwall Housing forcibly delogged her and her partner from the estate this week after she gave them an extended 11-week deadline to voluntarily move out.
The delogation was cheered by relieved neighbors who said their lives were made hell.
One testified, "They didn't care about anyone."
We lived next to them for 20 years and it's always been bad.
The countless times we tell the police here are unbelievable.
“We couldn’t sleep this morning.”
We stood up because it was so quiet here - we'ren't used to that!
Now that they're finally gone, it's going to be beautiful.
Adam Fitzpatrick, Neighbourhood Enforcement Manager of Cornwall Housing, testified that the family had failed to cooperate with local authorities.
The family lived in the house to which the police were called at regular intervals for 20 years
On the overgrowned side of the community house, rubbish and old toys were thrown down
He added: "It's a shame that the tenant failed to assume any responsibility for their behaviour and that of their family, as a delogation is always the last step Cornwall Housing is considering.
This was a very difficulent case, as the behavior was extreme and their actions had to be terrible for their neighbors.
I appreciate all the work that my team, the Devon and Cornwall police, as well as members of the community who courageously approached us and sent us the information have done.
I hope that this development will give comfort to some neighbors and convey the message that anti-social behavior will not be tolerated.
Fitzpatrick said that all three sons had lived in the house at different times, but that they were all over 20.
A dirty mattress left on the floor in one of the bedrooms of the house after delogation
Workers of property management will now have to clear away piles of rubbish that surround the house
In 2014, the police carried out a raid on the house and found over 70g of cannabis as well as scales, paraphernalia and £1,700 cash.
Skeldon received a fine after pleading guilty of possessing a Class B drug in commercial intent.
She claimed the money was for a tombstone of her son Lee Skeldon, who died in a car accident in 2013.
Having been without shelter, Tanya and her partner are now believed to be living in a private property in Plymouth.
Many room walls of the house were smeared with graffiti.
Police say it will deploy tenants who don't abide by house rules
Angela Crow of Devon and Cornwall's police said the delogation was a clear warning to the other tenants who do not abide by house rules.
"This goes to those who think that they can behave without self-reproach as well as those who have been harmed by such behavior," she said.
Devon and Cornwall police will never tolerate criminal and anti-social behavior that will continue to work with partners to protect our communities.
Tourists in Portugal panicked after a low-flying military jet flies over beach
Sun worshippers in Aveiro, Portugal witnessed the shock of their lives
A Portuguese P-3C Orion Air Force's aircraft put down an incredible crash flight
The air forces said it was on a training flight, but insisted that there was no safety risk
This was the moment when terrified tourists begin to panic after a low-flying jet crashes down over a full beach.
Dramatic video footage showed how the military plane flew almost within reach above the sun worshippers on the stretch of beach in Aveiro in northern Portugal.
Holidaymakers jumped up as they understandably began to worry about the noise of the jet engines without first seeing what was approaching them through the clouds.
The incident took place yesterday afternoon in Costa Nova, near Aveiro.
Daniel Fernandes, one of the many holidaymakers who posted videos about the dramatic fly-by on social media, said: "It was flying twice really very deep and there was a moment when it looked like it was crashing."
Nuno Arroja added: "I've just been surfing and it felt like the plane was falling down on me."
Tourists on Aveiro beach were panicked when the plane came out of darkness and flew incredibly low
The Portuguese Air Forces today stated that the pilot of the P-3C Orion plane was on a training flight to identify fishing boats off the coast and had to fly so low that this was the only ability to detect them.
The spokesman said the jet flew slightly deeper than usual due to the poor visibility due to nearby wildfires, but the air forces insisted that safety was at no time at risk.
Landfires have destroyed parts of mainland Portugal this summer, in which four people were also killed on the island of Madeira.
The Portuguese air forces said today that the pilot had to fly so low due to the conditions - strong fog and fire smoke
The plane was on a training flight in which the pilot had to identify fishing boats as part of the exercise
Super Cup: Barca with worries against Sevilla
