Instead, they want to support the candidates of the Republicans for Congress.
Olympics: Brazen scam in doping test
The IOC wants to set up a disciplinary commission on the case.
Kenya's athletes are looming trouble after the second doping affair at the Rio de Janeiro Olympics.
After an attempted fraud at a doping check in the Olympic Village, Kenya has sent a coach home.
The International Olympic Committee said it would immediately set up a disciplinary commission to investigate the case.
A Kenyan official had announced that the former sprinter and today's coach, John Anzrah, had dispensed a urine rehearsal in the Olympic Village under the name of 800-meter runner Ferguson Rotich.
This had been reported to the team leadership of Kenya by the doping inspectors.
World governing body IAAF has asked the IOC for information on the case in order to pursue its own investigations, an IAAF spokesman said.
"Depending on the results of the investigation and the IOC's subsequent actions, the IAAF will make an assessment," an IAAF statement said.
"He (Anzrah) has gone to the doping control station and impersonated Ferguson Rotich, rehearsed and signed the rehearsal, explaining Kenya's chef de Mission Stephan Arap Soi.
He was possessed the accreditation of the athlete, who was on the World Anti-Doping Agency's list.
Ferguson Rotich finished fourth over 800 metres in Beijing in 2015.
Four days earlier, Kenyan athletics delegation chief Michael Rotich had been deducted from the Rio Games.
He is said to have informed athletes of his country about upcoming doping tests against haush money payments.
According to media reports, he is said to have been arrested on his return at Nairobi airport.
Kenya has been under special scrutiny at the IOC after numerous doping offences for a long time.
Announcing the IOC's more than 2000 doping target controls prior to the Rio Games was unusually pointed out in advance that athletes from Kenya and Russia in particular are being targeted.
The World Anti-Doping Agency had put a lot of pressure on the African country ahead of the Summer Games in Brazil to create an anti-doping law and a functioning control system.
The law was passed a few weeks before the start of Olympia.
Subsequently, WADA took Kenya off the list of countries whose doping control is compliant with WADA's rules.
Kenya is considered a great runner nation.
The country's athletes won the most medals at the 2015 World Cup in Beijing.
