"West Side Story" with Cecilia Bartoli
In the Shakespearean year, the Salzburg Festival presents the "West Side Story," starring Tony and Maria as a tragic loving couple, much like Romeo and Juliet.
A surprising Cecilia Bartoli.
A musical conquers Salzburg: the "West Side Story," Leornard Bernstein's masterpiece.
The artistic director Cecilia Bartoli wanted to bring the musical into the temple of classical music and fulfilled a youth dream with the role of Mary.
In the orchestra the Simón Bolívar orchestra under the direction of the Venezuelan conductor Gustavo Dudamel: "This music is full of rhythm," he enthuses.
We always say that she is very Latin.
There are a lot of Latin American ingredients in their tempo and melody.
It's a wonderful mix, the perfect recipe for our orchestra.
I'd say we really have Bernstein's music in our blood!
In this production, Bartoli plays an aged Maria.
She remembers her love affair with Tony with wistfulness, while the younger Maria plays and dances.
The role of Mary has always fascinated me.
First of all, because it's musically extraordinary!
And then there's Maria's personality.
She says it in her song: "I feel pretty, oh so pretty, I feel and witty and bright."
So she's smart, naïve, of course, and very passionate.
"She's very similar to me, to my Italian character, which I have as a Roman," Bartoli laughs.
