Turkey: Kurdish community expects mass escape
"Those who don't adapt and praise Erdogan sit on packed suitcases," says Ercan Karakoyun, the chairman of the Gülen-affiliated Foundation for Dialogue and the World.
The Kurdish community of Germany expects tens of thousands of people seeking protection to flee from Turkey to Germany at short notice.
In the medium term, it could even become "a few hundred thousand," the association's chairman Ali Toprak believes.
Due to the crackdown by Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan after the coup attempt, the Kurdish community of Germany is expecting a mass flight to Germany.
"In the short term, I expect tens of thousands, in the medium term with a few hundred thousand people seeking protection from Turkey in Germany, if the Erdogan regime continues to fight the minorities and the democratic opposition," the association's chairman Ali Toprak tells the "world" of Friday.
Because the government had already covered Kurdish strongholds months ago with punitive actions, 500,000 Kurds within Turkey are on the run.
After the thwarted coup, secular and opposition ethnic Turks would be added.
Many will want to start afresh in Europe if they are suppressed further in Turkey.
"It can't be that a state that takes in refugees itself is producing refugees in its own country," Toprak said.
The association chief lamented the targeted settlement of Syrian Arabs in Kurdish but also Alevi cities.
With this, Erdogan wants to increase the exit pressure on the remaining Kurds.
"They are the last large minority in Turkey, the Christian Armenians and Greeks have been exterminated or expelled in the past hundred years," said Toprak, who is also a CDU member.
The head of the association demanded that the federal government "position itself as sharply as Austria against Erdogan and his supporters."
There is also a "witch hunt" against Erdogan critics in Germany.
For the first time in the last few months, I don't feel safe in Germany.
The German branch of the Gülen movement also fears the flight of many Turks abroad.
More Turks will seek asylum in Germany, especially Kurds and opposition activists.
The branding as Gülen supporters destroys the professional existence of teachers and other academics.
"Those who don't adapt and praise Erdogan sit on packed suitcases, by the way also the Kemalists and seculaters," Ercan Karakoyun, chairman of the Gülen-affiliated Foundation for Dialogue and Education, told the "world."
As the newspaper reports, citing figures from the Federal Ministry of the Interior, the Turks made up the largest group of asylum seekers recognized for political persecution in Germany even before the coup.
As of June 30, this amounted to 11,386 people and thus almost 29 percent of all those recognised as eligible for asylum (39,625).
In this survey there are not those who fled from war, poverty or lack of prospects.
According to the Basic Law, only political, i.e. from the state, individually persecuted persons.
Mecklenburg-Vorpommern: "Go where it hurts"
Strange Mecklenburg-Vorpommern: The locals don't get anything from the summer election campaign - and holidaymakers from North Rhine-Westphalia are not allowed to choose the state parliament.
In Mecklenburg-Vorpommern they understood.
In 2021, the state election will not take place again directly at the end of the summer holidays, the Schwerin parliament has already decided at the beginning of the year.
Because it can't be stupider than before: summer holidays, heat and state election campaign - that bites.
The locals, who are to be persuaded by campaigners, are on holiday.
The vast majority of holidaymakers who are in the country, on Usedom, Rügen or Hiddensee, are not locals, so are not allowed to choose the state parliament.
In addition, there are practical problems: During the holidays there are helpers who hang up posters, inflate balloons, distribute flyers and end up counting votes, only laboriously to find.
Until September 4, 2016, the election and last day of holidays, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern has yet to make an election campaign narrowly past the people.
The hot phase, if you can say that at all, has just begun.
And it's also about something: Prime Minister Erwin Sellering, leader of an SPD/CDU coalition, must fear for his office.
The SPD, the strongest party since 1998, is now clearly behind the CDU in polls.
It is exciting to see how strongly newcomer AfD cuts off and what in the end remains suitable for the coalition.
Sellering, 66 years old and head of government since 2008, is doing an almost inconspicuous election campaign, apart from its large posters.
He travels through the country, distributes roses and chats a little with the people, just as his legendaryly barren predecessor Harald Ringstorff did.
Otherwill, Seelering has gotten cold feet and blames Chancellor Merkel for the AfD's blossoming.
Her refugee policy, her "we can do this" has accelerated the rise of the right-wing populists, which will probably cost him the beautiful office now.
The CDU with its top candidate, the Interior Minister Lorenz Caffier, has already experienced what it's like to campaign at the wrong time in the right place.
She has twice invited chancellor Angela Merkel to major rallies; Merkel's Bundestag constituency lies in the north-east.
But the target group was not right, there were more North Rhine-Westphalia, Berlin and Saxony there there there than Mecklenburgers and Vorpommern.
"There was probably the majority from other federal states," Vincent Kokert, secretary general of the CDU in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, complained recently in the NDR.
But it hurts no one from other corners of Germany to listen to Angela Merkel.
Otherwise, the Northeast CDU largely dispenses with the usual campaign stands on marketplaces and in front of shopping centers with umbrellas and balloons.
According to Kokert, this only plays a "minor role".
The Union, too, has noticed that the base is crumbling away from it in the countryside, that it has to make a great effort to find a connection to the people again.
Like the left, the SPD, the Greens, it is scarcely present in the expanses of the country.
However, the advice of the CDU electional campaigner Kokert to his colleagues is also self-exposing: "They have to go where it hurts, namely where the people are: village festivals, golden weddings, every birthday party must be used".
Because much is at stake, Sellering has now called for a particularly committed election campaign.
In polls, the Social Democrats were last with 22 percent behind partners CDU (25) and only narrowly ahead of the AfD (19).
In the 2011 election, the SPD had clearly won with 35.6 percent ahead of the CDU (22).
The Greens, small party with seven percent in the polls, are likely to make the leap into the state parliament.
They make a modern virtue out of holiday hardship and move their election campaign largely to the Internet.
"Online election campaign," the country's chaist Claudia Müller calls it.
Otherwise, green campaigners go in search of local voters among the holidaymakers.
Silke Gajek, the top candidate, is currently curling through the Mecklenburg lakes with a houseboat and finds it still beautiful.
Her impression: "Elective leave in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is not a punishment".
B27 redevelopment: Restaurants suffer from closure
There is nothing to complain about the speed with which the B 27 is refurbished.
For those responsible for the construction work, which starts at 5.30 in the morning and do not end until around 8pm, there is everywhere praise.
