"There were considerable excesses," says King, who made himself aware of the situation on site.
Two to three per minute have already been blitzed.
The quickest was with 77 kilometers per hour in the tempo 30 zone.
However, since the construction site moved on, it has become quieter.
For the business people on the B 27, it is only a small consolation that the road is now open again for customers from Rottenburg.
You don't like to look into the cards business people.
But that the B 27 renovation has led to slumps in sales, lets everyone you ask look through.
However, some are more and some less affected.
If you have less to do with the "end customer", i.e. produced for other companies, the renovation has hardly any consequences.
Thomas Sulz also speaks of a "maximum slight decline" at car dealership Maier, but also acknowledges that, of course "the running customers are lacking."
The most battered are the restaurants along the road.
J. Albert Göhner, owner of the Steakhouse, formerly Krone, finds clear words.
It's already existentially threatening.
Not only him, but all the restaurants were "hit hard".
Customers from Hechingen, Balingen and Rottweil were missing.
We have to be happy about every euro turnover.
He puts the revenue losses at "well over 50 percent".
The roast remediation had thrown his operation "back by two years."
Half of his employees had to be fired.
If I had a company with 4000 employees and half would have been dismissued, politics would have reacted differently.
Instead, Government President Jörg Schmidt has responded to Göhner’s question about whether there could be a financial compensation, pointing out that the restaurant would otherwise benefit from the advantages of the state on the B 27. Now it must also bear the disadvantages resulting from the refurbishment.
Göhner knows about other counties where something like this has been handled more generously.
In all the lawsuits, Göhner is sure that his restaurant will continue to exist.
After all, the family business has been located here since 1715, it refers to the long history.
He can understand that other regular guests now stop by less frequently because of the complicated traffic management.
If you can't drive here, it's also nice somewhere else.
Göhner has no doubts about the fact that his regular clientele will come back after the end of the refurbishment work.
Despite the crises with BSE and bird flu, Göhner considers the impact of the B 27 construction work on his clientele to be the biggest crisis his restaurant has experienced in its long-standing history.
Six months of construction, that’s brutal.
Laws are constantly being screweded in the name of counter-terrorism.
After the recent attacks, Interior Minister de Maizière is now presenting a thick security package.
But what does a toleration status have to do with terror?
And what does the Coalition partner say about all this?
Thomas de Maizière gives the reconciler.
All his proposals are "politically reasonable" to the coalition partner, says the Federal Minister of the interior at the presentation of his new security package.
They don't overwhelm anyone.
Other, broader points would first be discussed within the parties.
I'll get involved in that, too.
As interior minister, however, he is interested in results and an "achievable consensus" within the coalition.
De Maizière has let a little lapse after the recent attacks in Würzburg and Ansbach before presenting his conclusions for security in the republic.
However, they are now all the more extensive.
Some things are already known, proclaimed, targeted: that a new security agency should take care of cracking encrypted communication is not new.
Nor the fact that Germany has to implement several EU requirements on air passenger data and gun law.
The call for more video surveillance and personnel for the security agencies also comes as no surprise.
In part, de Maizière launches a second attempt for proposals that have not yet been made with the SPD: he wants to punish sympathy advertising for terrorist groups and jihadists with dual citizenship the German passport.
"These will be difficult points for the SPD," admits the CDU man.
Nevertheless, he considers this to be reasonable and does not demand anything, which has no chances with the coalition partner anyway.
De Maizière also presents a few new ideas.
Some of them have it in them: he is planning rapid procedures for deportations and more opportunities to imprison people before deportation.
Refugees who are specifically brought into the country should have their smartphones checked beforehand and disclose social media accounts for a security review.
In the case of tolerations for rejected asylum seekers, he wants to make some even worse than before - for example, those who commit crimes or deliberately throw away their papers in order to disguise their identities.
Some of it is politically and legally delicate.
Some things still seem unsophisticated and leave questions unanswered.
And on some points - in the unequal treatment of tolerated people, for example - the question arises: What does this actually have to do with security and terror?
Yes, says de Maizière, it is important to differentiate between the issues of refugees and terror.
But it cannot be argued that the assassins of Ansbach and Würzburg were refugees.
In addition, in view of the high number of refugees, many people were worried about security.
And things that kept people deeply occupied could not be excluded even in election campaign times.
Internal security has becomed enormously into focus.
Only the attacks in the neighborhood: Paris, Copenhagen, Brussels, Nice.
Then the attacks in Würzburg and Ansbach.
Uncertainty is great.
And yes, there are election campaigns.
Union and SPD are therefore increasingly positioning themselves on the issue of internal security.
In the past few days it has become high between the two parties.
First a nasty dispute over the equipment of the federal police, then about a security policy collection of ideas of some Union interior ministers, in which the department chiefs also demand a burqa ban and a departure from dual citizenship.
De Maizière, too, strives for conciliatory tones.
He thinks nothing of a departure from a double passport.
We pacified the discussion in Germany.
Opening them up again doesn't make sense.
And at a burqa ban, he has constitutional concerns.
Whether his own package at all corners is constitutional and whether it is actually "reasonable" for the coalition partner has yet to become apparent.
There's a lot in it that could cause a dispute.
However, the SPD is also currently trying to profile itself on the subject of internal security.
Party leader Sigmar Gabriel cautiously signals willingness to talk and says: "The SPD is willing to talk about everything that contributes to further increasing security".
Volkswagen is still getting through smoothly.
Despite diesel scandals, delivery numbers are rising worldwide.
However, things aren’t going smoothly in all markets.
In the recall, there are swirls around a term.
Volkswagen starts the second half of the year more subdued in deliveries.
Europe’s largest carmaker can continue to show a plus in the course of the year so far, despite the diesel affair thanks to its many brands and some well-running markets.
But in July, the core VW brand weakened, especially in Western Europe, and thus again weighed on overall sales, as the Dax listed company announced.
Worldwide, the VW brand sold 1.8 percent fewer vehicles in July with 449100 cars than in the same month last year.
In Western Europe alone, the second most important market after China, sales were 14 percent below the same month last year.
Deliveries fell by almost one-sixth in Germany's home market.
Brand sales CEO Jürgen Stackmann attributed the weaker figures from July mainly to two sales days less in Europe.
In addition, there has been reluctancence in some core markets due to "changed economic conditions".
In addition to the situation around the coup attempt in Turkey, the British vote to leave the EU has also weighed on, explained a spokeswoman.
In the USA, where the emissions affair first escalated, there is also still a minus.
The mainstay for VW is the Chinese market.
Overall, the mainly affected house brand of the Lower Saxons still comes through the diesel scandal: After the first seven months, only a delivery minus of 0.9 percent to 3.37 million vehicles.
Fred Kappler, Group Sales Director, continued to show an increase in sales from January to July.
Volkswagen delivered 1.3 percent more vehicles during this period, 5.904 million worldwide.
Volkswagen had manipulated exhaust gas tests with a software.
In Germany alone, VW is calling around 2.5 million cars for conversion into the workshops.
In the course of the conversion, VW affirmed that the conversion of the cars has no negative effects.
After the conversion of the vehicles, there is no deterioration in fuel consumption, CO2 emissions or engine output.
The car manufacturer issued the customer after the conversion a "guarantee" certificate, but does not speak of a "guarantee", as it was said on Friday in company circles.
The reason is legal intricacies.
The "Focus" had reported that Volkswagen is now granting its German customers a guarantee for converted diesel models.
This would have confirmed VW representatives in the Federal Ministry of Transport last Monday.
It has been confirmed from the Ministry of Transport that this meeting took place.
"VW has to fully meet the requirements of my Commission of Inquiry and the KBA."
"Deviations from this will not be accepted," Federal Transport Minister Alexander Dobrindt (CSU) declared, according to the ministry.
VW has assumed the guarantee that there will be no deteriorations in the event of fuel consumption, CO2 emissions or engine power after the conversion and that all vehicle values relevant to the type-approval will continue to exist.
Volkswagen itself, however, doesn't use the term "guarantee," as it said in corporate circles.
With the broad term "guarantee," Volkswagen sees the possibility that customers, for example, half a year after retrofitting an engine damage to a workshop - and could lead this back to the new software, even if the damage has nothing to do with it.
It is also important in the interest of customers to avoid that there is uncertainty with regard to possible warranty regulations in the case of any other damage that has nothing to do with the exhaust gas scandal, as it was said.
That's why VW doesn't use the term "guarantee."
