Destination records: who actually buys records?
Lonely middle-aged men love records.
Before you rush to the commentary part in order to litter it under the pseudany Non-LonelyMen's age with rough insults, this statement derives from up-to-date data.
According to YouGov, the much-discussed resurgence of records isn't due to a boom in millennials who want to welcome the news of physical items, but to midlife nostalgia.
Those who have recently bought a record are in all likelihood between the ages of 45 and 54.
In fact, the 18-24 age group is the most improbable buyer group.
It's also not a horde by hobby collectors - it has an emotional meaning: older record buyers are more likely to keep their feelings to themselves (56% vs. 53%) and enjoy spending time alone (69% vs 66%).
But are YouGov's results true?
If this blog were a news report on TV, the camera would follow me while running in the middle of a bustly street in Soho, wearing a modest grey suit and wildly gesturing ahead of me, holding my hands and saying something decisive like, "So, let's take a closer look at it."
So, let's take a look.
Even if the heart of Soho is slowly losing its charm - hotels, restaurants and offices now fill the buildings where independent vendors were once located - there are still a handful of fantastic record specialty shops.
I'd presumably ask the camera to turn off the camera for the next part where I'm standing in close proximity to various men in such shops and trying to assess their age and mind through observation.
Stuart from Glasgow, 55, followed by Sister Ray (data number: two young women, three middle-aged men) to Reckless Records (data: eight middle-aged men, one woman, presumably around 20), is for a day because of a meeting in London and goes through the shops to fill the few hours.
He's a collector, I ask?
"I appreciate already," he says.
I've got about 3,000 or 4,000 records.
These records, pictured above (he emailed me the snapshot, I didn't follow him home), dress out the walls of his living room.
The reason he owns such a significant number of albums and singles is the result of his disposable income: he is now able to buy records he couldn't afford when they appeared and re-buy items he sold when he was young and broke.
"A lot of things I'm buying are from the late 60s, early '70s that appeared when I was 11 or 12. Things I've presumably been a little too young to buy me," he says.
He agrees that those who buy records are more likely to be introverted and like to spend time alone, adding: "I don't smoke and I don't drink very much.
It's my truck.
Wez, 25, who works at Sister Ray, also believes that many customers he meets apply to the YouGov profile.
However, he has also noticed a new wave of people who are influenced by the media, people who have heard about the comeback and are causing themselves to buy their old records again.
Of the conversations I've had, people usually got rid of their collection around 1998 or 1999.
Customers who once sold their records to buy CDs are now selling their CDs to buy their records again," he says.
That would explain the boom of supporters of record revival of a certain age.
But what about the supposed emotional significance of records?
It only takes a few seconds in a shop like Phonica to realize that record business offers a reassuring community of like-minded people.
According to Wez, some of the older customers took offense at the contactless payment system.
Some record fans feel overwhelmed by the digital world and want to retreat to the stability of familiar record sleeves, from objects that evoke happy youth memories.
"I think sometimes it can be the filling of an emptiness or the possession of material possessions as a kind of consolation," says Wez.
That's how it's with me as a collector and I think it's like that with a lot of people.
It's an easy way to engage mentally.
"We're like their social workers!" an employee flutters from a stack of plates.
An ICM survey in April revealed that nearly 50% of people who bought a record last month haven't listened to it yet.
The survey also found that 41% own a turntable that they never use, while 7% of those who buy a record do not own a record player.
At this point in the news report, I'd presumably look very worried while scrolling through a pile of Bossa Nova compilations.
Jonny, 42, who works at Sounds of the Universe (data number: three middle-aged men), has noticed a "trophy aspect" of the revival.
"Someone didn't come in here recently and said, "I don't own a turntable, but I'd like to buy a Radiohead record so I can put it on the shelf," he says.
This isn’t a big percentage, but it definitely happens.
More product-heavy, less music-heavy.
We've got people saying, "I don't have a turntable."
These are younger people, not older ones, who are only slowly coming in.
Fopp, which has dedicated almost an entire floor to the records, has a more colourful audience than I walk in.
There are couples around 70, tourists taking photos, teenage boys and middle-aged women.
Even if the atmosphere is a little less relaxed than in the above-mentioned stores - Wild Beast's latest synthesizer and sex-packed pop instead of experimental jazz roaring from the loudspeakers - it's good to see such diversity among customers.
None of them look very lonely, but gnawing psychic trauma is difficult to detect when standing next to someone for 30 seconds.
Elanora, 27, has been walking around in the store for several minutes, looking at the variety of plates for sale.
She's more of a shop window stroll than a bulk purchase.
She doesn't deserve much, solding records isn't a real option, she says.
It'll cost a lot.
It's easy to listen to music from a computer or in any other way, but the beauty of records is..." she drifts off with a sigh in love.
I don't know how to explain it - it's really unique.
My last stop is a business that is said to have inspired a new generation of record lovers: Urban Outfitters.
As I give the appearance of sampling the denim hotpants, I watch a group of young teenage girls clustering around Polaroid cameras for a moment before a father and his daughter consider buying a Crosley turntable.
To the left is a stairwell, a wall full of plates by Adele, Jeff Buckley, Amy Winehouse and Fleetwood Mac: a mixture of contemporary music and classics, the essential records for an average collection.
No one looks at them - perhaps because they're just purely decorative, some so far above that it's impossible to touch them.
It's pure "plate art."
But given that any attraction for these items could mean more money for the industry and send some customers to the depths of Soho for more, there's nothing to complain.
It's no surprise that a demographic that has more time and money than others is also the one that spends most on luxury items such as records.
Even though my research revealed that YouGov’s results are correct, they leave, at least in record stores in Soho, disregarding the large number of female collectors and record lovers who exist in reality, record stores and online forums.
These people presumably work hard at school or offices.
They certainly don't spend their Thursday morning strolling through Berwick Street's record shops and scrolling through plates while avoiding the staring gaze of an eerie, 30-year-old false presenter towering over the shoulders of unsuspecting middle-aged men.
