French court rejects offer over demolition of shops in jungle refugee camp
A French court has rejected an offer from authorities in Calais to tear down dozens of provisional shops and restaurants in the jungle of Calais, where thousands of refugees live.
At a court hearing this week, authorities in Calais argued that the state should be empowered to tear down 72 provisional shops where people within the warehouse sell goods, from cigarettes to energy drinks, offer hairdressing services or tea for 50 cents (40 pence) or simple meals for a few euros in makeshift restaurants.
Calais Prefecture argued that these places should be demolished because they would constitute a parallel economy from which no taxes would be paid and which pose a risk of fire and sanitation.
Humanitarian groups informed the court that the shops and restaurants were important because the free meals offered by the state-backed association and other groups would not provide enough food for the growing numbers in the warehouse.
They also argued that the makeshift shops and restaurants would often provide accommodation and free meals for people in need.
A court in Lille ruled that there was no legal basis for the expulsion of people doing the 72-provisional businesses, but acknowledged that Calais's concerns were "absolutely comprehensible."
Judge Jean-François Molla said the grocery stores, cafes and restaurants would play a much bigger role than merely feeding people who "live in extremely precarious conditions."
He said the areas provide quiet meeting places between refugees and volunteers.
One of the places that was threatened by tearing was the Jungle Book Kids' Café of the camp, which is non-professional and offers 200 meals a day, English and French classes, as well as advice on asylum items for the hundreds of vulnerable, unaccompanied minors in the camp.
More than 170,000 people have signed the petition to uphold the Kids' cafe.
In a letter to the Guardian, it said the closure "would be a disaster for these children, the youngest of which are eight years old."
The two aid organizations Help Refugees and L'Auberge des Migrants estimated on Friday that the number of people living in the jungle camp had risen to over 9,000 people - the highest number so far - after a steep increase in new arrivals over the summer.
