Group joins start-up for live video game broadcasts
Watchinginging other people playing video games is a huge trend – Microsoft also wants to benefit from this.
The technology group buys the start-up Beam, which specializes in this hobby.
How much money is flowing for the takeover, Microsoft did not announce on Thursday.
Beam did not launch its offer until January and already has more than 100,000 users.
You can watch others play, but also intervene yourself.
For example, they can give up missions to players and face enemies, as well as provide pieces of equipment.
Activision Blizzard is launching a new HD video service for the booming world of esports.
On the way to the global media event, Facebook should help.
The advertising industry is already listening.
The employees of the start-up, which was previously based in Seattle in the US state of Washington, are moving to Microsoft's headquarters in Redmond, according to Beam co-founder and CEO Matt Salsamendi.
For the customers, therefore, nothing should change as a result of the takeover.
