Here's the Enforcement Trailer
Germany's racers have to adapt to the next generation of speedometers: In various federal states, among others in Hesse and Brandenburg, new flash units are being tested, which are intended to convise speed sinners particularly effectively.
The futuristic-looking Enforcement trailers of the company Vitronic from Wiesbaden can be used, for example, at accident focal points, where it would be too dangerous for a mobile measurement by the police.
For example, at motorway construction sites or bridges to be refurbished with temporary speed limits, there is often no necessary infrastructure to ensure compliance with the speed limit.
However, a permanent mobile structure can be too dangerous or complicated.
The Enforcement trailer is self-sufficient: its battery lasts five days during continuous measurement operation, the case data is transmitted encrypted via a modem.
With laser measurement technology, all vehicles can also be detected simultaneously across several tracks.
Variable speed limits as well as drive-through bans according to time, lanes and vehicle class can also be monitored.
According to the manufacturer, you can move the trailer with any car with a trailer hitch.
So that it is not simply stolen, it is lowered in measurement operation, so that the wheels disappear in the armored and, by the way, also bulletproof outer shell.
In addition, a built-in alarm system is intended to protect the speed camera from vandalism.
According to Vitronic, the trailer has been tested in various federal states since 2015 and has now also been used.
Foreign authorities are also flashing with the new technology: France has recently re-ordered 100 pieces, following an order of 150 pieces last year.
U.K. picks up silver in 7th rugby after Fiji Masterclend
Britain's impressive performance at 7's rugby came to an abrupt stop at the last hurdle in form of the Fijis in the final on Thursday night when they were beaten with a result of 43-7 at Deodoro Stadium here in Rio.
Fiji awarded a master class in handling, side-stepping, advancing, line running and raw power to win gold - her first Olympic medal of any colour.
Defeat was sweetened by the not-too-bad consolation prize of the silver medal - Britain's fourth that day - and they could be quite proud of their efforts in this tournament in which they beat New Zealand, Argentina and South Africa.
The Boks' significant defeat, 7-5 in the same-day semi-finals, could fall back on them in the final.
But regardless, Fiji would have beaten a 15-man team at its best at that form.
It could be a consolation for GB - but probably wonn't - that at least one Briton will have a gold medal hanging around his neck tomorrow: Fiji's head coach Ben Ryan, the former boss forward from Wimbledon.
GB had a difficult path behind it.
Eight years ago they lost every game at the Adelaide Sevens under Ryan.
They only lost on one here.
This slump is going to hurt, but the progression is astounding.
Fiji started wonderfully and let the attacks bounce GB out of fun.
It felt like you're running into moving buses for captain Tom Mitchell and Co. The World Series winners went 5-0 up when Osea Kolinisau pied over in the corner.
With another four minutes of playing time, Jerry Tuwai added yet another attack from the right corner that he converted.
12-0 and GB barely touched the ball.
An outstanding tackle by Mitchell halted the mountain of man Leone Nakarawa to scoring a third goal, but it only stopped Fiji.
In the seventh minute Jasa Veremalua in the right corner a touchdown and it stood 0-17 for GB.
Nakarawa added a new one since kick-off and there was still over a minute of playing time ... in the first half.
Vatemo Ravouvou squeaked through further faint tacklings of the GB team and stormed all the way to the line to make a goal.
The conversion brought a 29-0 until the break.
It's like Fiji was born to play the 7 while GB is stilling.
That's the way it is.
GB made fundamental changes at half-time and closed the Fijian gap...for a full four minutes, which was good for the conditions of the first half until Joshua Tuisova went through after a brilliant interplay from Fiji.
Dan Norton started a try for GB, made a touchdown in the left corner for a 36-7, but the game had long gone in favor of the South Sea Islanders, whose fans were singing, waving flags and dancing in the grandstand.
It wasn't hard to admire Fiji's playful approach.
Mata Viliame set the icing on the cake with another try in the final seconds.
The whistle sounded, the fijis embraced each other and GB kicked crushingly beaten into the turf.
Earlier that day, Fiji had defeated Japan in the semi-finals with 20-5 and Britain pushed South Africa out of the race in a narrow semi-final match - Britain's conversion of the difference.
Kyle Brown brought the Boks forward in the first half, but Dan Norton broke through the line with an impressive footwork and scoring a goal under the post in the second half.
A brilliant late tackling by Marcus Watson, Anthony's brother of the English 15-Aside team, secured the win - and thus the silver medal.
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning inspired to rudder victory
There will be no more emotinal gold than at these games and that means something.
From the first rowing strike to the last, 220 hard Hieve going down in history, Helen Glover and Heather Stanning found an additional meaning in their golden moment at the Estacio da Lagoa.
Even if it was only the two who sat at the helm, there were three voices in the boat. The echo of coach Robin Williams, was all-presenting when they rowed for the second Olympic gold without a helm's wife in a rounder, the first women in the history of British sport who laid such a benchmark.
Her dominance was absolute, after all, her 39th undefeated deployment, which is also unprecedented.
What we see are two brilliant female athletes gliding across the surface with the elegance of swans, which doesn’t entirely reflect the nature of triumph.
Behind every gold in rowing there's a battalion of willing helpers contributing to the incremental advances that make the difference and it's been more helpful than the coach who didn't know 30 months ago whether he'd even survive this day when he was diagnosed with cancer.
Like he said, he's a little risky.
I've had bladder cancer that's operable and they did.
If you don't do that, he'll spread everywhere.
It would have been a pretty bad situation.
It changes the outlook a bit.
Williams was diagnosed in December 2013.
The trio were lucky that he was able to start his treatment in early 2014, out of season, which kept the interruption to a minimum, if that is the right term in such circumstances at all.
I needed money, so I had to get better soon.
It's been an incentive.
I've been at the hospital and walking around on the ward - 20 feet, 30 feet, 50 feet at a time when they said some steps would suffice.
That's how it starts.
Rowinging itself is already a challenge, but that makes it an even bigger one.
In the immediaty of the victory, her first thoughts went to Williams, who spent a nerve-wracking morning talking a little more than usual, trying to process his own fears.
Stanning, who was a captain at the Royal Artillery Regiment in a deployment in Afghanistan, said "I've been an emotional wreck this week. That's maybe a bit extreme, but it means so much."
It only repeats how much this means to me, how much I and Helen and Robin have worked.
It's been three fantastic years with Helen and Robin.
We wouldn't be anyone without Robin, so a massive thank you goes to him. He's the best coach in the world.
I couldn't have wished for a better person to train or a better coach.
Helena Glover and Heather Stanning haven't lost a race in the past five years.
The pair had a hard rhythm that their opponents couldn't keep up with.
Even in the last 500 meters, when the teams from New Zealand and Denmark started catching up, Stanning and Glover had too much in reserve.
We were very caught at the moment.
Helen shouted "stay in the process, stay in the process."
It's really important not to get carried away.
We had a good start and at the same time we weren't looking to do anything great.
We didn’t want fireworks today, we just wanted to get a good result, go out and win the race, which we did.
The couple will take time off for a year before making decisions about their future.
Glover must appear at a wedding next week, her own with TV adventurer Steve Backshall.
Now she just wants to enjoy the moment.
I didn't want to say this was my last race.
This would have meant too much pressure.
In London it took around six months to realise that I'm an Olympic champion.
It was all so new to me and I was so stressed.
It took about four minutes here.
That feels so much better," she said.
Provided clarity for 31 years in an exemplary manner
The managing director of the "Veitensteingruppe", Ruppert Göller, is retiring.
The association is considered "exemplary-wide".
A small Franconian drinking water association as a model for the big water producers.
So it said some time ago in the original tone in a contribution of the Bayerischer Rundfunk.
A little later, the Water Forum paid tribute to the government of Lower Franconia's special purpose association.
The work of the Veitenstein Group is considered "exemplary throughout Bavaria".
Rupert Göller, who has been an employee for 31 years in the special purpose association, stands behind all this as managing director for 28 years.
He was retired at the age of 65.
The drinking water association of the Veitenstein group, based in Kottendorf, Breitbrunn, conveys about 150000 cubic meters of water annually and supplies 32 places and hamlets with about 5500 inhabitants.
Member municipalities are the municipalities of Kirchlauter, Breitbrunn, Lauter, Ebelsbach and the city of Baunach.
There are water supply contracts for other cities and municipalities.
The current chairwoman, Breitbrunn's mayor Gertrud Bühl (FW), recalled that Rupert Göller was hired in 1985 as a water manager and was already appointed managing director of the special purpose association in 1988.
"His tasks have been very diverse and they ranged from water pipeline construction and maintenance work to office and organizational activities, budget preparation, calculation of fees and contributions to emergency operations on weekends," the chairwoman said.
She cited the commissioning and inauguration of the new wells (1994), land purchases in the area of new wells (1998) and compensation for farmers, the renovation of the high tanks (1999), the new acquisition of a central computer control (2004) and the partial renewals of water pipes.
There was no standstill and innovations have been recorded again and again, because water means life and drinking water is the most important food.
In her laudation, she praised Göller's faithful, reliable and correct service over 31 years.
He campaigned with a lot of passion for his water association and he has literally lived the water association as if it were his own company.
Just a few weeks ago, as a real practitioner, he was able to present the successful concept of the Veitenstein group for nitrate lowering in front of the water forum of the district of Lower Franconia.
For this, he received a lot of recognition and praise.
Rupert Göller, for his part, praised Wasserwart Alfons Griebel, who had worked with him for 28 years.
He's the best waterman and he's been a loyal squire.
Together we were an unbeatable team.
From his point of view, he described the well construction and the restructuring of the special purpose association as highlights.
However, the biggest challenge and the most difficult operation were the pipe break at the high container near Kirchlauter on 3 January.
Here it will have to work at minus 17 degrees throughout the night, so that the villages could be supplied with water.
The supply of Neubrunn could only be maintained once, because the high container from Pettstadt was filled with hoses.
The management of the drinking water association was handed over to Daniel Stürmer from Lauter, who succeeds Rupert Göller.
The soprano Anna Netrebko openly deals with the autism of her son Tiago - and turns to other affected parents: "They must have the courage to talk about it."
"Medicine has made great progress," the 44-year-old told the Italian newspaper Corriere della Sera.
When the disease was recognized by her son, she believed she had to die.
Eight-year-old Tiago has progressed well and is now learning privately in New York, where there are the best therapists.
"To give an example: when I ask him something that was two years ago, when I sang at Milan's Scala, he doesn't remember anything," Netrebko said.
But he is the happiest person on earth.
Tiago comes from Netrebko's association with the bass baritone Erwin Schrott.
She is now married tenor Yusif Eyvazov.
Relationship in Bargteheide - man shoots at partner
A 35-year-old man shot at his partner at around 10.45 a.m. on Friday in an apartment building in Bargteheide (Stormarn district) and fatally injured her.
"The man himself called us and stated that he had shot his significant other," a police spokeswoman said.
The perpetrator is on the run, according to him is being searched publicly.
Meanwhile nationwide, because Sven S. could be traveling by car.
The man is about 1.80 meters tall, muscular, brown-burned and has a bald head.
In the meantime, the police have released a photo of him.
However, it is a few years old.
In the meantime, the man wears a bald head.
The man should not be approached directly.
He could still carry the gun with him, according to the police.
If you see Sven S., you should call 110.
The police are talking about an "act of relationship."
The victim was born in 1988, the perpetrator in 1981.
The competent police in Lübeck did not initially want to provide any information about the course of action or the relationship between perpetrators and the victim; however, it confirmed that a person had been killed.
“It’s a homicide.”
"That's all we're saying right now," said a police spokeswoman in Lübeck.
According to eyewitnesses, a special operations squad (SEK) was at the scene.
Steel - Glimmer for German steel industry
The crisis-ridden and energy-intensive German steel industry is spared from additional costs in the millions.
Financial burdens that threatened the industry from green electricity funding are off the table.
It remains with the exemption from the renewable energy surcharge for the existing plants for the so-called self-power production in the German steel industry, said Federal Economy Minister Sigmar Gabriel (SPD) of the "Westdeutsche Allgemeine Zeitung" (WAZ, Friday edition).
"I'm really happy that we've finally been able to achieve that after two years of tough negotiations."
The European Commission had therefore initially planned to ask the steel manufacturers, who use gases from the blast furnace to generate electricity for their own consumption.
The steel industry threatened additional costs of 120 to 240 million euros per year, according to the German industry association.
Gabriel told WAZ that there was now an agreement agreement with the European Commission after talks with Competition Commissioner Margrethe Vestager.
Only when the generators in the steel mills are replaced, an EEG surcharge of 20 percent is incurred.
But even this is not raised in the steel industry.
IG Metall welcomed Germany's unification with the EU Commission on self-electricity generation.
Thus, the status quo of the previous regulation is largely stipulated.
Gabriel’s successful efforts are of particular importance for the steel industry.
“With our ‘Steel is the future’ campaign, this is a first step.”
"Others have to follow."
"Climate and employment targets must also be weighed up in the reform of emission rights trading," explained IG Metall CEO Jörg Hofmann in Frankfurt.
The steel industry in Europe has been suffering from overcapacity and significant price pressure since the 2008 financial crisis.
Last year, the situation intensified further, when the world’s largest producing country China threw mass steel on the world market in the face of weakening domestic demand.
European manufacturers accuse Chinese steel companies of price dumping.
Over the course of this spring, the situation has eased somewhat.
On the one hand, imposed import tariffs on import steel have an effect, on the other hand, the economic prospects in China have brightened.
It is not clear how sustainable the price recovery is.
Germany's industry leader Thyssenkrupp remained cautious on Thursday.
Most recently, the prices would have stagnated again.
The industry is responding to the difficult situation with further cost reductions.
Thyssenkrupp did not want to rule out closures of individual plants.
Around 86000 people were employed in the German steel industry at the end of 2015.
In July, German steelmakers will remain cautious despite rising prices and increasing orders.
At 3.4 million tons, they produced a good six percent less crude steel than in the same month of the previous year, as the business association Stahl announced on Friday.
After a brief increase in May, the quantity produced now declined for the second month in a row.
Since the beginning of the year, production has been two percent lower than in the previous year.
Thanks to an improved order situation, the association expects an increase in emissions in the coming months.
Community, spirit, health: Singing does seniors good
Ursel Kipp has accompanied the singing all her life: as a young girl she was in the school choir, she took the subject of music, and today the 78-year-old sings in a senior choir.
"Singing moves me - emotionally and physically," she describes.
After a rehearsal, she feels balanced, cheerful, and overall more alive, she says.
By the way, she does something good for her health and her mind.
From several perspectives, singing is a great hobby for seniors, says Erhard Hackler from the German Senior League.
Most seniors sing in the choir and share the joy of music with like-minded people, Hackler describes.
Through the regular togetherness, seniors can establish and maintain social contacts.
Because often social and family structures dissolve in old age, a chorus is then an ideal opportunity to spend time with other people.
Ursel Kipp also had this experience in her chorus: "Contrude and organize, to be able to rely on others and to learn from each other - a chorus is a very special form of togetherness".
Singinging can be exhausting in a positive sense, Hackler says.
After all, seniors train deep and controlled breathing.
If you demand your lung regularly, you will not get out of breath so quickly in everyday life.
Even when it comes to preserving the sound of the voice in old age, singing is an optimal hobby.
Singing often has a very special effect on older people, says Ursula Lenz from the Federal Association of Senior Citizens’ Organisations.
In the older generations, singing together had a much higher importance, it was part of everyday life, both in school and in leisure time, she explains.
Lenz often observes that singing or listening to songs awakens many memories in the elderly, often associated with positive feelings of their adolescence.
Through song lyrics and especially melodies, self-depressed people remember long-ago events.
When singing, the head is also demanded of a special performance, explains Lenz.
At the same time focusing on one’s own voice and on a given rhythm and adapting to the other singers – this is a great cognitive challenge.
Older people who live alone in this way practice not focusing solely on themselves.
Another positive aspect is the recognition that one learns by singing in the choir, Hackler thinks.
An enthusiastic audience is great praise.
The self-esteem also grows by singing in the choir.
To show yourself: If I practice for something for a long time, I get better and grow beyond myself - this experience strengthens.
Good reasons to start singing, there are enough.
If you want to try out a new hobby, you should not hesitate - even if you think you have little talent, advises Lenz.
You don't sing alone - with 50 singing people, who hears when a sound goes wrong?
In search of the right offer, seniors have different options, Hackler says.
If someone from the acquaintances sings in a senior choir, you can simply join.
Many cities also offer overviews of the choirs in the area.
Anyone wishing to sing in a church choir can contact the church leader directly.
Many senior choirs can also be found over the Internet.
If you do not have access to the Internet, you can ask family, friends or neighbors for help with Internet research.
If you feel like singing, you should definitely join a choir, Hackler advises.
Singing is good for the soul and has something liberating - this applies to all ages.
Cats react more sensitively than hangovers to cries of the juveniles
Biologists have investigated in Hanover the effect of calling cat babies on adult animals.
To their surprise, the meowing didn't leave the males cold either - however, unlike the females, they always behaved the same way.
Female cats can recognize the degree of excitation of cat babies in the meowing of the little ones.
They react ten percent faster to sounds of kittens, who feel themselves in great need, as scientists from the University of Veterinary and the Hannover Medical School (MHH) have found.
Hangovers also react to the calls of kittens - but always the same.
"Apparently, the acoustic differences between meowing less and strongly aroused juveniles are either imperceptible or not significant for the males," said biologist Wiebke Konerding of the MHH.
She is the first author of the study, which was published this Friday in the journal "BMC Evolutionary Biology".
In a previous study, the team of researchers had already found that the duration and pitch of the kitten screams differ with the urgency of their need for help.
A total of nine adult hangovers and eight cats from the animal husbandry of the MHH were observed.
On the one hand, they were played calls from little ones who were taken out of the littering box for three minutes and separated from mother and siblings.
On the other hand, they were confronted with screams of more excited kittens, which were additionally raised.
The researchers consider their results to be meaningful despite the small sample.
"All the females, even those who did not yet have their own offspring, reacted differentiatedly to the screams," Konerding said.
They turned faster to the loudspeaker, when this calls of the very more aroused kittens played out.
This ability may be innate or develop with sexual maturity.
"A surprise was that the males reacted at all," the biologist said.
In the past, scientists have already examined the reactions of women and men to baby sounds on several occasions in humans.
Also, some studies showed that women reacted more sensitively to infants, regardless of whether they were already mothers or not, reported biologist Marina Scheumann.
Podolski reaches for Supercup with "Gala"
Hakan Sükür is a football legend in Turkey.
An arrest warrant has been issued in Turkey against former football star Hakan Sükür.
He is accused of being a member of a terrorist group.
For the first time after the coup attempt, an important football match will take place again in Turkey.
Ahead of the arch-rivals between Besiktas Istanbul and Lukas Podolski's Galatasaray Istanbul for the Supercup in Konya, the official line is unequivocal: "Business as usual".
Even with the imposition of the state of emergency after the coup attempt in July, the government asserted that the everyday life of ordinary citizens should not be compromised.
However, this does not apply to those who are suspected of belonging to the movement of preacher Fethullah Gulen, which is blamed for the coup attempt.
This does not stop at football either.
Prosecutors issued an arrest warrant against former Starkicker Hakan Sükür.
According to the Sükür, now living in America, is accused of being a member of a terrorist group.
Sükür is said to be a supporter of the preacher Fethullah Gülen, who is blamed by President Recep Tayyip Erdogan for the coup attempt.
Although the state of state of emergency initially imposed for 90 days still applies until October, this actually has almost no effect on the lives of most Turks.
The nocturnal "democracy guards" in public places across the country declared Erdogan finished on Wednesday.
The sport - like the whole country - is divided into supporters and opponents of Erdogan.
Especially fans of the workers' club Besiktas do not traditionally count among the president's friends.
What unites the majority of Turks, however, in the face of dramatic experiences in history, is the strict rejection of military coups.
Even the fans agree - regardless of whether their heart beats for Galatasaray or Besiktas.
With cup winner "Gala," it will come down to Lukas Podolski in particular.
The Kölner proved his sporting value in the past season: With 17 goals and nine submissions in all competitions, the 31-year-old ended his first season as his club's top scorer.
In the final match against Fenerbahce, he scored the 1:0 victory.
The "Fanatik" newspaper cheered him: "He was the best on the pitch."
He finished the season in an excellent way and secured the trophy for his team.
Thanks to "Poldi," the disappointing season for the record champion found a conciliatory conclusion.
After the preliminary round knockout in the Champions League, the "Lions" also retired early in the Europa League.
In the Süper Lig it was only enough to rank sixth.
The season wasn't good.
The trophy was the only way to win anything.
"That's what we managed and that's the most important thing," Podolski said.
For Master Besiktas, the playing year went better.
Especially thanks to Mario Gomez, who laid the base for the 14th title with 26 hits of the season as Süper-Lig top scorer king.
This is also why fans continue to hope for the 31-year-old's return.
Gomez, who was under contract with AC Florence and was only loaned to Besiktas, had announced that he would not return because of the political situation in the country after his European Championship holiday.
"If Mario wants to come to us, he just has to say it," the trade magazine "Kicker" quoted club boss Fikret Orman as saying.
However, the striker would have to be prepared to forgo around a third of his €7.5 million annual salary estimated at €7.5 million, Orman said: "We can't pay his salary at Florence."
Despite doping: These athletes still have medal chances at Olympics in Rio
34-year-old Justin Gatlin has been banned twice for doping (2001 and 2006).
Rio de Janeiro - Whistleblower Yulia Stepanowa is not allowed to start in Rio.
Other convicted athletics sinners can only laugh about it - they're battling for medals in Rio.
Doping continues to determine the headlines at the Rio Olympics.
Russian swimmer Yuliya Yefimova, who had sued her right to start shortly before the games after a doping curfew, was mercilessly whistled and booed at her start (100 meter chest).
It offered itself a whimsical scenery: With a smile put on, she was still proud in the hall and provoked with her performance.
When it was clear that she would not fetch gold, the audience broke out into a cheering storm.
However, there are numerous other athletes who will compete at the Olympic Games in Rio despite a doping past.
Here's an overview:
Justin Gatlin (100, 200, 4x100-m relay/USA): The 34-year-old is one of the most controversial figures in athletics.
He was already banned twice for doping (2001 and 2006 to 2010).
Since his captivation, the Olympic champion of 2004 and former world champion runs even faster than before, he is the number one in the world over 100 m in 9.80 seconds and is considered the biggest challenger of Usain Bolt.
Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce (100, 4x100m relay/Jamaica): The two-time Olympic champion and seven-time world champion had to serve a six-month suspension in 2010 after she has been diagnosed with taking the painkiller oxycodone.
She had a severe toothache after an operation, the 29-year-old defended herself.
Sandra Perkovic (Discus/Croatia): In 2011, the Olympic champion from 2012, world champion from 2013 and European champion from 2010 to 2016 tested positive for the banned stimulant methylhexanamine.
Perkovic denied the knowing intake and was banned for six months.
The four widest throws this year go to the account of the big gold favorite.
LaShawn Merritt (200, 400, 4x400-season/USA): The Beijing Olympic champion and two-time world champion over 400 m, had tested positive in 2010 and banned for 21 months.
A means for penis enlargement had been responsible, Merritt asserted.
This year, the 30-year-old is the world's fastest man over 200 and 400 m to date.
Ivan Tichon (hammer throw, Belarus): Hardly an athlete was subsequently stripped that many medals.
So Tichon lost his 2005 World Cup title nine years later after he was diagnosed with testosterone doping.
The now 40-year-old also had to surrender Olympic silver in 2004 because of steroid doping and the 2006 European Championship title because of testosterone abuse.
He received the bronze medal of the 2008 Olympic Games, which was also revoked, on the basis of a verdict by the International Court of Sport CAS.
At 80.04 m, he ranks second in the world rankings.
Liu Hong (20 km walk, China): The world record holder and gold favorite only tested positive in May, but is still allowed to start in Rio.
The 29-year-old world champion found evidence of taking a prohibited dietary supplement, the suspension of the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) was mild and expired on July 13.
Bombing terror in Thailand - Austrian woman injured - Several dead
The Austrian embassy in Thailand is in contact with the woman, Foreign Ministry spokesman Thomas Schnöll said Friday morning.
She suffered lacerations and had been treated in the hospital, but had already been able to leave it again.
She had planned her journey home for the beginning of September.
It is still unclear whether Austrian nationals will be affected by the further explosions on the tourist island Phuket as well as in the southern cities of Surat Thani, Phang Nga, Trang and Chumphon.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs is in constant contact with the local authorities in this regard, according to Schnöll.
In Hua Hin, located about 200 kilometers south of the capital Bangkok, two bombs exploded on Thursday evening in a nightlife district.
According to the police, one Thai woman was killed and about 20 people were injured, including the Austrian woman, three Germans and three Italians.
On Friday morning, two explosive devices went up again, according to the authorities, another person was killed.
A bomb explosion was also reported by Phuket in the morning.
According to police, a taxi driver was injured in the detonation near Patong beach, which is popular with holidaymakers.
In Surat Thani on the country's south-east coast, another bomb then explodeded, killing a municipal employee.
According to the authorities, the explosive device went into the air in a flower bed in front of the precinct of the coastal police.
Finally, detonations in Phang Nga and Trang on the south-west coast as well as in Chumphon on the south-east coast demanded several injured.
One assumes a connection with the explosions in Hua Hin, it said.
"The bombings bear the same handwriting," Army General Danai Kritmethavee told reporters Friday morning.
"We assume it is a coordinated attack."
It is too early to comment on possible motives, but it is probably the actions of Muslim rebels.
Thai media also wanted to recognize in the attacks a pattern typical of the bombing of Muslim separatists in the south of the country.
The Thai police, on the other hand, stated that the attacks were not related to the Islamist insurgencency movement or to international terrorism.
"This is only local sabotage, confined to limited areas and provincies," a spokesman in Bangkok said.
Rather, it is assumed that the bombs were laid by opponents of the ruling military junta.
Authorities have received intelligence allegations of imminent attacks in the south of the country in recent days in recent days - but they had not been informed about the exact time and the locations.
It is still unclear which group was behind the attacks.
Police instructed their officials across the country to step up security around key government buildings, as well as airports, train stations, tourist attractions, restaurants and amusement neighborhoods.
There are always smaller bombings in Thailand, but they are usually not directed against tourists.
Hua Hin houses a palace that has been used for years by Thailand's King Bhumibol Adulyadej.
After the now bomb explosions, the Austrian Ministry of Foreign Affairs recommends on its homepage to follow the instructions of the Thai security authorities.
High security risk (security level 3) applies to Narathiwat, Yala, Pattani and Songhkla provinces as well as Preah Vihear and the surrounding area.
After clashes between security forces and Muslim sections of the population are advised against unnecessary travel to these provinces.
Increased security risk (security level 2) applies to the rest of the country.
The attacks on Thursday and Friday took place a few days after a disputed referendum on a constitution drafted by the military junta.
This grants the military great influence even in the coming years.
Junta chief and Prime Minister Prayut Chan-o-cha said the bombs had been an attempt to create chaos as the country moves towards stability, an improvement in the economic situation and tourism.
The military unta, which seized power in 2014 after a period of bloody unrest, considers itself to have led the kingdom back to stability and order.
However, the military has also failed to get to grips on the uprising of Muslim rebels, which has been simmering for years in the far south of the country.
More than 6500 people have already been killed in the conflict.
Nearly a year ago, 20 people were killed in an attack on a popular Hindu shrine in Bangkok - most of them foreign tourists.
It was the bloodiest attack in the country's recent history.
The right-wing "identitarian movement" has been targeted by the Federal Office for the Protection of the Constitution.
After several state offices, constitutional protectors in the Confederation have also put the group under scrutiny.
"We see indications in the 'identitarian movement' for efforts against the liberal democratic basic order," constitutional protection chief Hans-Georg Maaßen told the German Press Agency in Berlin.
Thus, immigrants of Islamic faith or from the Middle East are defamed in an extremist way.
This is why we are now observing the movement as well.
The "identitarian movement" is a group with French roots, which has also been active in Germany since 2012.
It opposes "multicultural madness," "uncontrolled mass immigration" and the "loss of one's own identity through alienation."
Constitutional protectors in nine federal states - Bremen, Bavaria, Hesse, Berlin, Baden-Württemberg, NRW, Lower Saxony, Saxony and Thuringia - are already observing the "identitarians".
Maaßen said his authority had coordinated closely with the state offices in the decision.
"Groups like 'Identitarian Movement' are trying to pick up their target group where it stands," he said.
In general, extremists try to address them with youthful language, often with poppy music.
Islamists and right-wing extremists would do that.
This isn't a significant advertisement for one's own cause.
The propaganda is supposed to appeal to people emotionally.
Young people are susceptible in a special way.
It's dangerous.
Most recently, there had been reports from several federal states about contacts of the "identitarians" with AfD politicians.
Maaßen said he had no knowledge of this.
Possible contacts of this kind are relevant for the protection of the Constitution only from a certain point.
It depends on whether the respective party - in this case the AfD - changes its direction and thus becomes extremist through certain persons who are extremist.
The decisive factor, therefore, is whether such persons have a controlling influence on the party.
This is not recognizable.
In view of the significant increase in right-wing-motivated offences and attacks on asylum seekers and refugee shelters, Maaßen said he would not yet speak of new right-wing terrorism in this context.
But in recent years we have found that there can be right-wing terrorist structures.
There was the 'Old School Society' or the Freitaler group.
It may well be that other right-wing terrorist groups or micro-groups are forming, planning attacks against asylum seekers, against refugee shelters or attacks for other right-wing extremist motives.
However, Germany-wide or Europe-wide management of right-wing terrorism is not recognizable.
The previous groups were created at the regional level or in social networks.
Maaßen expressed concern that many assaults went to the account of people who had not previously appeared in the right scene.
This is the worrying thing.
Many of the suspects in attacks on asylum shelters had not previously been known as criminals, politically motivated offenders or followers of the right-wing scene.
“Many people have therefore committed criminal offences that previously had nothing to do with right-wing extremists, who were perhaps politically indifferent or, for example, elected the CDU, SPD or leftists, but who then radicalized through right-wing extremist sentiment.”
Rio 2016: Robel Kiros Habte becomes an internet hit
The Olympians or world records do not always determine the headlines at the Olympics.
This is evidenced by Robel Kiros Habte’s courageous performance at the Summer Games in Rio de Janeiro.
At the Aquatics Center, the slightly overweight swimmer from Ethiopia swammed so slowly that he could no longer be seen in the TV pictures at times.
He simply disappeared - no matter how far the snippet was selected.
On the social networks, Habte subsequently became a viral Internet hit.
In fact, the 24-year-old finishes his run-up with a 48-second gap and finished 59th out of 59 starters.
However, it was rather his not exactly sporting figure that attracted the attention of the international media.
Why the 24-year-old is still competing in the Olympics?
The Ethiopian got a special starting right from the international swimming federation.
This is distributed to make swimming better known in underrepresented countries.
Since most athletes are running in Ethiopia, Habte simply wanted to do something different.
Preparation is everything: Robel Kiros Habte is getting ready for the big race.
The first nicknames quickly made the rounds.
Some medics referred to him as "robel of the whale."
Based on a "belly of prosperity," the English tabloid "The Sun" with "Dad Bod" made fun of him.
His time, he struck only with a minute and almost five seconds over the 100 meters freestyle, did not matter.
Comparations with "Eric the Eel" were also drawn.
At the 2000 Sydney Olympics, Eric Moussambani's run attracted media attention.
Unforgettable was the appearance of the swimmer from Equatorial Guinea, when he had to compete alone in a lead-up over 100 meters freestyle.
Frenetically, Moussambani was cheered on at the time.
The 22-year-old only learned to swim eight months before the Olympics and had never completed such a long distance before the competition.
A seat in the front rank of the stunning architecture of the Los Angeles Central Library
Find out more about the history of the central library in the city centre, as the Society of Architectural Historians/Southern California Chapter holds a salon starring Arnold Schwartzman and Stephen Gee, the authors of the new book "Los Angeles Central Library: A History of its Art and Architecture."
It is envisaged that Gee and Schwartmann will discuss and share pictures of the 1926 Bertram Goodhue design, as well as the 1993 restoration and addition by Hardy Holzman Pfeiffer Associates.
The salon is scheduled for August 21 from 14.00 to 16.00 at Salle Moderne at the Art Deco Southwestern School of Law, (formerly Bullocks Wilshire), 3050 Wilshire Blvd.
Visitors can visit the middle hall, style room, cactus lounge and tea salon.
Pre-salling tickets only; SAH/SCC members, $15; non-members, $25.
An open industrial loft in DTLA gets a cosy makeover
We are celebrating L.A.'s central library's 90th birthday with - what else? - a new book
Geretsried - The owner of a shop on the New Square has been stolen.
According to the police, the 67-year-old Geretsried woman had left her business at around 5.30pm on Monday, forgetting closing a window on the ground floor.
When she came back the next morning at around 8.15 a.m., about 250 euros had disappeared from the cash register.
Traces of the perpetrator could not be found.
There is also no video surveillance.
Trump doesn't see the looming election defeat so tight
Republican presidential nominee Donald Trump speaks at a campaign rally in Florida.
If it becomes nothing with the election to the US president, Donald Trump already has an alternative idea.
He's just going on vacation.
If Donald Trump loses the U.S. presidential election in November, he wouldn't care: "Ultimately, it's either going to work out, or I'm having a very, very nice, long vacation," the Republican candidate said in a phone interview with U.S. television station CNBC.
If it'sn't enough for him in the end for reasons of political correctness, that's fine, Trump said.
That's how he referred to U.S. President Barack Obama and Democrat's nominee, Hillary Clinton, as the founder of the Islamic State (IS) terrorist militia (IS).
Within his party, more and more resistance formed recently.
Post enters the trade of electric vans
An electric delivery vehicle from Deutsche Post in action.
The interest of other companies in the vehicle is apparently great.
The post office plans to sell its StreetScooter electric vehicle as well.
Shortly, the group intends to start trading with the van.
From next year, Swiss Post will also offer its electric vans on the free car market.
"We want to get into the sale to third parties," a Post spokesman said on Friday.
The company has been checking for months whether to sell its self-developed StreetScooter, with which letters and parcels are delivered, to other establishments.
Postal board member Jürgen Gerdes had said there were always demands, for example from craftsmen.
According to the news magazine "Der Spiegel", the Bonn-based group intends to soon enter the mass production of the vehicles.
In its post-owned manufacturing, the company plans to build up to 10,000 cars per year per shift.
Gerdes had announced that the Group wants to significantly expand its fleet of electric cars and is targeting a complete conversion to environmentally friendly vehicles in the long term.
"We will take a very, very robust step towards 2020," he had said at the end of July.
I can imagine that we're changing the entire fleet.
The StreetScooter has been active on Germany's roads since 2014.
GfK leadership resigns after disappointing business figures
The consumer research company GfK exchanges its boss Matthias Hartmann.
The resignations came suddenly - but not entirely surprising.
The difficult situation of the market research company GfK suggested that the chair of CEO Matthias Hartmann becomes more insecure the longer the successes he promised.
Turbulence at the head of the Nuremberg-based market research company GfK: After disappointing business figures and the resignation of corporate management, the world’s number five in the industry is facing a new beginning in terms of personnel.
GfK CEO wants to leave the company at the end of the year.
The Chairman of the GfK Supervisory Boarder Arno Mahlert is already leaving the Supervisory Board with effect from 12 September.
Gerhard Hausruckinger will take over the management of the Executive Board as early as 1 September, the company announced.
He is to officially assume the function of a "board spokesman" until a successor for Hartmann is found.
As a reason for the resignations, the company cited "differential views" on long-term business policy orientation.
A company spokesman did not want to give details on Friday.
Who will lead the market research company in the future is still unclear.
In investor circles, it was said on Friday that shareholders had recently expressed the disappointment of the lack of successes of the restructuring, which was initiated years ago.
Mr Hartmann has been at GfK's top since 2011.
"However, the fruits of the restructuring cannot yet be seen even after 5 years," it said.
In view of the recently poor GfK business figures, every shareholder will eventually become uneasy.
"If that were the case with BMW, the Quandts would do the same," it said.
After setbacks in previous quarters, the company was also unable to stop its loss drive in the second quarter of 2016.
At €361.2 million, sales were 6.1 percent below the level of the same quarter of the previous year.
Compared to the first half of the year, sales shrank by 3.4 percent to EUR 721.2 million.
Profit in the second quarter was mainly weighed down by the company's decline in view of its difficult economic situation.
The goodwill depreciation of €139 million, which became necessary in the Consumer Experience division, gave the Group a loss of €148.2 million.
In the second quarter of 2015, GfK still generated a consolidated profit of 19.7 million.
Accidents - Bus slips in Krummhörn into ditch: Eight injured
Eight people were injured in an accident of a coach with 43 seniors as passengers on Thursday in Krummhörn (Aurich district).
Two of the occupants suffered serious injuries, police said.
The 66-year-old driver of the bus of a travel company from Eisenach had to dodge an oncoming truck, partly on the oncoming lane.
The bus came to the right from the road and slipped into a ditch, as a spokeswoman for the Aurich/Wittmund police reported.
"Due to the location of the bus, people could not leave the bus independently," she said.
Firefighters freed the occupants.
Six ambulances and an ambulance came to the scene of the accident on Landesstraße 2.
The uninjured travellers were taken by a scheduled bus back to Krummhorner district Pewsum.
There they should wait for a replacement bus of the travel company.
Investigators urged the truck driver and possible witnesses to report to the police.
From 1 October 2016, the fees per cubic meter of wastewater for the Neudorf/Reuth/Welschenkahl wastewater plant amount to 3.44 euros instead of 2.10 euros for Zultenberg/Lindenberg/Lopp 3.36 euros (previously 2.26 euros) and 3.64 euros (previously 2.18 euros).
At the plant in Azendorf, the wastewage fee will increase again at the latest if appropriate improvement measures become necessary.
According to an overwhelming calculation, the wastewater price in Azendorf will then be €4.37 per cubic meter.
As mayor Bernd Steinhäuser (CSU) reported, it was determined during the approval of the budget by the district office Kulmbach that undecoveries for these wastewater plants.
Due to the legal foundations, the Kasendorf market is obliged to operate the plants in a cost-covering manner.
From 1 October 2016, the citizens as well as the commercial and industrial enterprises from Welschenkahl, Neudorf, Reuth, Azendorf, Lopp, Lindenberg and Zultenberg will have to go deeper into the wallet.
Fee increases are between 48 and 67 percent.
As Managing Director Herbert Schmidt pointed out to the committee, a recalculation of the fees for the wastewater plants of the market municipality was necessary.
In the period from October 2016 to September 2020, overlaps or undercovers from the previous calculation period must also be compensated.
Schmidt justified the serious fee increases by the fact that all three wastewater plants are relatively small facilities with a very small wastewater attack, which is between 6800 and 12000 cubic meters per year.
Due to the small distribution basis, for example, expenses for necessary repairs of several thousand euros would have a serious effect.
In addition, wastewater volumes in all three plants have declined slightly.
Furthermore, in the past calculation period for all three plants not least due to their age, refurbishment and repair expenses were incurred to a considerable extent.
The plant in Azendorf is almost 30 years old, the facilities for Welschenkahl, Neudorf and Reuth as well as Zultenberg, Lindenberg and Lopp for almost 20 years.
Schmidt pointed out that the mechanical ventilation system had to be replaced in the wastewater system in the Azendorf wastewater plant.
At the Welschenkahl pumping plant, the almost 20-year-old conveyor systems had to be overhauled from scratch and, after almost 20 years of operation, the first sewage pond had to be dredged, the sewage sludge had to be disposed of and an additional ventilation option had to be installed in order to be able to comply with the water law requirements in the future.
Schmidt: "These extraordinary expenses resulted in the updated care and maintenance costs, which have to be taken into account accordingly in the new calculation period."
The same applies to electricity costs, which were still well below the current level in the previous calculation in 2011/2012.
Steinhäuser made it clear that in the case of the low wastewate attack in the three plants, the expenses for the operation and maintenance "fully impact".
Schmidt added that the market community has been spared from large maintenance costs at wastewater plants over the past 20 years.
According to Steinhäuser, the situation for the above-mentioned plants will remain precarious in the long term, especially since the supply area of the wastewater association Friesenbachtal could not be expanded by these facilities because they are deficient facilities.
Bulgaria posts inflation for the first time in 8 months
National statistics show that inflation in Bulgaria in July was 1% on a monthly basis.
This is the highest value (since June 2012) and the first above zero since October last year, when a positive 0.2% was recorded.
The annual average inflation of the last 12 months (August 2015 - July 2016) compared to the previous 12 months (August 2014 - July 2015) as measured by the CPI was -0.8%.
Compared to June 2016, the consumer price index in July 2016 was 101.0%, i.e. monthly inflation was 1.0%.
The inflation rate since the beginning of the year (July 2012 compared to December 2015) was -0.2% and annual inflation in July 2016 was -0.2%.
Compared to the previous month, a price increase of the following groups of consumer goods was observed in July 2016: food and non-alcoholic beverages (1.7%); transport (1.8%); leisure and culture (6.3%); restaurants and hotels (1.2%); various goods and services (0.3%).
Prices fell in clothing and footwears (2.2%); furniture, household appliances and routine maintenance (0.5%); health (0.1%).
As in the previous month, prices remained unchanged for alcoholic beverages and tobacco; housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels; communication; as well as education unchanged.
Snapchat 'anime' effect criticised as Asian cartoon
Snapchat says this filter is anime-inspired, but some observers say it's insensitive to breeds.
With its filter tool, Snapchat allows around 150 million day-to-day users to change reality and play with identities in a way that is already bordering on the absurd.
You can turn into a pineapple, dog, or figure that could be from a Roy Lichtenstein painting.
The filters are blunt, property-wraving tools that generate more than 30 million improved selfies per day.
All missteps come to the public.
Snapchat filters have attracted criticism in the past, accusing that the app would promote Blackface or propagate white skin as an ideal of beauty.
When this week it proposed to some users a filter that gave them slit-eyes, false teeths and bloated cheeves, some critics called it a racist caricature of Asians - "yellow-face."
They wondered if these repeated controversies pointed to a bigger problem that the company has with diversity.
The news and outrage became widespread on Wednesday, with reports from The Verge and Motherboard, a day after Snapchat announced that it had disabled the feature.
The company offered an apology: the filter was intended as a tribute to anime characters rather than as a caricature of Asians.
But outsiders, who have witnessed racism, the filter was reminiscent of hurtful clichés.
Others strongly opposed the anime comparison.
In an e-mail, Grace Sparapani, a Korean-American art student whose tweet was widely disseminated via the photos, wrote that the filter was "infringing and to the least unpleasant."
She added that "it's difficult to argue with juxtaposing a very disgusting Asian cartoon and the filtering effects.
It shows that the filter is not yellow-sighted, but that the yellow face has been drawn into the derogatory extreme.
Snapchat isn't the only company that crosses these cultural stumbling wires.
American culture seems to be embroidered into an endless struggle for diversity and integration, from conference rooms to Hollywood and the devices we all hold.
And Snapchat's big audience of young people - who are more diverse in terms of race than their older opponents - might be more likely to expect sensitivity.
If one of Snapchat's filters generates an image that's offensive to a user, "it's much harder that stuff goes unnoticed than it used to," 25-year-old Katie Zhu said in an interview Thursday.
On Thursday, Zhu, who works as a product manager and engineer for Medium, decided to delete her Snapchat account urged others to do the same.
In an essay for Medium and in a phone interview, she said she believed racial controversies reflect a lack of diversity in hiring practices at Snapchat.
Zhu criticized the largely white, only male leadership and ended her essay with a hashtag: #DeleteSnapchat.
"Either she doesn't have a diverse representation of people with other skin colors in their workforce, so they can make such decisions," Zhu said, "or they have people with other skin colors who work there, but aren't in positions where they feel safe or comfortable to say about it."
Others share their opinion, a complaint that left Snapchat mostly unanswered.
The company doesn't release figures on the diversity of its workforce and points to its status as a private company.
On Thursday, Snapchat declined to discuss the cultural backgrounds of its employees, but according to a spokesman, the company recently hired a human resources manager focused on underrepresented demographics and internal integration efforts.
Zhu, for her part, said she's going to close her Snapchat account, adding "I'm wondering if they need more users like us to be able to say that's not O.K."
Military leadership falsifies ISIS intelligence, congressional committee notes
United States Central Commad military personnel altered intelligence to paint a more optimistic picture of the war against the so-called Islamic State in Iraq and Syria than it would have alloweded the actual conditions on the ground, a congressional investigative committee reported in a report on Thursday.
The preliminary report, prepared by a task force of Republican members of the Armed Forces Committee, Intelligence Committee and the Wehr Budget Committee, reports great discontent among Central Command intelligence analysts who reported how supervisors altered their assessments of American efforts in the fight against the Islamic State.
Cencom's Central Command is the military headquarters in Tampa, Florida that controls American military operations in the Middle East and Central Asia.
"Intelligence information, typically nodded by high-ranking Centcom commanders, regularly painted a more positive picture of American efforts against terrorism than the actual conditions on the ground would have allowed and were consistently more positive than analyses of other intelligence services," a newspaper article quoted the report as saying.
"What happened at Centcom is unacceptable - our soldiers are suffering when poor analysis is presented to our political leaders," noted Ken Calvert, Republican member of the House of Representatives.
The leadership errors in Centcom extend to the top of the organization.
The ten-page report shows in detail constant problems in 2014 and 2015 in the descriptions and analyses of American efforts in the training of the Iraqi armed forces.
Although it provides no direct evidence that government spikes under Obama have given instructions to change the reports, it shows how analysts faced pressure from their superiors to paint a more optimistic picture of the Islamic State's threat, also known as ISIS or ISIL.
"During the first half of 2015, many Central Command press releases, statements and statements before Congress were significantly more positive than the facts," the report noted.
For example, a Centcom member publicly announced that a military recapture of Mosul could begin as early as April or May 2015.
Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, remains under Islamic State control.
"After months of investigations, so much is clear," Mike Pompeo, Republican congressman from Kansas, said in a statement.
From the middle of 2014 to the middle of 2015, Central Command's peaks changed intelligence reports to downplay the threat of ISIS in Iraq.
The Republicans launched the task force after it was leaked that analysts were worried about manipulating reports on Islamic State.
The report released on Thursday will be completed by more detailed findings while the investigation will continue.
In addition, further investigations of the Centcom reports by the Inspector General of the Ministry of Defense are underway.
Democratic members of the intelligence committee released their own findings on Thursday that were consistent with some of the conclusions of the Republican task force.
"In 2014 and 2015, the Centcom created an excessively limited process for preparing intelligence assessments on ISIL and Iraq's security services," the highest-ranking Democrat in the committee, a member of the House of Representatives Adam B. Schiff, said in a statement.
This process, according to Mr Schiff, "delayed the release of intelligence reports," damaging morale among analysts and "gaving unduly little room for dissenting opinions."
But Mr Schiff and the Democrats said they had found no evidence of a White House Intervenement at Centcom analysts regarding a "certained political opinion."
The preliminary results of congressional investigations were released Tuesday through "The Daily Beast."
Last August, The New York Times reported investigation by the Pentagon's inspector-general, which was started following complaints from Centcom analysts.
According to Patrick Evans, a Pentagon press secretary, stated in a statement that the Department of Defense would not commentate reports from Congress, while the inspector general's investigation was still ongoing.
But he also stated that "experts sometimes have quite divergent opinions regarding the interpretation of complex data, and that the intelligence services and the Ministry of Defence welcome a controversial discussion of these important issues of national defense."
The investigation has repercussions beyond the question of a successful aeriation campaign under American leadership in Iraq and Syria (a claim now made by a growing number of members of the Ministry of Defense).
Last year's allegations, however, have raised the question to what extent President Obama can rely on Central Command for honest assessments of military operations in Iraq, Afghanistan, Libya and other crisis regions.
Criticism regarding the classified information about the Islamic State is reminiscent of quarrels more than ten years ago, when a Centcom analyst Gregory Hooker wrote a document about how, contrary to the recommendations of many analysts, only a small occupying forces in Iraq to think about the consequences of the 2003 invasion.
Mr. Hooker also played a key role in the insurgency against current coverage of the Islamic State.
Last year, according to Mr. Hooker’s team found that, contrary to the government’s statements, air strikes against the Islamic State’s refineries had no significant impact on the terrorist group’s financial position, as they would have put provisional refineries into operation to sell oil on the black market.
However, this finding was not published, the Times reported in September.
Centcom's then commander, Gen. Lloyd J. Austin III, was criticized by members of the legislature for his overly positive assessment of the war situation at a hearing before the Senate.
General Austin retired in April and was replaced by Gen. Joseph L. Votel at Central Command.
U.S. wins fourth match in women's field hockey, a showdown with Britain is imminent
A day after she scored three goals during a 6-1 victory over Japan, Katie Bam also made the first two goals for the women's American field hockey team in a match on Thursday against India that the Americans won by 3-0.
Five goals in two games should actually represent a memorable performance, but Bam (27) said she couldn't remember ever having done that.
"I don't know," she said.
I'm not one of those people who remembers their gates.
However, she knows this: the Americans are at 4-0 in the Olympic competition and are on the same level in their group with the Britons, who remain unbeaten after their 2-0 victory against Japan.
Britain and the United States will face each other in the final game of the first round on Saturday.
Both teams have already qualified for the quarter-finals, the match will decide which of the two within their group of six teams will each gain first and second place.
The first-place finisher will get out of the way of the strong Dutch women a little longer, leading the other group.
Whatever the outcome of Saturday’s game, the Americans have sparked a surprising whirle through their success story, four years after they landed in the last of 12 places at the past London Games.
Bam was part of that team.
She grew up in Blue Bell, Pennsylvania, and for the first time at the age of three had a hockey stick in her hands (her two older sisters played the sport at Drexel University), at the age of 16 she became a national player of the USA, the youngest in the history of the team.
She missed the Olympics in 2008 as she failed to qualify for the squad.
Then came the year 2012, in which everything went wrong for her and her team.
The following year Craig Parnham, an Englishman, became coach of the national team and with that, things began to turn for the better.
"A change of culture, a mental change and a physical change," Bam said.
We didn't have the workload in London that we have now.
We've been able to take the rudder completely.
On Thursday night, Bam opened the torreigen in the first half of the game with a backhand strike from close range.
India had gone into the game without a win in the tournament, but the team showed itself surprisingly tough and held the score from 1-0 into the third quarter until Bam took the game into their hands.
She performed a sprint with the flalk with the flaw ball, then pulled into the middle and luffed the ball into the goal to give the Americans a 2-0 lead.
Melissa Gonzalez scored the final goal in the middle of the final quarter.
The shutout was the first of the entire tournament for the U.S. goalkeeper Jackie Briggs, who has so far had to score one goal in each of the previous games.
She's having a run.
Same with Bam.
"We're in a good position," Parnham said, but how well will depend on the outcome of Saturday's next game.
in the midst of the rage of the Olympic Games, the Zen of the trampoline
Wherever you are, whatever time it is, it's the Olympics.
The events in Rio de Janeiro feel consistent and inevitable, a raging relay between competition and aspiration.
It's as if the Olympic torch itself is a burning rod that's handed from crossbow shooter to swimmer to gymnast to triathlete.
You can't turn on the TV, read a newspaper, or listen at your neighbor's queue in the coffe shop without hearing about Simone Biles, Katie Ledecky or the dispute between Michael Phelps and Chad le Clos.
And then there's Logan Dooley.
Dooley, who is at the Olympics for the first time and a woman’s swarm with dimples, could well have been among those well-known names sent to prime time, built up by sponsors and feel-good TV advertising if he were a swimmer, gymnast or even fencer.
But Dooley, 28, from Lake Forest, California, fights for gold in Olympic trampoline gymnastics.
The Olympic flame doesn't illuminate every event equally.
Further from the center, partly in the shade are the sports, which are not universally recognized: aerial pistol shooting, archery, badminton, taekwondo.
We'll always have the breaststroke and may it reign for a long time.
But if I'm modestly allowed to make arguments for my particular darling, let me draw attention to the discrete charm of the trampoline.
Although trampoline jumping has been an Olympic event since 2000, when it debuted in Sydney, it has been perceived so sparsely for the past 16 years that many of those I'd surveyed didn't know it's an Olympic sport at all.
This could also be regional ignorance.
Although the U.S. sent athletes to compete, it has never won a medal in trampoline jumping and had an athlete by 2012 who made it to the finals.
The Chinese and the Russians tend to share the prizes for the men and the Chinese and the Canadians for the women.
Dong Dong from China and Rosie MacLennan from Canada will defend their gold medal from London this year.
Trampoline jumping has a certain outsider charm, partly because it sounds more suitable for a clown's school for the unenlightened ear than for the Olympic Games.
At this point, I should admit that I'm not an expert, just an earthbound enthusiast.
It has humorous potential - the cartoon-like trembling of its never-ending-to-end impact - but also the gracy of gymnastics and scuba diving, the two sports it essentially unites.
The word "trampoline" comes from the Spanish El trampoline, which means as much as "Springboard."
Aspiring winners jump on a gigantic trampoline, shoot almost up to 30 feet into the air and have to have a series of falling movements (turning, piking and saltos) before landing on their feet.
They will be evaluated according to the official Olympic program according to execution, level of difficulty and "flight time".
Threty-two athletes from 17 countries, 16 men and 16 women, will compete in Rio.
The most common Olympian vector is straight ahead.
Medal hoppers have to go further in their pursuit of gold, faster, upwards and over, sometimes to the goal, sometimes back and forth, upwards and downwards on the playground.
They chase not only the finish line or the starting wall, but in the television broadcast also another sneering goal: those of the world or the Olympic record.
The purposeful pursuit is noble, admirable, even fanatical - and it's enough to move even a viewer to tears.
But it's also what I find a little tiring even when watching these games.
Of this, the trampoline offers a respite.
The trampoline jumper's vector is above.
A forward movement is not the goal.
He or she bounces off, seemingly weightless like an astronaut, goes up, then down, then effortlessly back up.
Here, too, there is a competition, of course not without difficulty, but during the exercise there is no hectic attack on the target, no bulging into the distance, only the dreamy, elegant abolution of time and gravity.
It's Zen as the competition at the Olympics can only be.
Women's qualifying and finalling rounds will take place on Friday afternoon, streaming at 13.00 Eastern Time; those of men on Saturday, also at 13.00.
NBC will broadcast the final rounds as part of an afternoon block on both days from 14.00.
In the midst of the Olympic mania, they promise a quiet, nice moment, one that is likely to be overlooked in general tumult.
If the increase in consciousness can only be achieved by the gaffing and objectification of male athletes - as the male U.S. gymnasts recently suggested, according to The Wall Street Journal - then Mr. Dooley is expecting his air-wrestling, new fan base.
'A generation protests' in Ethiopia, Long a U.S. ally
Violent protests in Ethiopia
Protesters calling for political changes in Ethiopia encountered violent resistance from the government.
Witnesses testify that dozens of protesters were killed during the clashes with the police during gunfire.
Is Ethiopia collapsing right now?
Over the past decade, it has been one of Africa's most stable countries, a solid ally of the West with a fast-growing economy.
But in recent months, anti-government protests rocked the country and expanded into more and more areas.
Last week, thousands of people rushed into the streets demanding fundamental policy changes.
The government's response was ruthless, according to human rights organizations.
Witnesses stated that police officers shot and killed dozens of unarmed protesters.
Circulating videos about the protests presumably from last or earlier this year show how security guards beat young people with sticks while they were forced to make a handstand against a wall.
The head of the U.N. agency for human rights is now calling for an in-depth investigation.
"It's always been difficult to keep this country together and in the future it's going to become even more difficult," Rashid Abdi, project leader of the Horn of Africa to the International Crisis Group, told a research group.
Ethiopia is Africa's most populous country after Nigeria and its stability is appreciated by the West.
American military and intelligence agencies are working closely with the Ethiopians to fight terror threats beyond the region, particularly in Somalia. Few countries in Africa, if at all, receive so much Western aid.
Ethiopia's economy was expanding at a considerable pace.
Its infrastructure has improved drastically - there's a new commuter train in the capital Addis Ababa.
The streets are usually quiet, safe and clean.
Even if Ethiopia was anything but a role model for democracy - human rights organizations have continuously cited repression by the government - the opposition in the country has been constrained, with dissidents being effectively silenced.
Many were sent into exile or imprisoned, killed or driven into the expanses of the desert.
Yet that could change.
"If you oppress people and these have no other options other than to protest, it breaks out," said Seyoum Teshome, a lecturer in central Ethiopia.
The entire youth is protesting.
A generation is protesting.
There are numerous complaints that cover everything from land use to strangling in terms of power by the ruling coalition.
After a sprawlingly criticized election last year, the ruling party and its allies received the opposition's last seat and now control 100 percent of parliament.
At the same time, tensions are rising along the border with Eritrea; a battle for this jagged, contentious line claimed hundreds of lives in June.
Analysts fear that separatists such as the Oromo Liberation Front or the Ogaden's National Liberation Front, which have been more or less conquered in recent years, could exploit the turbulences and arm themselves again.
Several factors explain why feelings of bitterness are now exploding after years of simmering under the surface.
The first seems harmless: smartphones.
Only in recent years many Ethiopians have been able to communicate via social media, as cheaper smartphones have become increasingly common and Internet services improved.
Even if the government blocks access to Facebook and Twitter, which it does at regular intervals, especially during protests, many people can still communicate via internet proxies that hide where they are.
Several young Ethiopians said they were rallying in this way.
The second factor is that there is more solidarity between the Oromos and Amharas, the two largest ethnicities in Ethiopia.
The Oromos and Amharas are not natural enemies.
For eons, the Amharas, who come from Ethiopia's predominantly Christian highlands, have prospered in politics and economics while exploiting the Oromos, many of whom are Muslims and living in lowland areas.
But that's changing, too right now.
"We are in the process of coordinating under an umbrella," said Mulatu Gemechu, a leader of the Oromos.
The largest protests took place in areas of the Amhara and Oromos.
Many Amharas and Oromos feel that Ethiopia is unfairly dominated by members of the Tigray ethnic group, who make up around 6 percent of the population and who are dominant in the military, intelligence, trade and politics.
The third reason for the unrest is Meles Zenawi's death.
Mr Meles, a former rebel leader, was Ethiopia's prime minister for 17 years until his death of an undisclosed illness in 2012.
He was seen as a tactical genius, a man who could think around corners.
Analysts say that he was particularly savvy about perceiving early signs of dissatisfaction and using special envoys to process opponents and render them harmless.
"The current regime lacks this fundamental skill," said Mr Abdi, a conflict analyst.
Ethiopia's new prime minister, Hailemariam Desalegn, was chosen for relatively opaquent reasons to take Mr Meles's position.
Unlike Mr Meles, who came from the Tigray region of northern Ethiopia, Mr. Hailemariam comes from the south.
Analysts say he doesn't enjoy the trust of the Tigray-controlled security services.
Many fear that the result will be more bloodshed.
The last time Ethiopia witnessed such turmoil was in 2005, after thousands protested against elections that, according to analysts, were seemingly botched by the government and subsequently stolen.
During the ensuing raids, many protesters were killed, even if it was less than in recent months and this period of unrest passed relatively quickly.
Development experts praised Ethiopia's leaders for their visionary infrastructure planning, such as the new commuter train, as well as their measurablable advances in poverty mitigation.
But obviously this has not prevented intensification of Ethiopia's internal disfavor.
And she's taking on a dangerous ethnic form.
Last month, protesters in Gondar, an Amharian city, attacked shops run by Tigray and the hatred towards the Tigray is becoming increasingly common on social media.
Analysts say the protests are putting the U.S. and other Western allies into a peculiar position.
The U.S. government used Ethiopia as a base for drone flights over neighboring Somalia, even if it recently announced that it has closed that base.
While the West clearly wants to support democracy, it doesn't want its ally to collapse in an already volatile area.
"This," said Mr Abdi, "is a very thin burring walk."
Donald Trump declares his claim Obama founded ISIS was "sarcasm"
Donald J. Trump attempted on Friday to dispel the latest controversy surrounding his campaign by stating that his repeated assertion that President Obama and Hillary Clinton were the "founders" of the Islamic State terrorist group had not been serious.
This clarification raised new questions about the communication skills of the Republican presidential nominee, whether he was able to communicate clearly and comprehensiablely with the American public.
It also puts his comrades-in-arms, who strive to protect him during an eventful phase, into the peculiar position of having to explain statements that he might not have meant.
Having made the insinuation at a campaign event on Wednesday night, Mr. Trump rowed back in reference to Thursday's claim, insisting in interviews that he really didn't intend to suggest that the president and Mrs. Clinton had created ISIS.
But in an early-morning tweet on Friday, Mr. Trump said he was simply sarcastic.
The tweet was the contrast to the previous day when Mr. Trump said in a series of interviews he meant the allegation perfectly seriously.
When Conservative radio presenter Hughugh Hewitt Mr. Trump tried to weaken the statement somewhat by reminding him that Mr. Obama wanted to destroy ISIS, the Republican presidential nominee didn’t want to know about it.
The last storm of criticism slams down on Mr. Trump, while he has tried to turn his campaign's focus to the economic situation because of falling poll ratings.
Mr. Trump and Mrs. Clinton gave speeches on the economic situation in Michigan this week.
On Friday, Mr. Trump attempted to return on this issue.
Reporting via Twitter, he warned that Mrs. Clinton's proposals would lead to higher taxes and that he had found her speech boring.
Mr. Trump has already brought sarcasm in his defense.
At the end of Juley, he drew strong criticism when he thought the Russians should chop Mrs. Clinton's emails, but he later declared "of course I meant this sarcastically."
This method allows Mr. Trump to push the linguistic boundaries in politics and then row back as needed.
"I think it's one of his ways to test certain messages are received by his fans, how they arrive, and then, if necessary, "clearate" depending on how the reaction turns out," said Ruth Sherman, a communications expert who observes the language of both candidates.
However, I also think he's clever enough to keep his statements vague enough so they can be interpreted in different ways.
Rather thancing the tone for the presidential election, Mr. Trump has taken an even more corrosive tone as his poll ratings have declined further.
A new survey by NBC/Wall Street Journal/Marist, released on Friday, showed a leadership with a large lead in the states of North Carolina, Virginia, Colorado and a head-to-head race in Florida.
While Mr. Trump generally tries not to apologize for his provocative remarks, he frequently accuses his critics of distorting his statements or misunderstaking his humour.
But as he is intensifying the focus of attention in recent months of his presidential campaign, Mr Trump's jokes have put his loyal comrades-in-arms in the uncomfortable situation of having to stand by him, no matter how provocative his statements may be.
Rudolph W. Giuliani, former mayor of New York and one of Trump's most prominent advocates, made great effort to defend the candidate today in a CNN intervesiary on Thursday.
"I believe what he's saying are legitimate, political comments," Mr. Giuliani said, so he was asked about the correctness of Mr. Trump's statements about whether Mr. Obama and Mrs. Clinton had founded the Islamic State.
It is true in this sense that before Obama ISIS was an almost unknown, small organization, he called them backbenchers, completely false, and that's why it happened because he withdrewed the troops from Iraq.
Most mainstream Republicans are trying to argue that the administration under Obama, in which Mrs. Clinton served as secretary of state, allowed ISIS to spread itself by failing to aggressively control the situation in the Middle East.
Ahead of campaign events in Pennsylvania on Friday, Mr. Trump continued his attacks on the media, accusing them of riding around on each of his words and being biased against him.
He taunted the "poor, miseraborate" television experts who try to understand him and said, "You can't!"
Some Democrats scoffed at Mr. Trump's apology as just further proof that he was unsuitable for the presidency.
Once ousted from the market via price, cafe in Greenpoint comes back
Verb Cafe was a shoddy café in Williamsburg where you could only pay in cash that didn't have Wi-Fi but served good coffee for 15 years.
Of course was pushed out of his Bedford Avenue store front by the prices.
The Soap Cherie store, which replaced Verb in the summer of 2014, sells soap in the form of cupcakes.
Locals mourned it as a final loss in a gentrifying city.
Artist Molly Crabapple wrote on Twitter, "I know New York is change, but I still can't believe that CBGB is dead, the Chelsea Hotel excluded and Verb is now a soap business."
But if New York is change, it's reinvention, too.
A revived Verb Cafe opened in Greenpoint last November, less than a mile north of its original location.
The new verb has Wi-Fi and accepts credit cards, but still has some of the same employees and a similarly simple atmosphere.
The coffee is still good.
The verb's new owner is Cisco Rodriguez, a longtime employee of the old cafe who is the owner and managing director of the new cafe.
He started working at Verb in 2001 and stayed there until the end.
When a customer offered him to provide funds for a new field office, he was excited to do it, he said.
Rodriguez, 36, kept a lot going.
Verb used the same distributor for the bagels bought the same espresso machine model.
It even uses the same lamps from the old verb.
But Rodriguez said he also wanted to experiment.
He introduced new ingredients.
On the menu are sandwiches with bacon and cheese and there are garnishes such as capers and sauted onions.
There are natural rice bowls.
The café no longer serves beer, but there are fresh juices.
After nine months, Rodriguez said that he had noticed a constant stream of old regular customers.
"Many old customers have also been ousted by awards from Williamsburg," he said.
Recently on a Sunday Rebecca Oliveira was such a customer.
Oliveira, 34, formerly resided in Williamsburg and visited the verb regularly.
Even after she moved to Greenpoint, she'd take the path.
After the verb closed, she gave up drinking coffee for a while, she said.
"I realized it was because of the coffee and culture in the verb that I liked," she said.
Now she's drinking coffee again, even if she ordered a blended chai tea on that day instead of her usual iced coffee, which is another new addition.
Tom Rosenthal has been another long-standing and loyal customer since the verb began in 2003.
He went there before work and inevitably always ordered the same thing.
"I came quarter past seven, every day, like a Swiss pocket watch and they're preparing my everything-bagel with butter," he said.
Photo Rodriguez said he wanted to experiment and add new ingredients to the menu.
These days Rosenthal still gets his usual order, but sometimes he alternates his Everything-Bagel with bacon, egg and cheese.
Rodriguez said he harbored no grubbish that the soap business took the old business space.
In fact, the owners of Soap Cherie offered to make a special soap for verb with the cafe's cold-brewed coffee as an ingredient.
"I think we're still going to respond to their offer," Rodriguez said.
And the traditional, Polish inhabitants of Greenpoint have also accepted the café.
A new customer has translated the menu into Polish and Rodriguez said he intends to be exhibiting it soon, making his new neighbors feel welcome.
The location also has other advantages.
A local juice restaurant wants to put the cold-brewed coffee into bottles and sell it.
There's a chance that the cafe will expand to an adjacent building.
For many customers, a new verb is enough to get their old favorites.
Joseph Witt was a regular customer and said that he was devastated when the cafe closed.
When the new cafe opened, Whitt said that entering the restaurant and ordering a new coffee felt like a homecoming.
"I had to smell that," he said.
The depressing eating of the economic crisis in "an ample meal"
In March 1933, shortly after becoming president, Franklin Delano Roosevelt recorded his lunch at the Oval Office.
As a gourmet, President Roosevelt had a penchant for sophisticated delicacies of Fifth Avenue such as Pâte de foie gras and Maryland turtle soup.
His menu that day was slightly more modest: stuffed eggs in tomato sauce, mashed potatoes and as a dessert a plum pudding.
"It's been an act of culinary solidarity with suffering people," said Jane Ziegelman.
Her husband Andrew Coe added, "It was also a message to Americans about how to eat."
The couple lives in Brooklyn Heights and are both food historians.
Coe's last book, "Chop Suey," was about Chinese cuisine in the U.S., while Ziegelman recounts in her book "97 Orchard," about life in an apartment building on the Lower East Side.
Her most recent collaboration, "A Decent Meal," which will be published by Harper Verlag on Tuesday, illustrates the history of American cuisine during the Great Depression.
They showed culinary solidarity as they meet with a reporter at Eisenberg's Sandwich Shop for dinner, a small, no-frills takeaway in the Flatiron District that's been in business since the stock market crash in 1929.
Ziegelman, 54, ordered a cream cheese sandwich with chunks of olives, while Coe, 57, ate turkey with mashed potatoes and vegetable mixture.
When the reporter ordered meatloaf, the couple found this appropriate for a discussion about the food in the Great Depression era.
"Rich foods were very popular," Ziegelman said.
There was peanut bread, liver cheese and bean bread.
They were prepared from an ingredient and something cheap stretching the ingredient.
Imagine eating so many peanuts that they serve as dinner.
Coe grinned.
"It must have settled like lead in the stomach," he said.
In the years prior to the Great Depression, the table was an all-you-can-eat buffet in an American household, especially in rural areas.
Teams of women cooked for male fieldworkers and freshly baked paste was served for breakfast, lunch and dinner.
It was the Americans who sent food to starving Europeans during the First World War. "A Square Meal" records the paths with which the land dealt to stop beinging squirrel land.
"It's been a time when food became a central, tense issue for the Americans," Coe reasoned why he and his wife wanted to write about it.
Just as tense was the new budget diet: Mysterious dishes such as breads and cassroles became staples, the casserole as a "wonderful way of recycling food scraps," Ziegelman noted, as chefs could hide unsavory ingredients under a creamy sauce.
Fresh fruit has been replaced with cheaper dried fruit.
Meat, which has been the focal point of American cuisine for years, became a rarely served luxury.
Strange blends were tried, as high-calorie and nutritious satisfaction took precedence over taste or even healthy cuisine.
During her research for the book, which also includes recipes, Ziegelman prepared a dish from this era, baked onions with peanut butter.
"It wasn't a popular addition at lunchtime," Coe says.
Ziegelmann continues: "It was surreal."
Peanut butter and baked onions do not match each other.
It was typical for many of the housekeeping recipes.
As never before or since, household economists - including Louise Stanley, head of the Federal Bureau of Home Economics between 1923 and 1943 - drove the country's eating habits like this.
By publishing recipes and articles in newspapers and magazines, they encouraged women to become "household politicians" and meet the challenge of transforming pampe like creamy spaghetti with carrots into delicious meals.
"A Square Meal" is a celebration of historic treats.
In particular, the reports about the government's response to starving citizens are palatable, such as President Herbert Hoover's Lass-mich-Cake-Eating attitude.
While he publicly praised his kitchen's simple Iowa farmhouse kitchen, he's dining like the multimillion-dollar man of the world he's become.
"He liked continental cuisine like fish with cucumber sauce," Coe said.
He ate in an almost gilded dining room and wore a tuxedo.
He was gloriously unrealistic.
President Roosevelt might have made the same mistake if his wife Eleanor hadn't asked him to make an example with his stomach.
As described in the book, hiring a housekeeper with no interest in favor of Ms. Roosevelt's favours led the White House to produce the most boless prepared.
Many of the species from the Great Depression era of dealing with ease were discarded as soon as the land got back on its feet.
The legace lies on our continued focus on calories and food, Ziegelman said, as well as on how science was applied to food.
When writing the book, the couple claimed to have better understood the eating habits of Ziegelman's mother-in-law - for example, how she couldn't throw food away.
"She's had a real fear of wasting food," Coe said.
Ziegelman recalled, "She actually made people sick."
That's when there was the windbag incident.
After the plates were cleared at Eisenberg's, she and Coe pulled out a dessert they had prepared at home: the plum dessert that President Roosevelt ate for lunch.
"It's called Prune Whip," Ziegelman said, adding that it was a common trick to give modest recipes a fancy name.
There was no whipped cream, just plums, some flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
Everyone somewhat reluctantly immersed a spoon in the tough, brown glibber.
Surprisingly, it didn't taste bad.
Coe picked up a look-up three times.
Like a can-do budgeter, he said to his wife, "A few chopped walnuts would help."
Killed in bomb attack: He was the hero of Aleppo
He put his own life at risk to help others: Khaled Omar.
He was the hero of Aleppo: Khaled Omar dedicated his life to the people of Syria.
He risked everything to save others' lives.
Now the 30-year-old himself has died in an Assad regime's barrel bomb attack.
Omar was a member of the "White Helmets," a civil defense group that saved nearly 60,000 people's lives so far, according to Bild newspaper.
2900 volunteers put their own lives at risk to draw people from bombed-out houses and to provide first aid in the war zone.
They are the heroes of war in Syria.
Omar was the group's face: A video of the civilian gunman showing him in an 11-hour rescue of an infant from the rubble of a house went around the world in 2014.
It made the Syrian the shining symbolic figure in the horrors of war.
However, as a hero, he has never seen himself, according to Bild: "For me, this is the real Jihad."
“When I die saving lives, I think God sees a martyr in me.”
Shortly before Omar himself fell victim to an attack, he had spoken in a video about the last bombing raids in Syria.
The helicopters dropped four barrel bombs over the Alzadia district, one was filled with chlorine gas.
One woman and two children died, seven people suffer from shortness of breath.
"We are now removing the poisonous remains of the bomb so that children don't play with it," the picture quotes his last words before he died.
The deadly bomb of the Assad regime had been supposed to hit terrorists.
Greenland shark: The oldest vertebrate is almost 400 years old
Rare Sight.
Greenland sharks usually only come to the surface in winter.
This shark was allowed to return to the sea after an examination aboard the research vessel Sanna.
He was born in 1624 and swims with a good 1 km/h through the North Atlantic - until today.
Greenland sharks only get offspring at the age of 150.
Greenland shark swims leisurely along the bottom of the North Atlantic, and in one hour it comes on average just 1,220 meters away.
Only when it matters, the five-meter-long animal accelerates to a whopping 2.6 km/h - and at this top speed is half as fast as a seal.
However, this slow-knocked lifestyle seems to be a patent recipe for old age.
Julius Nielsen from the University of Copenhagen and his colleagues report in the journal "Science" in any case about a Greenland shark that could be 392 years old.
"Madness," comments Alexander Scheuerlein from the Max Planck Institute for Demographic Research in Rospot this lifespan, which sets a world record for vertebrates.
The evolutionary biologist has long suspected that Greenland sharks will become stoned.
Only the evidence was lacking.
Greenland sharks are difficult to observe, after all, they live mainly in the ice sea off the sparsely populated coasts of Canada, Greenland, Norway and Spitsbergen.
Because they prefer water temperatures around six degrees Celsius, they usually only appear on the surface in winter.
Researchers know accordingly little about the lifestyle of Greenland sharks.
They measured their slow-motion pace when they equipped six animals with a transmitter in front of Spitsbergen.
Preoty makes these sharks anyway: In an animal's stomach, researchers found the remains of a seal, another had swallowed the jawbone of a young polar bear.
They may be chasing sleeping animals or patrolling at the bottom of the sea and are looking for carcasses that rumble down from higher layers of water.
Julius Nielsen and his colleagues now examined 28 Greenland sharks that fishermen caught between 2010 and 2013 off Greenland or Spitsbergen as bycatch from the depths of the North Atlantic.
The smallest shark was just 81 centimeters long, while the largest 502 centimeters measured.
To determine the age of the animals, using the carbon-14-method, they analyzed the lens core of sharks.
This center of the eye lens arises even before the birth of the sharks, the proteins contained in it are no longer renewed later.
The two smallest sharks had a striking amount of carbon-14 in the lens core.
Their birth, therefore, was supposed to lie after the early 1960s, when nuclear weapons trials hurled larger amounts of this radioactive carbon into the atmosphere.
In the third-smallest animal at 220 centimeters in length, however, the lentil core contained a small excess of carbon-14 from the nuclear weapons tests, so it probably celebrated its 50th birthday during its investigation in 2012.
All larger Greenland sharks, on the other hand, had in their lens nucleus normal carbon-14 amounts, which is produced by cosmic rays in the atmosphere.
In fact, the carbon 14 o'clock for a 493-centimeter-long Greenland shark showed an age of 335, while the record-breading shark at 502 centimeters is likely to be 392 years old.
It was well-known that Greenland shark females only have offspring when they are longer than four metres.
Now the researchers were able to assign an age to length.
Since one of the animals with 392 centimeters was probably on the threshold of sexual maturity and his age was determined at 156 years, Greenland sharks with the propagation can obviously be at least one and a half centuries.
This is also likely to be a world record for vertebrates at the moment.
It is no coincidence that such methusalemes live mainly in icy waters.
The previous record holder, a 211-year-old Greenland whale, in whose bacon stuck a 120-year-old harpoon tip, was also caught off Alaska.
Due to the low temperatures, the organism of the animals living there often also runs on austerity flame.
So they ripen later - and age more slowly.
In order to keep warm in the cold water, the animals often become particularly large.
Because the volume grows much faster than its surface, through which the animal loses heat.
The heat loss of one gram mouse is therefore much greater than with one gram of elephant.
Another advantage: you have fewer enemies.
"A smaller animal lives more dangerously and should focus on having as many offspring as possible as early as possible," explains evolutionary biologist Scheuerlein.
A very large animal, on the other hand, should invest its resources rather in a long life.
Building ground in a double pack
At the most recent meeting, the local council focused on issues relating to construction.
With relief, the panel took up Mayor Robert Putz's announcement that "finally the marketing of the recently designated residential areas can begin."
After the property, parceling and development issues could be clarified and the applications for survey could be submitted, nothing stands in the way of the sale of the plots in the residential areas "Straßlweg" and "Schönau-West" (Binderstraße - extension section 1) to interested parties.
The municipal council is convinced that the building areas in the rural and quiet residential community Schönau appeal to young families and sophisticated seniors alike.
As it was said, builders can now report to the town hall, the municipal council is expected to discuss the allocation of the parcels in its September meeting.
In order to be able to comply with requests for the establishment of other commercial enterprises, combined with the creation of jobs, the municipal council decided to amend the land use plan, which had only been newly drawn up in the spring of this year.
Thus, in the Unterzeitlarn area, the industrial area already designated there will be expanded by two plots towards Staatsstraße 2112.
Frankfurt benefits from Brexit
In competition with Paris, Dublin and Luxembourg, the City on the Main has the best chance of attracting jobs and companies relocated from London.
"It is very likely that Frankfurt will benefit the most from the Brexit vote," says Michael Voigtländer, head of IW's financial markets and real estate markets.
After the Brexit referendum, it is uncertain whether money houses will be allowed to continue to conduct financial deals across the EU from London.
For the so-called EU passport, they have so far been granted admission in the UK.
Large U.S. banks are using this right now.
Several banks have already announced plans to relocate jobs.
The "Frankfurt Main Finance" lobby association expects 10000 new jobs on the Main within five years.
According to the IW, the headquarters of the European Central Bank (ECB), which combines responsibility for monetary policy and oversight of the largest banks of the eurozone, as well as other important authorities such as the European insurance regulator EIOPA and the risk council ESRB.
Although the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) has its headquarters in Paris, this is less important for the financial sector than the ECB.
"Proximity to the central bank is important for banks, as they can communicate directly with supervision," says IW Professor Voigtländer.
Luxembourg is home to a large fund industry, but performs worse in terms of infrastructure, just like Dublin.
Thanks to the large airport, Frankfurt is much easier to reach.
In addition, the city scores with easily available commercial properties.
"It's not that easy to find free high-quality offices in Paris," says Voigtländer.
In addition, rents for first-class commercial properties are 50 percent higher on the Seine than on the Main.
In addition, Frankfurt offers a higher quality of life than the competition thanks to good medical care and infrastructure as well as less crime.
In a ranking by the management consultancy Mercer, the city ranks seventh, Luxembourg in 19th place, Dublin in 33rd place and Paris in 37th place.
Even if quality of life is a question of preferences: "With its already strong position as a financial location for the European single market, Frankfurt could attract more banks and investors," the IW concludes.
London, however, will remain the most important financial center in Europe despite Brexit votes, according to the economic researchers.
According to figures from Landesbank Helaba, around 144000 people worked in the banking industry in London at the end of 2015, compared to 62500 in Frankfurt.
Gilead Receives Legal Costs from Hepatitis C Patent Dispute with Merck
A U.S. incumbent ruled that Gilead Sciences Inc is entitled to the reimbursement of the legal costs incurred by it from the Hepatits C litigation with the pharmaceutical company Merck & Co Inc.
In June, Gilead was freed from $200 million in damages for the infringement of two patents by Merck, which were related to blockbuster drugs Sovaldi and Harvoni of Gilead after a U.S. judge uncovered a pattern of misconduction by Mercks, including perjury and other unethical acts.
In filing the lawsuit on Thursday, U.S. magistrate Beth Labson Freeman said Gilead is entitled to enacting the costs incurred while defending the case.
