Clintons earn 10.75 million in 2015 and pay 34.2% federal taxes
Hillary Clinton on Friday released her tax return for 2015, which yielded an income of $10.75 million and an effective federal tax rate of 34.2% for the Democratic presidential nominee and her husband.
In 2015, the Clintons donated $1 million to charity, mostly to the Clinton Foundation; former President Bill Clinton earned nearly $5.3 million in speech fees; the former secretary-of-secrate gave an income of $3 million from Simon & Schuster for her book about her tenure at the State Department.
Clinton's nominee for the vice presidency, Senator Tim Kaine of Virginia, together with his wife Anne Holton, published his tax returns for the past decade.
They paid an effective federal tax rate of 20.3% in 2015.
"Hillary Clinton and Tim Kaine continue to set standards in terms of financial transparency," Clinton's campaign assistant Jennifer Palmieri said in a statement.
In stark contrast, Donald Trump hides behind false excuses and withdrawals in relation to his previous promises to release his tax returns.
It's common for U.S
Trump, a New York businessman, and his lawyers have indicated a tax audit by the tax authorities as the reason for his denial.
"They're on the move," Clinton's campaign assistant Ian Sams wrote on Twitter, referring to the publication of the Democrats' presidential nominee.
On Thursday, Michael Cohen, Trump's special adviser, told CNN that he would not allow Trump to release anything before tax audits aren't completed.
Trump's critics, among others the Republican presidential nominee of the year of 2012 Mitt Romney, have already announced that his refusal raises questions regarding assets, his donations to charitable organizations, his business conduct and various other connections, in particular Russia.
Clinton has already pounced on the issue and posted a video on the Internet on Friday in which high-ranking Republicans urge Trump to publish his tax returns.
On Thursday, she also addressed the issue during a speech on the Michigan economy.
"He refuses to do what every other presidential candidate has done in recent decades and to release his tax returns," she told the crowd.
Politico has reported that during the 1990s, Trump paid no or very little taxes for two years, and a New York Times economic journalist quoted a number of tax lawyers and tax advisers on Friday that stated that might still be the case.
Federal taxes have become an issue in the election campaign.
Clinton supports a scheme named after billionaire and investor Warren Buffett, which would ensure that individuals who earn more than $1 million pay a tax rate of at least 30%.
President Obama supports the proposal as well.
During a campaign rally for Hillary Clinton last week in Omaha, Nebraska called for Buffett, whose Berkshire Hathaway industrial conglomerate is based here, called on Trump for a meeting to exchange their tax returns.
Buffett said he had also undergone a tax scrutiny by the IRS and Trump was "fearied" of voters rather than the tax authorities.
Clinton's campaign has released tax returns back to 2007.
The Clintons, who now live in Chappaqua, New York, payed on average an effective federal tax rate of about 32% between the years 2007 to 2014 and an effective combined tax rate of about 40.5%.
The Clinton's 2015 tax returns showed that these only drew $100 of their income as wages, unlike the majority of all Americans.
Their main investment was a low-cost index fund from which the Clintons taxed dividends and earnings of $109,000.
