Condoms with chilipuder, firecracker promote Tanzania's elephant protection
Conservationists in Tanzania use an unorthodox species to stop elephants from going to human-inhabited settlements - they're throwing condoms filled with chilipuder.
The method has proved to be effective and the Honeyguide Foundation, which had the idea years agove, has intensified its promotion together with U.S. nature conservation by training volunteers in villages in northern Tanzania to use a non-violent quadruple method to protect their homes and crops without injuring animals.
Previously, many spears have used to defend themselves.
Friday is world elephant day dedicated to animal welfare.
"Since we developed the ... toolkit, we have been able to observe a change in their attitudes within these communities, much more confident that they will be able to keep the elephant from their fields without harming the elephant in the process," Honeyguide's managing director Damian Bell said in a statement.
The first two steps include illuminating the elephant with a torch and blowing into a horn.
If he is not quenched, a so-called "chilli cloud" is thrown.
Chilipuder mixed with earth is packed into a condom together with a firecracker, the end of which is turned down to the ignition cord.
When lit, the condom bursts open with a bang and sprays fine dust on chilipuder into the air.
A hint, as a rule, already out to chase away the elephant.
One last option is a launcher that shoots a gold rairy in the air, causing a noisy, bright explosion.
