Some insinuated to the Kempinski Hotel that it had "given in" to the alleged requests of Arab guests.
"Shame for you," it said in another post, pointing out that the hotel, which is located on the noble Kurfürstendamm, stands near a synagogue.
The hotel quickly dismissed all the accusations and called the incident an "accident" and "apologized" to Lanzmann, Der Spiegel reports.
"There has never been a direct instruction" not to include Israel in the quick-dial list," the Süddeutsche Zeitung quoted a Kempinski Hotel spokesman as saying.
It adds that the country code has now been added.
Police deploy tear gas & rubber bullets against protesting Brazilian students
Students collided with the police on Thursday in the Brazilian city of Sao Paulo as they gathered to demonstrate against the government's reforms to the public education system.
The police used tear gas and rubber bullets against the protesters.
The difficulties began when police officers tried to drive the students apart, causing the police to use rubber bullets and tear gas.
Several hundred students took part in the demonstrations to express their dismay over the plans.
According to proposals by the governor of the state of Sao Paulo Geraldo Alckmin, slightly less than 100 public schools will be closed, while 300,000 students will be implemented to save money.
Some of the protest participants were seen spraying graffits against the provisional President Michel Temer on walls.
The latest protests occurred a few days after protesters took to the streets in crowds to support suspended President Dilma Rousseff, who had been withdrawn from office by a Senate vote to dismissal.
Trade between Russia & Iran suddenly rises to 71% - RT Business
With the lifting of the sanctions, according to the Russian ambassador to Iran Levan Dzhagaryan, trade turnover between Moscow and Tehran grew by 70.9 percent year-on-year.
Since the international sanctions against Iran were lifted, interest on the part of Russian companies in business in the country grew sharply, the ambassador said.
Russian companies are establishing existing ties with Iran, while new firms are ready to gain access to the market.
Of course, further trade expansion is one of the biggest priorities of bilateral cooperation with Iran," Dzhagaryan said.
He noted that trade turnover between Russia and Iran increased by 70.9 percent in the first five months of 2016 compared to the same period in 2015 and was $856 million.
In particular, Russian imports into Iran skyrocketed by 91.5 percent, amounting to $697 million.
Russian imports from Iran rose 16 percent to $158 million.
The trade upswing was achieved through supplies of machinery, land vehicles and weapons," the ambassador said.
Dzhagaryan assumes a record number of Iranian tourists in Russia for this year.
Last year, the Russian mission abroad issued around 35,000 visas, primarily travel visas, to Iranian citizens.
Furthermore, Iran reached the top 20 countries in terms of the number of tourists in Russia.
One could say that the record numbers of Iranian tourists in Russia from last year will be broken," he said.
At the same time, relatively few Russians are visiting Iran, something the ambassador hopes will change.
"I personally can recommend visiting the cities of Isfahan, Shiraz and Yazd, which have preserved monuments of ancient Persian civilization in good condition," he said.
Witnesses wanted: Beverage market in Waldbröl raided
A robbery was carried out on Thursday evening at the beverage market of Rewe XXL on the Waldbröler Gerberstraße.
Under the supervision of a firearm, three darkly dressed persons, who had masked their faces demanded the surrender of cash from the employee of the beverage market and subsequently fled first on foot and then presumably with a silver-coloured VW Golf.
How much money was captured, the police do not say.
The perpetrators are said to have been between 1.60 and 1.70 meters tall, i.e. relatively small.
The beverage market is accessible directly from the parking deck of the Rewe market and is difficult to see.
The perpetrators must have run after the robbery in the direction of Gerberstraße, Friedrich-Engelbert-Weg and fled from the gravel parking lot opposite the parking lot with the silver-coloured car, possibly in the direction of B 256 (Schladernring).
Anyone who has made observations related to the robbery at the time of the crime (around 19.45) should urgently report to the police.
Nagold: games and fun at summer leisure
The children and young people of the socio-pedagogical day group/social group work of the Kinderschutzbund Nagold with their caregivers spented eight days on their summer vacation in Nussdorf, near Mühlacker.
In the leisure home Hardtwäldle, the lively group was quartered during the first school holiday week.
Several full-day excursions were on the program.
On the one hand we went to Bretten with a visit to the petting zoo.
On the other hand to Maulbronn, where there was a special guided tour of children in the world-famous monastery.
The third trip led to Pforzheim.
At the Celtic Museum in Hochdorf, the group learned a lot about everyday life and food of the Celts.
On the hot days, the group enjoyed themselves at the Tiefensee in Maulbronn, which invited you to jump, swim and splash.
Culinary children and caregivers were pampered with their self-prepared favorite foods such as the Sauren Spätzle, the Seebronner Pfännle, Iselshauser-Bloff, the Englischen-Breakfast and Tübingen Bolognese.
In workshops was baked, beautiful pieces of jewelry were created from pearls and stones or made of wooden a Roman mill game.
Other highlights of the free time were fishing in a nearby pond, the evenings by the fire and the concluding "Colorful Evening".
On the day of home the sun laughed and the children and young people returned with satisfaction.
Haigerloch: Pilgrimage to the Gate of Mercy
The ministrants from the pastoral unit Eyachtal-Haigerloch St. Anna were on a four-day pilgrimage from Villingen to Freiburg.
Together, four stages were completed.
In order to bridge the time until the next Rome pilgrimage of all ministrants is expected to bit in two years, the senior ministrators decided to prepare and carry out a multi-day pilgrimage.
Various pilgrimage destinations were examined and it was finally chosen for the episcopal city of Freiburg, where this year on the occasion of the Holy Year of Mercy with the Gate of Mercy at the Freiburg Minster was offered a special Pilgrimage.
16 Minis from Bittelbronn, Gruol, Haigerloch and Heiligenzimmern made their way.
The night before, Pastor Storost sent out the pilgrims in a small celebration in St. Anna, blessed the Pilgrimage Crosses and granted the pilgrim blessing.
From Horb it was taken by train to the starting point Villingen, where there is also a minster of Our Lady and thus the pilgrimage route was started by the cathedral.
The individual daily tours were framed in the morning and in the evening by a short impulse with different prayers, songs and written texts.
But also on the way, the minis at field crosses, chapels or churches stopped again and again to sing a song out of specially compiled pilgrim songbook together.
For the overnight stays, parish halls of the local parishes were available.
Alexandra Burkhardt and Heidi Faller provided a tasty as well as fortifying dinner and breakfast.
On the second day we continued via Furtwangen to Gütenbach.
The following day, the stage to St. Peter, which was the most strenuous route, had to be climbed and then climbed again in sultry heat.
The deserved reward was in the evening a joint barbecue at the Badweiher.
After the morning impulse on the fourth day of pilgrimage in the imposing monastery church of St. Peter, we went on the final stage to Freiburg.
Visibly relieved and happily, the minis arrived at Freiburg's Minster later this afternoon and passed through the gate of mercy.
With a small impulse was thanked God here.
In the eveninging mass in Münster, the minis arrived at the Pilgrimage destination were greeted by co-operator Markus Obert.
After the service, the pilgrims were able to visit the sacristy and the high altar.
The next day there was still time for the Breisgau metropolis.
In the afternoon went back to Horb by train.
In the end, all participants agreed that the days were characterized by spirituality and a strong community experience.
Scot's Callum Skinner storms home as Briton win gold for cycling
Scotland's Callum Skinner raced to glory last night in the men's team sprint at the velodrome in Rio when the British team defended the London 2012 title.
The 23-year-old, from Glasgow, took on the anchor role his compatriot Sir Chris Hoy had made his own. The youngster made the legend proud when he stormed home to claim a win from favourites New Zealand.
England claimed a shocking victory four years ago, while Hoy clinched the fifth of six Olympic gold medals.
They repeated the trick after picking up only a modest sixth-place finish in March at the track World Championships in London despite Hoy's resignation.
The issue between Beijing and London was the replacement of hitchhiker Jamie Staff.
They did this in time when Hindes showed up.
Hoy's retirement created a gap Kian Emadi and Matt Crampton tried to close.
Skinner was the man they stayed with when they saw potential in the Scot who has now unfolded.
Hindes, Kenny - who won with Hoy four years ago - and Skinner set an Olympic record in the qualifying rounds, but in the standard rounds the New Zealanders won in the first round.
And the British trio won the duel with gold.
Hindes narrowly lag behind after his lap, but Kenny brought the team forward and Skinner held it.
A change of strategy accounted for much of the transformation where Hindes raced in a higher aisle to slow it down and, most importantly, allow Kenny and, most importantly Skinner, to keep the pace.
Hoy looked out from his position as a BBC pundit and fell on Skinner, Kenny and Hindes as these got onto the podium.
The 23-year-old skinner from Glasgow said: "It's a flattering comparison."
I'm just here to do my best and make a name.
We set an Olympic record in the finalum and defeated the world champions.
It's just been unbelievable.
There’s no better way to win this.
After the 2012 triumph alongside Kenny and Hoys, Hindes admitted he intentionally fell onto the track after a weak start.
He withdrew that statement later and there was no objection from England's rivals.
With such an outstanding victory, there was no such problem this time.
Hindes said: "It feels like London with the whole GB flags in the stands."
We've come together as a team over the last three months.
We've gone away so badly with so many world titles that it's been unbelievable to win again at the Olympics.
That's an event, a gold medal for the nation, won seven out of 10 titles in both Beijing and London.
If that's a barometer of our progress over the past five months, we're expecting successful five days at the velodrome.
With Kenny's form, it's within the realm of the possible that he repeats Hoy's victory of three Olympic gold medals in a competition.
That would mean a yield of six gold medals and a silver medal for Kenny - who is exactly 12 years younger than Hoy, who was also born on March 23 (an apparently holy date for British sport; on the day Steve Redgrave, Mo Farah and Roger Bannister.
That's the same from Hoy.
Kenny next participates in the sprint, which starts today and will go over three days.
The 28-year-old from Bolton said: "I'm just enjoying this for now."
The team event is always at its best.
You can win it with your colleagues.
It feels a bit lonely to win alone.
Hindes said: "If he wins two more gold medals, he should be knighted."
It was the only medal competition of the day, but there's been more good news for England.
Sir Bradly Wiggins, Ed Clancy, Steven Burke and Owain Doull qualified with the fastest performance in the four-man-four-kilometer race.
They arrived at 3:51,942 at the finish.
The world record, which won gold in London in 2012, stood at 3:51,659.
The first and final round (22.42) will take place today on the second day of the competition, where Wiggins will almost certainly be in focus.
He had argued yesterday with appointees before qualifying, when officials called him to him to measure his body size.
In the corresponding women's competition, which will continue and finish tomorrow, Laura Trott, Joanna Rowsell Shand, Elinor Barker and Katie Archibald of Milngavie advanced to 4:13.260.
The GB team relies on its strong track cycling team to dominate the middle period of the Olympics and pick up a host of medals.
After a truly extraordinary depiction of absolute dominance in Beijing and London, expectations for Rio have been shuttered.
Dramas played out in the background, according to the resignation of technical director Shane Sutton in April amid sexism and bullying accusations, as well as the retirement of greats such as Hoy and Victoria Pendleton.
However, if you look at the first evening of the competition at the Velodrome, it looks as if the now legendary reputation of GB, which has reached its climax at the perfect moment, could be repeated again.
Future of Portland's smeltery due to cancellation of energy contract questionable
The future of Portland's aluminium smelt in Victoria - as well as hundreds of workers' jobs - are at stake, as the plant operator will terminate the energy supply contract and thus cancel all supply contracts, as the supplying energy company announced.
This move followed the decision of the government in Victoria earlier this year not to award any further subsidies to operate the facility.
A subsidy set for 20 years expires in November.
Up to 2000 jobs at Alcoa's facility in Portland are at stake.
No fewer than 2000 jobs - direct as well as indirect - could be at risk in the event of closure.
In addition, it could lead to domino effects, as the smeltery consumes around 10 percent of Victoria's total power generation.
In 2014, Alcoa closed the Point Henry smeltery, which led to a closure of the Anglesea power plant last year.
Alcoa's smelter in Portland is losing money and risking closure.
This move follows years of speculation regarding the future of the plant, originally as a result of the strong Australian dollar and most recently due to the price of electricity for the plant.
AGL, which is supplying the large aluminium smelterry with its Loy Yang A power plant in Latrobe Valley, said it expects that three other owners will terminate their contracts.
The plant is majority-owned by a joint venture between Alcoa and Alumina, of which the Chinese company CITIC and Japan's Marubeni Corp hold smaller shares.
"AGL expects the plant to continue operating in Portland, which means further physical demand for electricity from the smeltering company," the company said in a statement.
AGL also testified that the contracts were so-called "fusification transactions," which should have come into effect from November 2016.
The contracts have a 12-month notice period, so they will end in 2017.
"AGL therefore assumes that the contracts will contribute to the revenues in fiscal year 17," said the electricity supplier.
All potential impacts on AGL from GJ 18 onwards are currently limited by forecasts of the strong futures markets for wholesale electricity prices.
AGL said that it had already written off $187 million in "anticipation of termination" the value of the contracts in its $187 million financial statement.
Alcoa said that the decision to book out the treaties did not interfere with Alcoa's "capacity to operate the smeltering or ensuring power supply to the smeltery."
The U.S. company said it signed a 20-year contract with Loy Yang in 2010, which should have come into effect from November this year, even though it still owes an explanation as to why it resigned.
Analysts warn due to the industry's poor economic conditions of the possible closure of the Portland smelting plant in Victoria and the Tomago facility near Newcastle.
Referring to analysts' statements earlier this week, Head of Operations Stephen Mikkelsen said that Portland consumes around 600 megawatts annually and Tomago.
Should both plants disappear from the market, it would mean a significant reduction in demand in the [electricity market].
And for us as power generators, that wouldn't be good," he said.
More than a Kardashian reality TV star, Kendall is on the cover of Vogue's September issue
Kendall Jenner, 20, model and fourth daughter of the manager and mother of Kardashian-Jenner Clans Kris is on the coveted September edition of US Vogue.
Kendall, who is Estee Lauder's brand ambassador and ran at Fashion Weeks in Milan, Paris and New York, announced the news on her Instagram account on Thursday.
"In a room full of people I love, the sight of this cover made me cry," Kendall wrote.
The revelation of the cover was followed by a bizarre (or simply Keeping Up with the Kardashian's like it?), scripted reality video showing the entire Kardashian-Jenner family (with the exception of Rob and Caitlyn's problem cases, as well as in the presence of all the most popular showbiz-uncle and producers of Keeping Up Ryan Seacrest), how they've forgotten their sister's cover and that ends with a classic twist.
This represents the clichéd plot of almost every birthday episode of a children's show (Seacrest brought the sentence, "You thought we'd forgot you?" with the honesty of an animated rabbit at an 8 o'clock in the morning), but Kris somehow had to let her other girls participate in this publicity.
With regard to the September edition of Vogue, a new era of the Kardashians has certainly begun.
Kendall's big sister Kim may have beaten her as far as the Vogue cover (she and her husband Kanye West were seen on it in April 2014), but the cover of the magazine's September issue is a league in itself.
The September issue is the best-selling edition of the year for Vogue.
With a perimeter that resembles a picture book rather than a fashion magazine, the edition attracts a large number of advertisers (according to Fashionista, of the 832 pages of the waltz were 615 pages of advertising).
The revelation of his cover star is so eagerly awaited that in the last two years pictures of the magazine leaked even before the official announcement.
Women who were already on the cover include Naomi Campbell, Linda Evangelista and Kate Moss.
In recent years there have clearly been more "celebrities modeling" than "Models who are celebrities" on the cover.
Oscar-winning actress and Dior brand ambassador Jennifer Lawrence was on the cover in 2011, a year after Lady Gaga had the honor.
But even the low chance that Ms. Jenner - described by the magazine as the "breaking model" of her generation - falls under the earlier category, she finds herself in good company.
These celebrities aren’t related to famous individuals or reality stars or teenagers of today who know how to shoot a good Snapchat video (even if it all applies to Jenner).
They are really talented women who excel in the entertainment industry.
Beyonce, who appeared on the last cover, had one of the most successful years of her career with Lemonade's release.
Of course, Jenner's success doesn't please everyone.
With her reality TV story, Jenner's position in the fashion world has always been somewhat tense.
In June, one-time Victoria's Secret model Stephanie Seymour criticised Jenner and her colleague Gigi Hadid, calling them "the bitches of the moment."
These feelings boiled up again when the news came out with Jenner's appearance on the Vogue cover.
But riding on the displeasure seems to go past the point.
Kendall Jenner is on the cover of Vogue's September edition.
She's literally a supermodel.
She's running for Marc Jacobs.
She's campaigningly promoting Estee Lauder.
She and all her sisters sell you pictures of themselves on their respective paywall apps.
She sells pictures of Cindy Crawford's daughter to LOVE magazine.
And in her spare time, she can be seen on Reality TV. Presumably only to see her sister Kourtney and her children.
Communal law centres dismiss 160,000 people annually
More than 16,000 of the country's people in need of aid are dismissed annually by the community law centres, while the industry prepares for the impact of further funding cuts on already overburdened transactions.
In Queensland, three out of five people - mostly older people, immigrants, workers in need of help, victims of acts of violence and those who have no funds for unjust deployments and the like - are already being dismissed in their request for free legal advice and legal assistance.
Legal services of the municipality, which are already unduly utilized, are getting ready for further cuts.
From June next year, it is envisaged that these legal centers will lose another 30 percent of their federal funds.
Queesland James Farrell's director's director's community law centers said the last survey by the National Association of Community Law Centers revealed that thousands of people are already being turned away each year.
Farrell said the situation would only get worse without a repeal of the federal government's decision.
"Queensland's community law centers are chronically underfunded, which means thousands of people are turned away from important services you need," he said.
Peouples leaving violent relationships, people with unmanageable and crushing debts, families going through break-ups, workers who have been unjustly dismissed - all of these people have problems of a legally nature and this survey shows they can't get the help they need.
The federal government will cut funding by 30 per cent from June 2017, which means that thousands of people in need from Queensland will be forced to cope with their legal problems without any help.
We urge the Attorney General and Federal Minister of Justice, as well as senators of Queensland George Brandis, to immediately lift these funding cuts so that people can get the help they need for legal problems.
Last year, the state government fulfilled its commitment to raise funds for legal aid to the national average.
It is now time to look at the needs of community law centres and their customer communities, as well as for the Palaszczuk government to invest in this important work.
Donald Trump election campaign charged after employee allegedly drew gun
New York: Donald Trump's campaign has been charged with alleged inaction after she was told that her campaign manager for North Carolina had pointed a firearm at an employee in February while they were traveling in a jeep in February.
The incident is described in an indictment filed Wednesday in Charlotte's regional court by Vincent Bordini, a former software programmer for the campaign in that state.
Bordini claimed to have waited for months that the matter would be settled internally before realising "that wouldn't happen."
The campaign manager, Earl Philip, "drew a pistol, put his right index finger on the trigger, and rammed the run into Vincent's knee," Bordini said in his indictment.
Having complained to the local and regional campaign management, Bordini found that at least four other people had already had the same experience with Phillip, he stated in the indictment.
"Weapons don't have to be fired to cause damage," Bordini said.
The indictment comes just two days after the Republican presidential nominee sparked a turmoil when he told a crowd in North Carolina that the "Second Arrangement people" - firearms owners and gun friends - should take action should his rival Hillary Clinton be elected and appoint judge.
Trump's campaign clarified that the media had taken his words out of context and that he would not incite violence.
Bordini's complaint seeks damages from the campaign for breach of due diligence and from Philip for assault and permanent mental damage.
Trump's press secretary Hope Hicks could not be reached for comment.
Attempts to contact Philip also failed.
WCCB, a local news channel in Charlotte, reports that Phillip is no longer an employee of the campaign.
There was no response to calls to his consulting firm, Innovative Consulting Services.
Republican presidential nominee Donald Trump has so far waged a controversial campaign.
Bordini v. Donald J. Trump For President Inc., 16-CS-14300, Supreme Court, County of Mecklenburg (Charlotte).
Berlin/Schwerin: Poll before country elections: SPD and CDU lose
Significant losses for the ruling parties SPD and CDU in the upcoming elections in the federal states of Mecklenburg-Vorpommern (September 4 September).
This was revealed by representative surveys by the opinion research institute INSA for the "Bild" newspaper (Friday).
While the red-and-black ruling coalition in the north could narrowly continue, this is not possible according to the poll ratings in Berlin.
In both federal states, however, the SPD would be the strongest party and would thus have a good chances to continue the head of government.
In Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, the SPD crashed from 35.6 percent five years ago to 24 percent.
According to the survey, the party would only be slightly ahead of the CDU, which could come back to 23 percent.
The third place is disputed between the left and the AfD.
The left would come to 19 percent (previously 18.4 percent), the AfD would be represented with 19 percent for the first time in the state parliament.
The Greens are rejoining the Schwerin state parliament despite slight losses with 6 percent (previously 8.7 percent).
NPD and FDP would not be represented in the state parliament with 3 percent each.
According to the poll, the SPD would only reach 23 percent in the Berlin parliamentary election (election 2011: 28.3 percent).
The CDU reached only 18 percent after 23.4 percent.
It would thus still be behind the Greens with 19 percent (2011: 17.6 percent), but ahead of the left with 15 percent (previously 11.7 percent).
The AfD would be represented with 14 percent for the first time in the House of Representatives.
With 5 percent, the FDP could hope for a return, the pirates, on the other hand, in contrast to 2011, would miss the entry.
Nokia 9000 Communicator: The first smartphone
E-mail and internet in slow motion: 20 years ago, the Nokia Communicator was launched.
The successors dominate the world today - its manufacturer experienced a disaster.
The device weighed almost half a kilogram and was more than four times as heavy as an iPhone 5.
It was thicker and bigger, so it wasn’t good in your hand.
On the contrary, it was quite clunky.
The built-in memory amounted to eight megabytes, which would be enough today for about two high-resolution selfies.
An iPhone is now shipped with at least 16 gigabytes.
The "Nokia 9000 Communicator" came into the stores exactly 20 years ago, on August 15, 1996 - and it was the first smartphone.
For a few euros lovers can still buy the devices on the Internet, but they somehow come from the Stone Age.
20 years ago, however, the Communicator was a sensation.
The Finnish mobile phone manufacturer had announced the product a few months earlier at the computer trade fair Cebit in Hanover as an "office in vest pocket format".
In fact, the new mobile phone could do a lot for standards at that time: SMS and e-mails could be sent and received, even by fax the communicator could be reached.
Even access to websites was possible via HTML browser, albeit excruciatingly slow.
A calculator was available, a digital address book and an electronic calendar.
There was a small antenna, and the phone could be unfolded similarly to a laptop.
Then there was a small keyboard and a black-and-and-white screen.
Quite proud was the price: 2700 D-Mark, so about 1400 euros.
The average selling price of a smartphone in Germany was 392 euros in 2015.
No one could have guessed in August 1996 that the Communicator would become the beginning of the smartphone era.
Since then, everything has been different.
The smart phones are a success story, though not for Nokia.
The Finns' mobile phone division experienced an unparalleled disaster, was eventually solded and unwound to Microsoft.
Providers dominate the market today, all of which came much later than Nokia.
Be always and everywhere online, this is everyday life for a lot of people today.
In 2015, there were nearly two billion smartphone users worldwide.
About 1.4 billion devices were solded, Samsung and Apple alone accounted for 555 million units.
The worldwide turnover with the devices is around 400 billion euros.
In addition, there are the well-running businesses of countless app providers and telecommunication companies.
In the meantime, an entire industry has emerged around the smartphone.
The decisive breakthrough to the mass market came only at the beginning of 200, when Apple CEO Steve Jobs announced the introduction of the iPhone at Mac-World.
He promised three devices in one: a music player, a phone and an internet communicator, all easy to use via touch screen.
Apple's triumphal march began, and the iPhone is still the company's most important product today.
Nokia, Motorola and Blackberry, which dominated the market, had the go-ahead.
They eventually disappeared, Blackberry is struggling.
Apple and Asian suppliers such as Samsung are now in charge.
The smartphone is a matter of self-evident 20 years after its invention.
On average, every German now looks 88 times a day on his mobile phone.
"We all have to learn to put the smartphone away," said Janina Kugel, responsible for personnel on the Siemens board.
Permanent accessibility is now considered a curse and a blessing at the same time.
England must steer the Olympic spirit and expand Heathrow
Heathrow will bring renewal and prosperity not only in our local communities and throughout London, but across the country.
For me, that was Heathrow's biggest attraction, just as it was back in 2012 in London, when we made a huge effort to ensure that all of our nations and regions benefited from the Games.
The Olympics brought great benefits for Stratford and the East End.
And that will happen again by creating new opportunities for locals and companies near Heathrow.
For our local communities alone, we will already create 10,000 new apprenticeships, create 40,000 new jobs and put an end to youth unemployment in the airport environment.
Just as they were ahead of us in London 2012, there will be those who will think that the challenge of Heathrow's expansion is too great.
I've been told England will never be ready for the games and that we're going to embarrass ourselves on the world stage.
The doubters were wronged in 2012 and they will also be wrong at Heathrow.
There are always obstacles to overcoming, but Heathrow answers every question asked.
Like the Olympics, Heathrow is an infrastructure project for many rather than individuals.
Enlargement is one way to create up to 180,000 new jobs, £211bn in economic growth, leaving a legacy of skills and employment both around the airport and throughout the UK.
We have a great team at Hearth that implemented a £11bn infrastructure investment programme in time and budget, from the T5 - which was voted the world's best airport terminal for five years in a row, to the T2, which was built in the midst of the world's two busiest catwalks.
Now they stand ready to deliver a third catwalk, supported by £16 billion in private investment.
The London Games of 2012 have been a huge success for GB and I am confident that Heathrow's expansion will also be one.
It is time for us to steer this Olympic spirit and expande Heathrow while securing our future commerce and creating a legacy of skills and employment that England can be proud of.
Lord Paul Deighton is Chairman of the Heathrow Board and Former Commercial Secretary to the Treasury
Adblock Plus, Facebook in the "cat and mouse game" regarding ad blocking
For companies that make money with online advertising, according to one of the software manufacturers, attempts to bypass ad blocking are equivalent to a "cat and mouse game".
On Thursday, just two days after Facebook announced that it would blost ad blocks on its desktop page, Adblock Plus posted a blogue in which it announced that users could update their filters in the program to block advertisements on the social network again.
On Tuesday, Facebook shared that its desktop page would change encoding regarding its ads to make it more difficult to detect adition blocking to recognize which part of the side of advertising.
But Adblock announced that it has found a new way to bypass the new codes.
In a blog post, Adblock Ben William's spokesperson wrote that "this kind of back-and-forth battle between the ad-blocking open-source community and bypassers has already been going on since the invention of ad blockers," and that it's possible for Facebook to come soon with a new solution.
The fact that the world's largest social media company has embarked on the fight is a strong indication of how worrying display blockers have become for the industry.
Globally, according to last year's Pagefair and Adobe study, around 200 million people use display blockers; nearly four times as much in 2013.
With 1.03 billion day-to-daily active users accessing Facebook through their mobile devices, mobile advertising accounts for 84 percent of Facebook’s advertising revenue.
The company is targetinging advertisers on desktop PCs as mobile ad makers have no effect on Facebook's mobile app for the social network, a spokesperson said.
Advertising on desktop PCs accounts for a large enough share of the revenue, making Facebook the investment of such a move seems worthwhile.
On Tuesday, Facebook shared that it would update its "ad preference tool" to give people more control over advertising targeting them.
On Thursday, Facebook claimed that Adblock's new filters were worrying because they would also block content on the page that is not advertising.
"This does not represent a positive experience for users, so we plan to address the issue," a spokesperson for Facebook said in an e-mail statement.
Ad blockers are a blunt means, which is why we’ve instead focused on developer tools like Ad Preferences to put more control in people’s hands.
Woman run over in presumed suicide on Swiss motorway
The victim was lying on the highway in the middle of the night.
Police are calling witnesses after a woman was run over by several vehicles on a highway in the early hours of Thursday in a presumed suicide.
The incident took place on the A1 near Morges in the canton of Vaud shortly after midnight.
A woman lay down on the road "in an act of desperation" and was hit by several cars, police in Vaud testified.
It's obvious that a Swiss woman at the age of 49 wanted to kill herself.
She died on the spot.
Several drivers have already been heard, but some have still not been identified, said the police, who as well as every witness to the incident are calling for them to come forward.
Investigative proceedings have been opened, which is a "logical" step in such a situation, Gilles Hofstetter told Le Tribune de Geneve.
However, it remains to be seen whether one of the drivers will be displayed.
Was a clash preventable?
This is something the prosecutor has to decide," Hofstetter said.
Two similar cases over the past five years show drivers are not being held accountable.
In each of the two cases - in which a person in the dark ran on the highway - it was decided that motorists do not have to assume that a pedestrian was walking on the highway in the middle of the night, as it was said in the newspaper.
Last year, a policewoman of negligent homicide was acquitteded after running over and killed a man who was lying on the A1 near Payerne in August 2014.
The 22-year-old victim had drone and became unconscious on the street, the court heard.
The court found that the policewoman -- who had responded to an emergency call -- drove in a justified way and that a man's presence in the street was a "perfectly extraordinary and unpredictable situation," the judge said.
Anyone who has more information about Thursday's incident near Morges should report to police on 021 644 83 46 or 021 644 44.
NASA climate model: Venus was once habitable
Venus was probably once habitable, according to the projections of a new NASA climate model.
Scientists at NASA's Goddard Institute for Space Studies developed a model to discover Venus' past using tools and algorithms similar to those of climate scientists.
The model was designed to determine whether Venus, with its hot, waterless surface and CO2-tight atmosphere, might have once exhibited Earth-like conditions.
Scientists have previously hinted that Venus's slow rotation could be explained by its dense atmosphere.
But the latest simulation suggests that Venus may have had a thin, Earth-like atmosphere and yet slowly continuing to turn.
The model also illuminated the topography of early Venus.
The planet presumably exhibited more dry areas than the earth, but once had enough water and geographical variability to harbor life and protected against rapid evaporation.
Early Venus also benefited from a sun that was 30 percent matter.
"In the GISS model simulation, the slow revolution of Venus exposes its daily side of the sun for almost two months," GISS scientist Anthony Del Genio explained in a press release.
This warms the surface and produces rain, which creates a thick layer of clouds, which acts like an umbrella to shield the surface from much of the solar heat.
The result is average temperatures that are some degrees cooler than those of Earth today.
The model produced a history of Venus that resembles evolution that was previously suspected by planetary scientists.
Although it was once presumably habitable, Venus was exposed to too much heat and ultraviolet light over the course of several billion years.
Their oceans evaporated and their water vapor was burned away from the ultraviolet light.
As their hydrogen, only a thick atmosphere of carbon dioxide with a deadly greenhouse effect was left behind - Venus, which as we know it today.
The researchers shared the results of their new Venus climate model in the Geophysical Research Letters.
Patrick Roy resigns as Avalanche's coach
Patrick Roy resigned as coach and vice president of hockey operations of the Colorado Avalanche on Thursday and leaded too little co-determination in the team's decision-making process.
Alternate-president/general manager of Colorado's Joe Sakic confirmed this decision and noted that the club will immediately look for a new coach.
"Over the past three years, I've fulfilled my duties as head coach and vice president of hocke operations at Colorado Avalanche with energy, passion and definitency," the 50-year-old Roy said in a statement.
I've been thinking for a long time over the course of the summer over the course of the summer about how I could improve this team to give it more depth, it needs it and taking it to a higher level.
To achieve this, the vision of the coach and Vice President Of Hockey Operations must be perfectly matched to that of the organization.
He must also have a right of co-determination in decisions that affect the team's performance.
These conditions aren't being met right now.
Sakic, who hired Roy on May 23, 2013, thanked him for his contributions to the team.
"Patrick informed me about this decision today," Sakic said.
We all appreciate what he has done for our organization and wish him only the best for the future.
Roy has been recognized as the winner of the Jack Adams Awards as the league's best coach during his debut season at Avalanches 2013-14.
He led the club to 112 points and to first place in the Central Vision, but Colorado was beaten in the first round of Minnesota's playoffs and missed the post-season in each of the last two years.
The Avalanches recorded a 39-39-4 mark in the 2015-16 season, falling behind the Wild for a second wildcard spot in the Western Conference by five points.
As Hall of Fame goalkeeper, Roy won four Stanley Cups -- two pieces with the Montreal Canadiens and the Avalanche.
Northesee oil workers resume talks, suspend strike
The labor disputes regarding remuneration for North Sea oil workers will be suspended, while all parties to the dispute sit at the table for a new round of talks, negotiators said.
Strikes against oil field service provider Wood Group have been sporadically carried out on platforms in the North Sea operated by Royal Dutch Shell.
"Wood Group, as well as Unite and RMT unions, can confirm that we have agreed to restart the settlement process of the current dispute," it said in a joint statement.
We're going to hold some all-day sessions over the next few weeks, which will start early next year to tackle the issues anew.
The unions said that their last strike, which is the first in over 25 years, was a protest against possible wage cuts of up to 30 percent.
Wood Group this year cut rates to its contractors in the North Sea by about 9 percent due to negative market pressure.
The company's installment cut follows a 10 percent decline for onshore contractors in 2014.
The company said additional cuts give the company the space needed to get through the market down.
Shell has laid off thousands of people and announced plans to give up its oiling and gas operations in up to 10 countries as it streamlines its operations as a result of its mega-combination with British energy company BG Group.
Energy companies are under pressurity due to lower crude oil prices, an economic signified by Britain's decision in June to leave the European Union.
Scottish MP Dave Anderson met with labor leaders earlier this week to discuss one of the biggest labor disputes that have taken place in the North Sea for a generation.
The North Sea is important for the regional economy, he said.
"In the North Sea and across the country, we need an economy that works for many and not only for a few," he said in a statement.
In 2016, less than $1.4 billion in spending on new projects in the North Sea is expected compared to an average of around $7 billion over the past five years.
Kepler measures rotation rate of stars in Pleiades' star cluster
The latest mission with the Kepler Space Telescope has helped astronomers collect the most complete catalogue of rotational rates under the stars of the Pleyades' star cluster.
The new data help astronomers understand why stars rotate at different rates in the star clusters as well as what the rotation periods are related to planet formation.
"We hope that by comparing our results with other star clusters, we will learn more about the relationship between a star's mass, its age, and even the history of its solar system," Luisa Rebull, a researcher at Caltech's Infrared Processing and Analysis Center in Pasadena, said in a press release.
The star cluster of the Pleiades lies 445 light-years from Earth.
Many stars in this star cluster, created around 125 million years ago, are now reaching adulthood.
They're spinning the fastest now.
These ripening stars emit large amounts of stellar winds at peak rotation rates.
Since these winds travel through the stars' magnetic fields, they have a braking effect on the stars.
When a star ages, its rotation rate begins to slow down.
Kepler measures each star's rotation rate by recognizing the time between appearing a recognizable star spot -- such as sunspoot.
The latest catalogue of rotation rates indicates that more massive stars rotate more slowly than less massive stars.
Rebull compares the stars to dancers, with larger stars rotating piroutets more slowly than smaller ones.
"In "Ballet" of the Pleiades, we see that slow rotors tend to be more massive, whereas faster rotors tend to be very bright stars," Rebull said.
Since the star cluster of the Pleiades is so close, it serves as an ideal cosmic laboratory to compare stellar qualities such as rotation rates.
"The star cluster of the Pleiades offers an anchor for theoretical models of stellar rotation in both directions, younger and older," Rebull said.
There's still much ahead of us what we'd like to learn more about, how how, when and why stars slow down their rotation rates and, so to speak, hang up their "dancing shoes."
Rebull and her colleagues have reported extensively in three newspaper reports on their latest analysis of the Rotation Rates of the Pleiades, which will soon appear in the Astronomical Journal.
Controversial baseball star resigns
Tears runned faster at Alex Rodriguez than the U.S. baseball star in prime days from base to base.
Following his emotional resignation announcement, the 41-year-old pro will wear the jersey of his New York Yankees for one last time against the Tampa Bay Rays on August 12.
“I love this game and this team.”
"I'm saying 'goodbye' to both," the 14-time all-star-game contestant from Major League Baseball (MLB) explained.
Rodriguez was voted the league's most valuable player three times, winning the World Series with the Yankees in 2009.
His signature under a ten-year contract gave him $27 million annually since 2007.
Until the end of the contract on December 31, 2017, he will act as a consultant and youth coach in the future - with a full salary.
As a player, Rodriguez repaid for a long time with performance.
With 696 home runs, 3114 hits and 2021 points for his teams ("Runs") for the Seattle Mariners (1994 to 2000), the Texas Rangers (2001 to 2004) and since 2005 the Yankees he managed some milestones.
With Hall-of-Fame members Hank Aaron and Willie Mays, only two other baseball players feature similar scores in key statistics.
However, the 41-year-old is unlikely to be included in baseball's hall of fame.
Because as successful as Rodriguez - nicknamed "A-Rod" - was on the field, so controversial is his demeanour outside the pitch.
As early as 2009, the superstar on "ESPN" admitted to having taken banned steroids between 2001 and 2003.
In August 2013, he was banned for 211 lots until the end of 2014 due to involvement in the so-called biogenesis scandal.
Rodriguez is considered a prime suspect to have received performance-promoting funds for years via an anti-aging clinic in South Florida.
After a litigation, the suspension was still reduced to 162 encounters.
"The league might even have done me a favour for 2014 because I've played 20 years without a break," Rodriguez said at the time.
His farewell days don't go silently either.
While many baseball legends receive the big stage once again in their last games, Rodriguez frequently had to sit on the bench.
“My job doesn’t include a farewell tour.”
"My job involves trying to win every game," Yankees coach Joe Girardi etched.
After all, Rodriguez, who was married to actress Cameron Diaz and is said to deal with pop star Madonna and actress Kate Hudson, received sympathy expressions from fans from Yankee arch-rival Boston Red Sox.
"We want A-Rod," the supporters sang in the 4:9 defeat against the Rodriguez team.
Chen Xinyi, Silwia Danekowa and Adrian Zielinski
Catched: Because of a positive doping trial, weightlifter Adrian Zielinski from Poland is not allowed to start in Rio.
Rio de Janeiro - The 2016 Olympics are overshadowed by the first official doping cases.
A caught athlete almost won a medal.
Ahead of the seventh day of competition, the first official doping cases have become known for the Olympic Games in Rio de Janeiro.
Chinese swimmer Chen Xinyi, 3000-meter obstacle runner Silwia Danekowa from Bulgaria and Polish weightlifter Adrian Zielinski were tested positive in Brazil and ruled out - immediately before the start of the already critically-eyed athletics competitions, this news comes untimely.
At Chen, the diuretic hydrochlorothiazide, which is used as a masking agent for doping substances, had already been detected last Sunday.
The International Court of Sport CAS confirmed its exclusion from the Olympic Games on Friday afternoon local time.
On Sunday, the 18-year-old had taken fourth place in the final over 100 m butterfly, only by 0.09 seconds she missed the bronze medal.
On Friday, Chen should have participated in the run-of-free over 50 m freestyle.
Chen has requested the International Olympic Committee (IOC) to open the B sample and a hearing.
"The nation takes the matter very seriously and demands full cooperation from Chen in the investigations," Xinhua said in a statement: "We will work with the International Court of Sport CAS and will accept the verdict."
For the first time in history, the IOC is no longer responsible for the sanctioning of the athletes with positive cases.
Also on the night of Rio, the news about the positive rehearsal at Danekowa leaked, the 33-year-old went on the offensive herself.
"I've had three tests, all negative, and my fourth test has been positive," this was "an unbelievable shock," she told BNT.
She is innocent, "the only explanation could be dietary supplements," the athlete explained.
According to the report, the banned substance is said to be the blood doping agent EPO.
Danekowa has been provisionally suspended, according to BNT, the B-sample has already been positive.
Bulgaria's Prime Minister Boyko Borissov immediately spoke with criticism of the athlete.
"I don't understand why the athletes don't draw the right conclusions," he said: "We Bulgarians are under observation, so many have already been excluded."
For example, all weightlifters from the chronically doping-stressed nation may not participate in Rio.
On Friday, the "Adrian Zielinski" case was also known, the London Olympic champion in the class up to 85 kg, like his brother Tomasz tested positive for the anabolic steroid Nandrolone three days earlier.
Both deny the intake, but both are not allowed to start in the class up to 94 kg.
Ahead of the start of the athletes, the Danekova case in particular is another major blow to the reputation of the entire sport.
The world association IAAF had banned the entire Russian team, with an exception because of systematic doping, the runner nation Kenya also produces numerous bad news in Rio.
In swimming, the earlier offenses in China had also caused violent clashes between the athletes.
Frenchman Camille Lacourt harshly attacked Chinese Olympic champion Sun Yang.
"Sun Yang pees purple."
"When I see the podium over 200 m freestyle, I want to throw up," Lacourt said on French radio.
Sun was also the focus of an altercation with Australian Mack Horton.
"I don't have time and respect for doping scammers," he said.
Sun had tested positive for stimulant trimetazidine at the Chinese championships in 2014, but only had to sit out a three-month curfew.
Data theft: How perfidious hackers extort their ransom
Programmers of extortion malware are currently attacking companies in a targeted way - and are successful.
According to a recent survey by malware defense specialists Malwarebytes among 540 companies in North America, Great Britain and Germany, 40 percent of the companies surveyed have been the victims at least once in the past twelve months at least once, in some cases multiple times an attack with blackmail software.
In Germany, almost 20 percent of companies were already the target of ransomware extortion.
40 percent of the victims willingly paid the ransoms demanded by the perpetrators in order to be able to use the attacked systems and data again.
The perpetrators specialize their extortion software, according to the observations of Malwarebytes, increasingly in attacks on company networks.
If they are successful there, the ransom money to be extorted is significantly higher than with private users.
"We are currently observing a terrifyingly rapid evolution of the malicious software," Malwarebytes CEO Marcin Kleczynski said.
In 2008, most malware programs were still designed to send advertising.
Then the perpetrators discovered extortion software for themselves.
At first, private users were the target, but the switch to company attacks took place terrifyingly quickly in recent months.
Ransomware works according to a simple pattern: Once active on a victim's computer, she specifically searches for recently changed files such as Word, Excel or database files as well as archives of images and videos.
These files encrypt the ransomware and deletes the originals sustainably.
Subsequently, the software presents the demands of the blackmailers.
To get the enducation code to the files, the victims have to transfer money - mostly in the form of the cryptocurrency Bitcoin.
But when the personal photo database is at stake for private users, companies are about sensitive customer data or project databases, without which the production stands still.
Accordingly, the damage is higher if a ransomware strikes.
22 percent of the affected German companies reported revenue losses through ransomware, 13 percent even had to completely interrupt their business activities, in one case, people were in danger of life.
In Germany, hospitals have already been victims of attacks on several occasions, whose patients may be acutely threatened by the loss of medical records.
"We always say to the victims: Don't pay, you're just promoting the problem with it," Kleczynski explains.
However, 80 to 90 percent of companies that pay get their data again.
It is difficult for managers to reject the demand of the perpetrators in the face of standstill in the company.
The hackers, in turn, know this and try everything to build a good reputation.
In a recent study, F-Secure’s security researchers examined the user interfaces and contact with the extortionists and come to the conclusion that the perpetrators are actually trying to build some kind of customer service in order to raise more money.
They explain to their victims via chat how they can pay via Bitcoin, set up freely accessible support websites or decrypt sample files for free.
And if private individuals are redeemed after payments of a few hundred euros, companies sometimes have to negotiate hard and pay five-figure sums to get their data back unencrypted.
In view of the potentially high and relatively risk-free gains from ransomware blackmail, the hackers are becoming more and more professional and sharing the work, explains security expert Chester Wisniewski of the antivirus provider Sophos: "A single person can write a ransomware approximately within a week."
However, it is more likely to be three or four people.
The programmers then sell their malware in the form of a software builder to the actual extortionists.
The previous malware kits were created by buying the malware itself, consulting and additional tools for a one-time, fixed price between $1000 and $10,000.
"Included is a customization tool so that smaller configurations, appearance and branding can be changed," says Wisniewski.
The extortionists then look for more hackers to spread the freshly purchased software among the victims.
We are living in a service economy.
So you log into one of the forums underground, buy the set from a criminal and commission another to install the software on as many PCs as possible.
The payment is made per infected computer.
"Usually, the criminals then commission spammers to spread," Wisniewski explains.
The risk of ransomware is now known by many companies that better secure their data.
More than 50 percent of company IT executives in the Malwarebytes survey trust to be able to fend off ransomware using file back-ups and scanner software.
Accordingly, the perpetrators are already pursuing new strategies.
"If the encryption of data alone is no longer a sufficient threat for successful processing, the perpetrators instead threaten with the publication of sensitive data such as customer lists or price calculations," explains Malwarebytes CEO Marcin Kleczynski.
Depending on the nature of the data, the damage potential is thus still many times higher than before.
The perpetrators could also target completely new goals in the Internet of Things in the future.
At the Defcon hacker conference, security researchers demonstrated how they could get an internet-connected thermostat under their control.
In the middle of winter, the activation of such a blocked heater could well be worth a ransom for so many victims.
If the perpetrators then block an entire production line in a factory instead of the heating in the future, ransomware could also become an issue in Industry 4.0.
One for all cases
HONDA HR-V is flexible and suitable for everyday use
Honda wants to pack everything into the small HR-V: a little SUV, a little coupé and also the versatility of a minivan.
Sounds pretty cluttered, but the HR-V, designed on the same new platform as the new jazz, actually proves to be extremely flexible in everyday life.
Although the only 1.60 m high HR-V looks rather small, the passengers have a lot of space both in the front row and in the rear, it does not look tight at any point.
The materials of the interior are well-crafted and look pleasant, only the cockpit top falls off.
The HR-V is more versatile than comparable vehicles for storage space.
There is not only a trunk of 453 l available at raised and 1026 l with folded backrests, but there are the "Magic Seats".
This is less magical than it sounds: The seats of the rear seat can be folded up like cinema chairs and thus offer storage space that is 1.24 m high from the floor to the ceiling.
The trunk can be well loaded, but the loading height is slightly higher at 650 mm than the comparable Peugeot 2008.
The tested 1.6-litre diesel with 88 kW/120 hp accelerates the HR-V very powerfully thanks to the torque of up to 300 Nm at wide speed ranges.
However, he is likely to do his work a little quieter.
The everyday consumption of 4.7 l/100 km is pleasing.
There is another engine: a 1.5-litre petrol engine with 96 kW/130 hp.
An all-wheel-wheel drive is not offered.
The driving behavior is balanced, a good compromise between dynamism and comfort.
However, the steering is likely to be a little more direct.
The equipment leaves nothing to be desired in the testeded top variant "Executive".
Safety systems include the city emergency braking assistant (working up to tempo 32), the collision warning system and the lane-keeping assistant.
The collision warner irritates sometimes because it evaluates many obstacles as "approading objects" during manoeuvring.
Very reliably the high-beam assistant works.
And the smart tempo controller responds to traffic sign recognition and throttles speed if necessary.
However, the full connectivity is only available for the top equipment: 7-inch touchscreen, navigation, app integration and more.
Two USB and an HDMI jack are under the armrest and are therefore somewhat cumbersome to achieve.
The approximately 29000 euros for the well-equipped top diesel are fine.
If you do not want to invest so much, you will receive the petrol engine from 20000 euros, the diesel from 23000 euros.
The engines meet the Euro 6 emission standard.
With the HR-V Honda offers a versatile and everyday car.
Airline is not liable: Crash on air passenger bridge
An airline is not liable for the fall of a passenger on the passenger's bridge.
Because liability is only conceivable in case of dangers that are typical for air traffic, argued the Higher Regional Court of Düsseldorf (Az.: 18 U 124/14).
The plaintiff had fractured a kneecap in the fall.
However, the accident could have happened anywhere - it was a general life risk, according to the court.
The airline didn't have to pay damages to the man.
Illness case: When the organizer pays
Sick holidaymakers only get money back after their trip if the cause of the disease can undoubtedly be found in the hotel.
However, this is only the case if more than ten percent of the guests are proven to show the same symptoms.
In the negotiated case, on the second day of their Turkey holiday, a family was seriously ill with diarrhea and vomiting.
She spent almost the entire holiday in the room.
Holidaymakers attributed their illness to a faulty sewage treatment plant that allegedly polluted the seawater.
The family was of the opinion that the trip had thus been deficient - and complained against the organizer.
In trial, the plaintiffs were unsuccessful.
The sewage treatment plant was indeed defective.
However, the organizer only learned about it shortly before the plaintiffs left.
Until then, he had to assume that the sea water was fine.
So he did not violate his information obligations, according to the ruling.
Especially since no impurities were found in the samples of the water.
The illness of several hotel guests - possibly due to contagion among themselves - is part of the general risk to life.
Out of 691 guests, only 38 had fallen ill.
De Maizière said after the violence committed by refugees in Würzburg and Ansbach in July, many people were worried about further attacks.
At the same time, the state must react calmly and resolutely to terror threats.
What is certain is that our country will not answer the violence of the perpetrators with hatred and division.
We will not grant this triumph to the terrorists.
However, if criminals had to leave the country more quickly, this increases security and also strengthens the approval of the population for the refugee policy.
De Maizière wants to achieve an implementation of the proposals by the 2017 federal election.
He also announced a further staff reinforcement of the security forces, "in the mid-four-figure magnitude over several years."
Sympathy for terrorism must be punished again.
Video surveillance is to be improved, the gun law is to be amended in such a way that the purchase of guns on the Internet is made more difficult.
According to de Maizière’s words, the much-discussed secrecy for doctors is to be safeguarded.
However, in dialogue with the medical profession, he wants to look for solutions on how to reduce the danger to the citizens, he said with a view to psychological abnormalities.
In order to counteracise a radicalisation of refugees, the minister considers better social care necessary.
Teachers need to be better prepared for dealing with traumatized people.
To prosecute criminals in special areas of the Internet (Darknet), de Maizière is planning the use of undercover investigators.
SPD leader Gabriel said: "The SPD is ready to talk about anything that contributes to further increasing security".
It is important that the Minister of the interior has clearly spoken out against actionism.
"This was a slap for the sharpeners in the CDU/CSU," Gabriel told the Funke Mediengruppe newspapers (Friday).
De Maizière had previously dismissed calls from the Union for a burqa ban and the abolition of dual nationality.
He considers a general ban on full veiling to be constitutionally problematic.
You can't forbade everything you reject, and I reject wearing the burqa.
You can talk about rules, for example, in road traffic or for witnesses in court.
Green Party leader Simone Peter accused the minister of wanting to simulate energy and ability to act with new anti-terrorist packages.
Left Party leader Bernd Riexinger said: "Fleet sayings and a tightening of the right of residence do not bring more security to the people in this country".
Better traffic flow on the motorway feeder
It regularly builds up on the bypass road - here's a picture from November.
But this is to change: the right turn lane is to be extended.
This is good news for all motorists, who are in traffic on the surrounding road every evening when they drive over the motorway feeder (A 43).
Next Tuesday and Wednesday (16/17 August) in the area between Hammer Straße and the Weseler Straße exit will extend the right-turn lane by 700 metres.
Motorists can then thread directly from Hammer Straße to the right.
The big advantage is that the traffic divides earlier, which improves the overall process on the busy section of Bundesstraße 51.
The idea with the extended exit had the CDU faction in the district representation Münster-West.
The narrowing of the lane on the feeder annoys motorists especially between 5 p.m. and 7 p.m.
On weekdays, the backlog extends to the bridge over the Albersloher Weg and sometimes beyond.
The reason is the needle eye on the B 51 shortly before the Weseler Straße exit.
From here, the traffic is steered only in one lane over the motorway bridge.
The second track had to be dismantled a good three-quarters of a year ago.
The reason: Static calculations had shown that the bridge no longer has the required load-bearing capacity in order, in particular, to accommodate heavy-duty traffic in two lanes.
How long this bottleneck and the associated daily traffic jam will last, the responsible state company can not say.
However, years are to be expected: because the bridge has an official remaining useful life until the year 2034.
For the extension of the right turn lane, the state company has deliberately selected the weaker time in the school holidays, because disabilities are to be expected.
The traffic will be led one-lane past the construction site on Tuesday and on Wednesday.
Intercultural journey: cultural offerings for schools
The international class of the forest school attended the play "The Invisible House".
Nima's association and the Junges Theater are now planning a music project.
Nima's association enables young people with a migration and refugee background to visit cultural institutions in Münster.
The "Class International" of the Waldschule Kinderhaus has attended a presentation of the award-winning play "The Invisible House" of the Junges Theater Münster.
In the context of the "Intercultural Journey" initiated by Nima's association, 19 students between the ages of eleven and 15 attended the staging in the Little House.
Since the beginning of the year, the association, which aims for the cultural integration of young people with a migrant and refugee background, offers schools with welcome classes the opportunity to visit cultural institutions in Münster as part of this project.
Most young people in the class came to Münster between September 2015 and March 2016 from Afghanistan, Iran, Serbia and Syria, among others, and spoke little German.
Thanks to the choreographies, the scenic images and video animations, it was still possible for them to follow the course of action, according to the press release of the Junges Theater Münster.
The Nima's association, founded by Dr. Tannaz Westerberg from Sprakel, and the Junges Theater will work together in the coming season as part of a musical theatre project.
From October to December, young people with and without refugee and migrant background between 14 and 20 years of age will be given the opportunity to attend professionally guided courses in the fields of singing, dancing, acting and music.
Offers for children and families in the theatrical play and theatrical language teaching are planned.
The "intercultural journey" project continues.
In view of the increased number of refugees, the association expects many classes to participate.
Rain instead of shooting stars: hope for weather improvement
The weather has put a dash through the bill for the shooting stars in south-western Germany in the night to Friday - but there is still hope.
Due to clouds and rain, the night was poorly suited to observing the celestial spectacle, Hans-Ulrich Keller, said on Friday morning.
Our hope is on the night of Saturday.
The weather should be good, then we will certainly see a lot of shooting stars in the sky.
The star sheds are Perseids, which at 216000 kilometers per hour is one of the fastest shooting stars.
They have their name because they apparently come from the constellation Perseus, which rises in the north-east.
Relaxing night: tips against sleep disorders
In the evening you roll around forever, at night you lie awake for hours, in the morning you can no longer think of sleep well before the alarm ringing.
That's anything but restful.
After all, one is in good company with such problems: According to the Robert Koch Institute, about a quarter of adults have sleep disorders and more than ten percent often or permanently perceive their sleep as non-restful.
How do sleep problems or sleep disorders manifest themselves?
There are several forms of sleep disorders, and there can be a variety of causes under them, emphasizes Hans-Günter Weeß, board member of the German Society for sleep research and sleep medicine.
However, what is commonly understood by this is insomnia, i.e. a sleep and sleep-through disorder.
Many people in particular find sleeping through as unsatisfactory or inadequate, says Jens Wagenknecht, member of the board of the German Family Doctors’ Association with practice in Varel in Lower Saxony.
What's behind sleep disorders?
The causes of sleep disorders are complex.
Organic, psychic or behavioural factors, but also medications, can be behind this.
Not uncommonly the sleep disorder is an expression of another disease.
"Very early waking up is common, especially in the case of depression," says Weeß.
And it may be that different factors condition each other.
For example, if you have a herniated disc, you can lie bad, wake up every two to three hours and may begin to think about everyday problems and worries, Weess gives an example.
This leads to tension, and sleep is no longer possible.
When should you go to the doctor with sleep disorders?
Anyone who does not sleep well over a period of one month in at least three nights per week and is impaired during the day should go to the doctor, Weeß advises.
The impairment can look very different: you are knocked off, tired, unslept, have difficulties with attention and concentration, is easily irritable, has mood swings or physical complaints such as stomach or headaches.
To alleviate the sleep disorder, it is important to find out their causes.
Treatment options include behavioural changes, cognitive behavioural therapy or even medications - classic sleeping pills only in exceptional cases and at most for two weeks.
There are scarcely sleeping physicians in Germany, emphasizes Prof. Ingo Fietze, head of the interdisciplinary sleep medicine center at Charité Universitätsmedizin Berlin.
That’s why you should first contact the family doctor in the case of sleep problems, Weeß advises.
He can clarify with examinations whether the problems have a physical cause.
Who should go to the sleep lab, and what's going on?
For examination in the sleep laboratory, physicians advise, for example, snoring with respiratory failures, with pathological daily fatigue, in sleepwalkers or if one does not find the cause of a sleep disorder, explains Fietze.
You don’t really have to prepare for the investigation.
One should spend the day normally.
"Just don't take a nap, please don't take a nap," Fietze says.
After wiring, you go to bed at your normal time.
In addition to the electrodes on the body, one wears nasal glasses, a chest strap and a feeler on the finger.
In addition, a camera on the ceiling records the sleeper.
How can those affected get to grips with the problems?
There are quite a lot of adjusting screws that you can attach to for a better sleep.
Weess counts on: regular bedtimes and get-up times, don't lie in bed too long, don't sleep during the day, don't watch TV for falling asleep, avoiding alcohol and don't looking at the clock at night - then you start to reckone immediately, and that's not at all conducive.
What the Jungle Camp with Missing Medals in Swimming
Former German swimmer Markus Deibler has commented via Facebook on the disappointing performance of the DSV team at Olympis.
In December 2014, he won gold for the first time at the short track World Championships in Doha and at the same time set his first world record.
Former DSV athlete Markus Deibler has now placed protective in front of his ex-team-mates, who are under criticism.
"In a country where an Olympic champion gets €20,000 and a jungle king €150,000, no one should wonder about missing medals," the 26-year-old wrote on his Facebook page in the night to Friday.
The support system in swimming is poor, but the doping controls are very good.
With this, we cannot compete with countries that promote a lot and do not look so closely on the subject of doping or even operate it.
"I'm not saying the checks are to be stopped here," he continued.
If he wanted to make a comeback, Deibler would have to be reported nine months before his first launch of the National Anti-Doping Agency Germany (NADA).
This is exactly how it should be kept with countries.
"Those who cannot be controlled for a certain time before World Cup/OS by independent controllers of WADA/FINA are not allowed to participate," demands the Wahl-Hamburger, who now runs an ice cream parlor.
Airport: gang of baggage thieves arrested
Professional thieves at Düsseldorf Airport
Civilian investigators of the Düsseldorf police succeeded on Thursday the arrest of two women and two men between the ages of 50 and 61.
The suspects had committed as a gang several pocket and luggage thefts.
At around 21.30, civilian officials recognized a gang in the area of Düsseldorf Airport, which was known to the police officers from past theft offences.
The two women and two men had committed several offences since mid-July this year and had proceeded communityly, work-divided and particularly professionally.
The specialists of the Criminal Investigation Office 34 can prove four acts to the thieves, who had entered Germany specifically to commit criminal offences.
In doing so, they had captured a total of over 15,000 euros.
Old buildings, a few lime trees, in between still a remnant cobblestone: Rigaer Straße in Berlin-Friedrichshain does not look like it at first glance, but the police count them among the "dangerous places" of the city.
If yound the road from east to west, you will pass playgrounds, a newly painted kindergarten and an organic shop.
On the façade of a grey house, someone sprayed "ugly!"
Opposite a Lidl, which will soon be demolished, is already being built: By 2017, a house with high windows and 133 new rental apartments is to be built here.
However, articles about this "trendy district", as the responsible real estate company Friedrichshain calls, have been preferably illustrated with burning cars in recent weeks.
The reason is the house with the number 94: Once it was occupied, meanwhile the residents have leases, on the ground floor, however, rooms are still occupied and are used for events.
Since the beginning of the year of the year, Berlin's interior senator Henkel had repeatedly advocated a crackdown by the police against the project, describing the area as a "no-go zone" for police officers and the 94 as a starting point of autonomous violence.
After the rooms on the ground floor, the Kadterschmiede, had been cleared with 300 police officers with 300 police officers for three weeks, according to official statements with 30 to 40 officials and three crew cars on site, cordoned parts of the street and stopped visitors from entering the house with the number 94.
Meanwhile, on behalf of the unknown homeowners, construction workers renovated the rooms.
On July 13, a Berlin administrative court ruled that the eviction had been illegal, and a day later, police and construction workers withdrew under the cheers of sympathizers and local residents.
On this summer afternoon, the street seems peaceful: parents walk with children, people sit on the sidewalk in front of the houses in the sun, others talk in front of Späti.
In the immediate vicinity of the house projects are a primary school and kindergartens.
Left-wing violence isn't the main concern of many local residents: Kerstin Neugebauer, who has lived in the neighbourhood since 1999, and Jana*, who raises three children here, are mainly annoyed by the police presence.
With the residents of the house projects, they share concern about rising rents and gentrification.
It doesn't sound like a neighborhood, which mostly wanted police protection in front of vandancing leftists.
The trench that the Berlin interior senator and the police between squatters and their neighbors apparently does not exist.
Friedrichshain in the east of Berlin was actually a traditional working-class district, which still has a low average income and a high risk of poverty compared to the rest of the city.
House occupations and the residential projects born from them have also been part of the neighborhood for a long time: in 1990, numerous houses were occupied in the former GDR, including almost the entire Mainzer Straße very close to the Rigan Mountains.
While the people of Mainz had been cleared under a large number of police in a real street battle, many of the projects were legalized in the Riga region and are partly still inhabited by the people who occupied them 26 years ago.
At the same time in recent years, the district has increasingly become a magnet for tourists and Berliners from all over the world: Residents from GDR times and former squatters now share their neighbourhood with them.
This is also noticeable with rents, which have risen by almost 60 per cent for new rentals since 2009.
Many of the new buildings in the Riga region are attacked as symbols for this development: they can be seen by thrown-in discs or freshly painted facades, which have been pelted with paint bags.
No other street has been mentioned so often this year along with the words "left-autonomous violence."
One reason that not all neighbors see this as a problem could be that they aren't necessarily affected by crime: According to statistics, crimes such as bodily injury are almost exclusively directed against police officers.
The majority of the offences account for damage to property, which tends to affect new buildings and newly renovated homes.
Kerstin Neugebauer, who has been living in a tenement building next to the controversial 94 for 17 years, there can be no talk of uncertainty.
As far as acts of violence are concerned, it is appeasing: the people in the 94 are their neighbors, they certainly were not.
Even the famous burning cars do not exist directly in the Riga region for a long time.
For the studied architect, her street is not a criminal focal point, on the contrary: "Here you help each other, live together on the street and get more from each other than elsewhere".
Neugebauer has deliberately moved into Riga Street - and stayed.
That’s why it was so important to her that local residents get their street back: from the police, politics and the media, who suddenly claimed the sovereignty of interpretation over the district in July.
However, in the eyes of residents such as Neugebauer, the road continues to be taken away from them: through faceless new buildings in former construction gaps that displace the old residents.
"These are condominiums that aren't interesting for many here - there are rather people who aren't so well-heeled," she says.
“Violence and destruction I absolutely can’t approve.”
But what should you do if no one ever listens?
Frustration begins on all sides, including the police and the residents.
Kerstin Neugebauer has little understanding for newcomers who are annoyed about paint bombs: the house projects and the left scene belonged to the neighborhood.
Anyone moving here should be aware of this - just like someone who moves to a pub district can complain badly about noise there.
