Urbach: Annika Deuschle is the new Refugee Officer
Annika Deuschle is the point of contact for integration and organiser.
She is also a social worker on the ground.
Annika Deuschle has been the refugee commissioner for Urbach since July.
The social pedagogue supports the team of the administration around Achim Grockenberger.
She sees herself as a point of contact for all actors: refugees, working group refugee aid, administration, authorities and anyone else involved.
Regine Kunde from the working group indicates that the volunteers are more than grateful that Deuschle is now there, the egg-laying Wollmilchsau, which above all has a problem.
For example, some - less mocking but rather aimed at the large number of desirable areas of responsibility - had meant when the administration wanted to tender the position.
An administrative specialist with a talent for languages and people, combined with many years of experience in the field of social work, was looking for an egg-laying woolly sow.
But administrative work is not their central area, says Annika Deuschle.
Above all, it is about organization, working with the people on site and also behind the scenes as well as networking activities.
The 31-year-old has been there for about six weeks.
She's paid a 50 percent job to three years.
"It's high time," says Regine Kunde, that Annika Deuschle supports honorary and full-time actors.
There are problems big and small, organizational, human and communication problems.
Soon new containers are standing in Wasenstraße
A serious problem for some time has been finding apartments for the connecting refugees who have been recognized.
The municipalities are responsible for connecting accommodation.
They have to accommodate the refugees in their own or rented living quarters.
The costs for the construction, rent or renovation of these shelters must be borne by the municipalities themselves.
The responsibility changes from the district office to the municipality and job center.
Problem: There are free properties, but not every landlord wants to offer his apartment.
The search has become very difficult for some time.
De facto, the administration does not find private housing.
It does not work so far, but is dependent on private apartments, according to Deuschle.
Therefore, communal housing must be upgraded, as it so nicely says.
So new containers will soon be erected in Wasenstraße.
However, it is only not done with housing.
Perspective is the keyword, Regine Kunde and Annika Deuschle agree.
In addition to a permanent residence, there are internships, apprenticeships, school positions, one-euro jobs and temporary activities for refugees and migrants that need to be found or established.
"More seats would be good," reports Regine Kunde.
Many work in the building yard or help caretakers, do charitable work - and this motivated and very neat, as Annika Deuschle reports.
She hopes that working for the polity will lead to a positive external impact.
This is "not occupational therapy."
However, activity doesn’t just mean work.
The day needs a fixed structure, Deuschle and customer say.
The people would have to develop a sense of belonging.
So it needs offers such as sports, contacts with locals and, above all, language courses.
Professional courses are scarce.
So administration and volunteers are also stepping in here.
It is central that the refugees get the feeling that they are welcome and welcome in Germany.
The long duration of the procedures is a problem.
They have to explain to the refugees that it simply takes time and they can bridge the time meaningfully, for example with language courses.
Anyone who eventually develops the feeling that he can’t help anyone in Urbach is not allowed to work and is useless will never integrate.
The vast majority is motivated.
However, there are also problem cases, says customer honestly, "people with whom nothing works at all". The work of Deuschle and customer is therefore always to motivate others.
All this is already a "mammoth task".
A difficult question: After the recent events, are the two afraid of radicalization, even in a small community like Urbach?
"No," says Regine Kunde very clearly, "what some may call naive," but a residual risk can't and doesn't want to rule it out.
However, the helpers are already sensitized.
These incidents are also an issue among refugees.
They'd be afraid that the assassins would bring them completely into disrepute.
"They want to show that they have nothing to do with it," says Regine Kunde.
Many would like to stay in Urbach.
Thus, full-times and volunteers on site and in the region must look for activities.
In doing so, they always have in the background that a new push of refugees and migrants could soon come to Urbach.
Does Germany need an upper limit?
Answering this question clearly excludes politics.
Actually, it's unfair to put them on the spote helpers.
"We haven't arrived in Urbach where we want to go yet," says Annika Deuschle.
No new refugees, that would relax the situation.
Regine Kunde also makes it clear that the Urbachers are already busy integrating the previous people reasonably.
That's currently around 100 people, mostly young men.
But when people are in need, you have to help them, both women agree.
The political world situation is so complicated, there are no easy answers.
Schorndorf: Burglar wanted by helicopter
The police helicopter flew off various areas for about an hour - unsuccessfully.
Two unidentified men forcibly invaded an apartment in an apartment building in Lindenweg at around 8.50am on Friday morning.
The resident surprised the two and smashed the afflictions.
The two men were about 1.65 to 1.70 metres tall, arguably masked, woring both blue jeans and black hoodies.
They fled towards the city centre.
For manhunt, the police deployed several patrol crews as well as a police helicopter.
The manhunt proceeded without success.
Possibly, the unknowns were noticed before the fact near the apartment or during their escape.
Baden-Württemberg: police investigate after rocker attack on takeaway
After an attack on an Ulm snack bar, the police are investigating six suspects from the rocker milieu for serious breach of land peace.
Evidence and drugs had been secured during apartment searches, the police said on Friday.
Around 20 masked men of a rocker-like group had thrown bottles and stones against the shop window of a takeaway, according to police in July.
After that, they allegedly attacked two guests and slightly injured one.
After that, they fled.
Damage of €10000 was done to the snack bar and the adjacent building.
According to investigators, it is about a dispute in the Ulm rocker milieu between a Kurdish and a Turkish gang.
Thuringia: Zeiss Meditec approached the revenue billion
A high demand for medical technology in Asia has boosted Carl Zeiss Meditec's sales.
In the first nine months of the financial year that began in October, consolidated sales rose by 6.7 percent to €798.6 million, the listed company announced in Jena on Friday.
Asia's business grew disproportionately by 17.1 percent.
Earnings before interest and taxes increased from €89.5 million to €110.5 million, according to the interim balance sheet.
The Jena Medical Technology Group intends to exceed the sales mark of one billion euros in this financial year, the Management Board confirmed.
Zeiss Meditec manufactures equipment and equipment for medical practices and clinics.
The company, which is part of the optics and electronics group Carl Zeiss (Oberkochen), employs almost 3000 people.
